        <p>PLEASANT PAR AGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>" BINKS has got one of those talking machines." "A phonograph ?" "No, a wife.' IT is believed that even the old woman who lived in a shoe insisted on having it several sizes too small.</p>
        <p>MOTHER: "Dear :re, Augustus, what in kes your face so dirty ?'' Her Son: "I've been play- ing in the snow."</p>
        <p>AN enterprising hosier has announced a new button, which he calls The Old Maid's Wedding. Why? you ask. le9ause it never comes of</p>
        <p>MANS IMPRACTICABILITY. —Husband: "I think you'd better save that money for a rainy day." Wife: "But on a rainy day I can't go shopping !" _ _</p>
        <p>Too LATE.—" Be mine !" said the Emancipated Young Woman to the lovely young man. "I am sorry," replied he, gently, "but I can only be a atepfather to you. I have promised to marry your mother."</p>
        <p>ONE HALF THE Won.Ln.—Unfortunate Friend: "How I envy you with your comfortable home, and plenty to eat and drink !" The Family Cat: "It's little you know what I have to go through. The last baby has just learned to walk."</p>
        <p>A POOR TEACHER. —Friend (on the ice): "What are you thinking about !" Mr. Ugliniug : "I can teach any girl to skate in a day, but with that fool Mr. Handsome they don't learn to stand alone in a month." "IF your husband illtreats you, you should heap coals of fire on his head," said the city missionary. " Well, sorr," replied Mrs. Rafferty, "Oi didn't do thot exactly, but Oi did the nixt thing to it. Oi hit him on the head with the poker, but it didn't do a bit of good, sorr." "I CAUGHT sight of a robber under my bed in the hotel the other night." " Dear me! What did you do? Drag him out?" "No. I sent for the chambermaid, and got her to sweep the room. The poor fellow died of suffocation." EVIDENTLY MISTAKEN. —Father : "Nowadays it costs more to mend shoes than to buy new ones." Daughter: "You Ennst be mistaken." "Why do you think so?" "If it did, patched shoes would be fashionable."</p>
        <p>HIS DREADFUL THREAT.-11.1ildred : "I wouldn't have accepted h.m if he had not made such a per- fectly dreadful threat." Blanche: "What did he threaten to do? Commit suicide ? " Mildred: "Worse than that ! He threatened to marry some one else."</p>
        <p>KEEPING THEM Our. —Witherby "When we get our new house, I want to have a room by my- self—a room that no one else will think of enter- ing." Mrs. Witherby "That's easily arranged, my dear; I'll let you select the wallpaper for it yourself." Mtn-Imes : "Mary, go to the door at once. Someone has knocked three or four times." Mary: "Oh, its all right, mum. It's only that young feller as is mashed on Miss Maud. Yer needn't be afraid of his goin' away in a hurry.'</p>
        <p>A COOLER. —Elderly Damsel: "Last night I dreamt you gave me a kiss and asked me to be your wile. Did you dream anything of the sort ? " Young Gent : "No, madam, I never suffer from nightmare, I am happy to say." A GOOD jUDGE. -Enthusiast: "Ah ! you see, only a born musician can play like that—he seems thoroughly wed to his instrument." Cool Member of the Audience: "That's just why he thumps it so unmercifully, I suppose." A MISFIT. —Visitor: "How is *it you always engage such stout women cooks?" Lady of the House: "Because it is the only way I can think of to prevent my dresses being worn when I am away at the seaside." Too Mum Or A GOOD THING. —A. : "Only think! I gave that little brother of my Laura a shilling to procure me a lock of her hair, and—" B. : " Well, and—" A. : "The rascal actually _brought me her whole head of hair 1" "An' thot's a chrysanthemum,' said Mr. Dolan, •deeply interested. "It is," replied his wife, who ha.d been indulging in some floral purchase. " Well, a wonderful flower it is. If the t'ing could ciuty bark, it 'ud be as fine a Skye terrier ez ye'd want to see !"</p>
        <p>A CASE OF NECESSlTY.—Assistant : "No earthly ,use in asking the old man for a rise this year. He won't do anything for us." Cashier: Gr,,at Scott I We mime ask him. If we don't, he'll he dead sure to think we're helping ourselves." "I shall never marry until I meet a woman who is my direct opposite.' She (encouragingly): " Well, Mr. Duller, there are numbers of bright, intelligent girls in this neigh- .bourhood."</p>
        <p>A StatsTrrcrilr..—Mr. Kidder (solemnly): "Now, Johnnie,you are just starting in business life. Never forget the example of George Washington, who never told a lie." Johnny (who works down town) : "That's all right: but I bet he had an office boy on the outside desk."</p>
        <p>As To APPEARANCES. —" You are eying me, air, as if you thought I had designs on your umbrella," ,said the lean passenger, as the train drew into the sta' ion. "Do hook like a man will could be cap- aka of stealing an umbrella?" "Since you ask me the question, answered the fat passenger. "I am compelled to ray that I think you do." "And you look as if you misht possibly be a genteman," retorted the other. "How deceptive appearances mrnetimes are " A DECIDISD SCCCESS.—Dora : "What is that D.R.A. that you belong 'o?" Clara: "The Dancing Reform Asso- iation. Gentlemen dance with gentlemen, and ladies with ladies." Is that idea a success?" "Yes, indeed. At our last dance no one danced at all. We just promenaded about the conservatories." "Do you call that a success?" "Do I? Look at . his ring." EVEN•HANDED JUSTICE. —Lawyer sliarpe : "I beg the utmost clemency for my client, your honour. He was hypnotised at the time the crime wan committed, and did n t know where he was nor what he was doing." Judge Hooper: The zourt wiil consider your plea, air. For the safety of the community the prisoner is sentenced to three imprisonment; but the same man may hyp- notise him again, so that he will imagine he is spending the time in Paradise.' A ROYAL ACCOMPLICE.—One day, when Francis I. was in his chapel attending .mass with several of his noblemen, a well-drcssed pickpocket went and to I behind the Cardinal of Lorraine and ab- stracted his purse; but, unabie to do this without the Kings perceiving it, he put up his %nger to intimate that the later should keep silence. The King tool( it for a practical joke, and said never ain ord. 311:7, after the service he asked the Cardi- nal what lie had done with his ~orse. The prelate, not being able to find it, ve,s very mull lnnoyed, an, took the King to task, who gre.,,ly enjojed the fun, and at length ordered the purse to be re:Aored to the Cardinal. The thief did not, how- ever, come torwa.rd, and the King discovered too tate that he had been trickail.</p>
