      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>CAPITAL" CH A't.</head>
        <p>CAPITAL" CH A't.</p>
        <p>The first of those unconventional meetings that have received the very appropriate and free-and- easy name of "Smoking At-homes," which have just been introduced by Lord Kelvin among the fellows of the Royal Society, has passed off with the most complete success. These " At-homes " are to be confined exclusively to the fellows of the society; and as no business is transacted at them, as there is no formality, and as they are designed for the cultivation of the most unrestricted social intercourse, Lord Kelvin's happy idea is immensely appreciated. Over a hundred fellows out of the 450 who belong to the society were present at the opening meeting. The new departure will increase in popularity. Although they are not busibess meetings, and although nobody is put up to make speeches or read papers, yet the friendly inter- change of views between individual members or groups of members cannot fail to smooth the busi- ness arrangements of this distinguished society,and to stimulate scientific work.</p>
        <p>It is by no means certain that Lord Salisbury will receive a deputation of Norfolk agriculturists. In official circles it is freely said that the Norfolk farmers ought to be satisfied with the speech of the Prime Minister at Watford, instead of" bother- ing" him with their views on the effects of the de- pression in a particular county. But the fact that they wish to interview him shows that they are not satisfied with the speech. They want, of course to urge the advisability of taxing foreign barley. The question is whether, if Lord Salisbury decided to hear their argument, he would offer them any- thing more substantial than a vague promise to consider the matter. It is significant that a leading Ministerial organ scoffs at the idea that Govern- ment will protect the trade of agriculture.</p>
        <p>Now that Lord Wolee.ey has taken over the com- mand of the Army, great interest attaches to the proposals he will make with regard to the Volun- teers. He is fully aware of the importance of the I force and of its defects as at present constituted. Chief orthese defects is the lack of (fficers,and I am glad to learn from a good source that he will pro- bably take steps almost at once to improve the status of the officers as far as he can. His first re- form will be to concede to them the right granted to their brother officers of the Militia, with excel- lent results,some seven years ago,to wear gold lace instead of silver, but with either a distinguishing letter on the shoulder-straps and cords, just as the Militia wear an M. That something more radical than this will have to be done is evident. The net loss of commission this year in the Volunteer force is 21, a figure which has only twice been exceeded within the lost twenty years. But that is not the worst. The number of new appointments was only 574—very Much below the average of the last seven sears. The actual figures nre :-1888 9, 653 ; 1889- 90. 655; 1890-1, 618; 1891-2, 637; 1892-3, 609; 1893-4, 565 ; and 1894-5, 595. It is said that when there is a prospect of a call for their services Volun- teer officers will come in plenty. But of what use would they be? They would be untrained, and soldiering is more than ever a matter of training.</p>
        <p>A correspondent who has just returned from America writes :—" The Marlborough craze in New York at the moment outvies the recent 'Trilby craze in the same city. Vanderbilt pere, thoug., labouring under the disadvantage of having been divorced from his wife, was so pestered by inter- viewers at the Waldorf Hotel (where he has a suite of rooms permanently) that he took refuge else- where,and the Duke of Malborough was later driven from the same hotel by the same course taking up his quarters at the quiet and exclusive Plaza. Hotel, in the immediate vicinity of Central P.trk. It is significant of the difference beween American and English home life that the bride elect and her mother, in spite of possessing a palatial mansion in Fifth Avenue, have been staying for the last few weeks at the Savoy Hotel, within a few minutes walk of the Plaza.</p>
        <p>"I wonder how the duke likes his American— shall we call it popularity? At Coster and Bial's. the Empire Theatre of New York, among the super- bly mounted tableaux vivants now running, is one representing the Duke in his coronet, assisting on hoard an ocean steamer a gorgeously attired Miss Vanderbilt, who is heasily handicapped by an enor- mous bag of money which is tucked under her free arm. The name of this picture on the programme is" The Exportation of Gold." " A cartoon in one of the comic papers is equally careless of the ducal sensibilities. A huge pile of building—presumably Blenheim—is seen tottering to its fall. The duke, in the guise of a small schoolboy, stands at one side crying, while on the other—with set face and ' the muscles of a prize fighter—the fair Consuelo is supporting the edifice with one shoulder. "But perhaps the greatest trial to the duke is running the gauntlet of the American Press. Only one thing can be more distasteful than the ill-natured reports which are so freely circulated about him— their contradiction. Ten days ago a leading New York paper contained a paragraph, headed in large letters : ' Is not pigeon-breasted. Does not speak i n a whisper.'"</p>
        <p>One of the subjects most talked about in London to-day has been the outgoing Lord Mayor's invita- tion to leading city magnates to meet Mr. Barney Barnato at dinner, to felicitate " that gentleman on his recent courageous, honourable, and successful efforts to avert a financial crisis." It was at first widely supposed that the letter attributed to the Lord Mayor must be a fabrication, but on this theory being authoritatively exploded by Sir Joseph Renal s himself, it is with tolerable unanimity out- side the Bernath circle admitted that nothing in his year of office has become the Lord Mayor of London so ill as his last act before ending it. It is all the more regrettable that Sic Joseph should make :his blunder on the eve of leaving the Mansion tiouse, because on the whole he has borne himself as chief magistrate with dignity and vigour. His French triumphal processions did, it is true, expose him to some derision on the part. of the scoffers, but it was always felt that, although unduly theatrical, there was a solid substratum of usefulness in his Bor- deaux progress. The city is now greatly exercised with speculations as to the manner in which the magnates of financial and eommercial London will respond to the invitation to meet Mr. Barnet°. There is an impression that, to far as those who represent what is best and mp-t substantial in tne City are Concerned, the banquet will be a "frost."</p>
        <p>It would not be easy to describe the amount of I interest felt here among Liberal and Radical circles respecting the quarrel whioh has broken out in the ranks of the Nationalists. The latest phases of it eQpecially have given a fresh edge to curiosity, and Mr. Dillon's last denunciation of Mr. Healy is re- garded as utterly closing the door to the possibility of any arrangement or compromise. When Parlia- ments meet we may reckon on three Irish parties, for by that time Mr. Healy will either be drummed out of the Wearthyite party or he will have re- , flounced it himself. His chief inducement not to detach himself voluntarily from it would be that so hug as he remains in it he has always the chance of winning over to his side some additional mem- bers of it, whereas if be set out upon independent lines, with no general election in view for seven years, his prospect of increasing his following would be small. Mr. Healy has about 26 followers more or less faithful to him in the House of Com- mons. There is not now in the whole of the M•Carthyite section a man equal to him in resource or ability, and if he is withdrawn from it the M'Carthyitem will be practically effaced except as a voting phalanx. The next few years, therefore,may witness a fundamental 'change in the character of the Anglo-Irish alliance, for Mr. Healy has already, in effect, cast it off.</p>
        <p>The Cabinet Council which was held on Tuesday at half-past twelve at the Foreign Office was of very great importance. There was an inner and an outer ring in Lord Salisbury's Cabinet of nine- teen members. The outer ring cannot until Tues- day have known what proofs of our failure in Con- stantinople were in the possession of Lord Salis- bury. Now they know, and although the strictest secrecy is maintained, there is some reason for be- lieving that the word used by Lord Lansdowne on Monday night, in connection with the Chitral cam- pain, will bsst deseribe the attitude of the Cabinet stiffening." What that stiffening portends will naturally depend upon many things, most impor- tant of ell—upon the attitude of European powers.</p>
        <p>-- The Society of Friends evidently felt the impulse nf the times, and see the necessity of speaking out on questions of the hour. Theylpropose holding a conference at Manchester next week, when a num- ber of topics relating to Quskerism will be dis- ewsed, and in particular the subjects of "adult chools " and the attitude of the Society of Friends towards social questions and modern thought.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
