      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 9, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 9, 1895.</p>
        <p>Nearly all the glass eyes used in the world are made in Thuringia, Germat y. People who cannot aff ard the luxury of a fire in the bedroom will find a light woollen shawl thrown tent-wise over the head of service.</p>
        <p>Sea-swallows' eggs are a bonnebouche at Chinese dinner-parties; but they are terribly explosive. A dish for a party of half a dozen persons may cost from eight to ten pounds. When windows are dirty from smoke or fog, it is a good plan to add a teaspoonful of ammonia to the water; and the same mixture is excellent for looking-glasses in overmantels which are often affected by smoke from the fire.</p>
        <p>Every nt w and then one hears of people bein! poisoned from eating food preserved in tin cans. The amount of risk we incur from eating these preserved foods is eXceedingly small, and it is likely to be done away with altogether, for paper cans are now manu- factured in the United States, which are designed to supersede the use of those in tin altogether. Of the amount of " green stuff "eaten by Londerers in the course of a twelvemonth, potatoes head the list with 400,000 tons; cabbaves coming next with 111,000 tons. After this there is a great falling-off, as the con- sumption of "greens" is returned at only 500 tons while onions, carrots, and turnips together disappear at the rate of 100,000 tons. To cleanse and preserve oilcloth : Shred half an miner of goon bees'-wax into a saucer, cover it entirely with turpentine, and place in the oven until melted. After washing the oilcloth thoroughly with a flannel, rub the whole surface lightly with a bit of flannel dipped in the wax and turpentine, then rub with a dry cloth. A polish is produced, and the surface is lightly need with thr wax, which is washed off, together with any dust or dirt it may have contracted, while the oilcloth is pre- served.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>- MY DEAREST NELLirs,</p>
        <p>In spite of the number of astrachan and other rough cloths one sees in the shops, it is almost certain that smooth-surfaced cloths will be exten- sively worn. They will be much trimmed with rich embroidery, sequin worked cream lace, &amp;c.— that is to say, so far as the bodices are concerned; the skirts will continue to remain plain—a style not likely to go out of vogue so essentially becom- ing and comfortable it is. Fur, as I told you, will be very much used this season, both on dresses and capes. The latter are very elaborate, being made principally of velvet handsomely embroidered and trimmed with fur. Tight-fitting jackets made entirely of Persian lamb are being shown in the West-end houses, but I must premise that they are rather expensive. Cloth capes will be lined with fur and trimmed with deep borders and collars of the same. A smart little velvet cape was trimmed with bretelles of jet and feather tips that formed deep epaulettes on the shoulders, the same trim- ming being repeated on the collar. Another elegant cape had each side ornamented with a handsome drooping garniture of jet. It was surrounded entirely with wide feather trimming, and a large flat bow of black satin ribbon finished</p>
        <p>each shoulder. Then there is a pretty little cape made of smooth thick blaok cl, VI. It has a turn- down velvet collar, and has a second cape over the shoulders.</p>
        <p>Just at this time of the year there is a great run on long cloaks, coats, and pelisses for little girls' wear, as these obliging garments cover up the thin and shabby summer frocks and make the small maidens look spick and span and feel warm out of doors at any rate, while the new dresses are in process of making.. Velvet and velveteen are as fashionable for children as for their elders, although for the former plain colours are more in vogue than the rather loud and striking patters which are so much favoured for adults. A pretty pelisse or coat which might be worn by a girl from three to six years old, is of greenish blue velveteen. The full skirt is put into a sort of plain straight double- breasted bodice, apparently fastening with a double row of large smoked pearl buttons, and there is a full cape collar slightly pointed back and front, of the velveteen, edged with narrow brown fur. The sleeves are made with a large puff to the elbow, where there is a band of fur, and quite close-fitting to the wrist, which is also fur-edged. A big hat with a white telt brim and a loose blue velveteen crown, with a band of fur round it, or a little blue velvet bonnett, would go nicely with this pelisse. Here is a neat little afternoon dress. It is of blue. grey fancy cloth, trimmed with black jet insertion. The skirt is cut with gored front, one gore each side, and gored back, which is cut without a seam in the centre. It is lined with linnette, and inter- lined at the lower edge for 12 inches with hair. cloth. The front is fitted with darts, the back being gathered. The bodice is arranged with a V-shaped vest strapped across with insertion. It is stitched in on the left side and hooked on the right. The over-fronts are arranged in fine pleats over the tight lining, from the point of basque to just above the waist, the pleats kept down by stitching, the fulness falling free above. The bodice hooks invisibly in the centre of the termination</p>
        <p>of the vest. Insertion edges the pointed basque, also both sides of the plain collar. The wide rever collar is lined with muslin and faced with silk, and is trimmed with two rows of insertion. The sleeves have the upper part lined with crinoline, and are trimmed at the wrists with three rows of insertion. The following is an awfully neat little travelling costume, one, namely, of putty-coloured faced cloth trimmed with appliqu6 work of a.cleper shade; double frillings in almond. green silk define the inserted front in appliqu6 work and the side box pleat, which correspond with those in front and at the back of the skirt. There are large mother o' pearl buttons on the revers faced with green silk, like the turned-down collar. With it may be worn a straw hat adorned with bunches of roses, clusters of white feathers, and large bow in green velvet. Hats are still as pretty as ever, but I must con- fess I don't care much for some of the most fashionable bonnets one sees in the windows. One of the most taking chapeaux I have seen recently was a picture hat in brown velvet. It slightly drooped in front, throwing the features into</p>
        <p>becorninishadow. How well is this fascinating poise understood across the Channel ! It has "a huge bow in rich " Paisley " ribbon, and a cluster of feathers in natural white and brown. Your loving</p>
        <p>Mexico proluces anything that can be raised in any other country. So varied is the climate that in the same State can be raised any product of the tropics and of the polar regions. SHOW THIS TO SOMN WOMAN. I will send a valuable, instructive, and highly interesting book which every woman would be glad to have Contains important and confidential advice and tells both matried and single how to keep hemthy and avoid the anxieties so &amp;stressing to the ,sex. Sent tree, securely sealed, for one stamp to cover postage. ADDRI2IB s Professor E. 1418/SLIS., 17, Eastbourne Terrace Paddington, London. W,</p>
        <p>keiFfi-,, t--" • / ' t,. a N , , -6, 1 ' 1 . X --- ----% i '---- - --1-=- &amp; --- slit, / I, 1„ , Il' 116114 I 1 it 1, ,</p>
