      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>Frightened into Elealth,</head>
        <p>Frightened into Elealth,</p>
        <p>Many persons- have, ssays "Science Siftings," been frighten‘d to death, but ton days ago a pa- tient in a New York hospital was frightened into getting well. The man was brought in an ambu- lance, supposedly dying from heart failure. He Was laid on a table, and a diagnosis showed him tu be suffering from hysteria. The surgeon turned to one of his assis- tants and,asking for a knife,remarked that he would cut down to the heart, and Rad out what the trouble sass The patient gave yell, and, leaping for the table started for the ,s,,or. Remonstraiace was in vain. The man was , cured, and never came back.</p>
        <p>Eating Slew or Fast. While we have always maintained that rapid eating is unfavourable to digestion, yet there is something to b« said against that sort of slow and deliberate eating in which one thinks all while about the food with a sort of worry lest some particle may go down before it has had sufficient attention. Mastication should be performed with energy mad with a sort of pleasure. The energv given to it stimulates the flow of saliva and the gastric juice, and favour digestion. One danger in mumbling over cur food is that it inhibits these secretions. It does not follow, however;:hat with energetic mastication we should bolt our food half chewed. We should simply form the habit of masticating it with vigour, with energy, as if it were a pleasure rather than a task.</p>
        <p>The Inventor of the P4..lka. The origin of the polka is not generally known, the inventor of the dance having been a young Bohemian girl named Haniczk Selezka. She was a blooming young peasant maiden, and the best dancer in the village of Costelec, on the river Elbe, and used to perform SOIO dances of her own invention at the various village festi- vities. It was in thr year 1830,at a farmhouse, that the assembled guests asked her to dance a solo,and she said, "I will show you something quite new," and to the music of her own singing she danced the polka step, though with more elaboration than it is now performed The dance became so popular that it was later made a national dance, and Heniczka named it Pulka, as she said it was danced in short steps; from Pulka came Pol. ku, and finally Polka, the dance three years later, in 100, became popular in Prague, and in 1839 it was al- ready danced at the Vienna balls and one year later it was the most popular dance in Paris. Haniczka Selezha is still alive, surrounded by numerous grandchildren and great-grandchildren sprung from her own six sons and daughters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>"THE WISDOM OF OUR ANCESTORS.'</head>
        <p>"THE WISDOM OF OUR ANCESTORS.'</p>
        <p>Mr. Francis Watt has compiled and Mr. John Lane har published an interesting little volume which will not, says the " Manchester Guardian," increase the public re- verence for the "wisdom of our ancestors "—once the favourite topic for Tory panegyrics. Mr. Watt has been rummaging in "The Law's Lumberroom," and has brought out of it some very odd things. By "the benefi' of clergy" a man convicted of a capital crime escaped death if he could read the first verse of the 51st Psalm in the vulgate. By the aid of the three words it contains. Ben Johnson evaded the gallows after he had killeci Gabriel Spencer, the actor, in a brawl. An accused per- son who refused to plead either " guilty " or "not guilty" was adjudged to the peine forte et dun—that is, was pressed to death Some had the constancy to endure this torture in rder that their families might retain the property that would have been confiscated on convic- tion. Anything that had been instrumental in the d.a- truction of human life was forfeited to the Crown. "If a man fell into the water, was carried under a mill wheel, and perished, the wheel was forfeit, but not the mill." If a cart and waggon came into collision and the driver of the cart was thrown under the waggon wheels and died, both vehicles, the whole of their con- tents, and the horses that drew thew were thus deedands The right of sanctuary flourished in the Middle Ages. Manchester tor a time was a place of sanctuary, but quickly and sensibly dispensed with the privilege, which was trans erred to Chester. Trial by ordeal was claimed I and refused in the seventeenth century, and wager of battle was claimed and granted in one famous instance in the nineteenth century. But the case of Ashford v. Thornton proved fatal to the latter foolish law, which was abolished in 1819. Sumptuary laws were once common enough. "Henry VIII.," observes Mr. Watt, "saw to men's garbs as well as their beliefs," and under Queen Mary "common folk who wore silk on hat, bon- net, girdle, scabbard, hose, shoes, or spur-leathers were grievously amerced." The law regulated the dead RS well as the living. In the reign of Charles IL, as linen was coming into fashion, a law was passed which ordained that all corpses should be buried in woollen shrouds. The object of this regulation was to encourage the woollen industry. The penalty for a breach of this statute was £5. There are many allusions to this law in the literature of the last century, and it retained its place on the Statute-book until 1814. Various other curious enactments are cited by Mr. Watt. We do not doubt that all this legal lumber had once a meaning and was well intended. Probably some of the enactments now on the Statute-book will excite in our descendants the same sort of amazement with which we now regard the right of sanctuary and benefit of clergy.</p>
        <p>THE TESTIMONIALS Published on behalf of Hood's Sarsaparilla are as reliable and as worthy your confidence as if they came from your most trusted neighbour. They will stand the closest investigation. They are not purchased, nor are they from our employes, nor are they written up in our office. They are reliable statements from living people, and they prove be- yond a question the POSITIVE MERIT</p>
        <p>of this great medicine. Try it. "For some time my little boy, three years old, had I been troubled with eczema. Since taking Hood's 6arsaparilla he has improved wonderfully. The complaint affected his eves severely, but now they are entirely well. He has also gained a splendid appetite. I trust this will encourage others suffer- ing similarly to give Hood's Sarsaparilla a trial." MRS. ANNIE ELLIMAN, 14, Bunhill-road, Beckenham, S.E. A BLOOD CLEANSER.</p>
        <p>"Messrs. C. I. Hood Sc Co., "34, Snow Hill, London, E.C. "I bought a bottle of Hood's Sarsaparilla for my son. He had pimples on his neck and body, and lam pleased to inform you that one bottle has taken them all away. He seams all right now, and if at any time he or any of my family requires anything for cleansing the blood, I shall send for some more of Hood Sarsaparilla at once. I can confidently recommend Hood's Sarsaparilla as a blood cleanser." PETER LOCKWOOD, Market-street, I Paddock, Huddersfield.</p>
      </div>
