        <p>Mr. John D. Rockefeller, the Standard oil lmillionaire, has (says our Now York correspondent),added 1,000,000 dots. to his previous gift of 4,000,000 for the endowment if the Chicago University, Ile promises 2,000,000 more of anyone else will subscribe a like sum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>HIDDEN HAND;</head>
        <p>THE HIDDEN HAND;</p>
        <p>CAPITOLA THE MADCAP. BY MRS. E. D. E. N. SOUTHWORTIL</p>
        <p>CHAPTER V. THE DISCOVERY. "And at the magistrate's command They next undid the leathern band That bound her tresses there, And raised her felt hat from her head, And down her slender form there spread Black ringlets rich and rare."</p>
        <p>Old Hurricane meanwhile dined at the public table at the Astor, and afterwards went to his room, to rest, smoke and ruminate. And he finished the evening by supping and retiring to bed. _ _ _</p>
        <p>In the morning, after an early breakfast, he wrote a dozen advertisements, and called a coach and rode around to leave them with the various daily papers for iknmediate publication. Then, to lose no time, he rode up to the Recorder's office to set the police upon the search. As he was about to enter the front portal, he observed the doorway and passage blocked up with even a larger crowd than usual. And seeing the coachman who bad waited upon him the previous day, he inquired of him— "What is the matter here 7"</p>
        <p>" ;Cept a boy tuk up for wearing girls' clothes, or a girl took up for wearing boys', I claim° which," said the man, touching his hat. "Let me pass, then, I must speak to the chief of police," said Old Hurricane, shoving his way into the Recorder's room. "This is not the office of the chief, sir; you will find him on the other side of the hall," said a by- stander. But before Old Hurricane had gathered the sense of these words, a sight within the office drew his steps thither. Up before the Recorder stood a lad of about thirteen years, who, despite his smart new suit of grey casinet, his long rolling black ringlets, and his downcast and blushing face, Old Hurricane immediately recognised as his acquaintance of the preceding day, the saucy young tatterdemalion.</p>
        <p>_ Feeling sorry for the friendless boy, the old man impulsively went up to him and patted him on the shoulder, saying: " What ! in trouble, my lad? never mind—never look down I'll warrant ye an honest lad from what I've seen myself! C 'me, come pluck up a spirit! I'll see you through, my lad!" "'Lad I Lord bless your soul, sir, he's no more a lad than you and I. The young rascal is a girl in boy's clothes, sir I " said the officer who had the culprit in custody. " What—what--what " exclaimed Old Hurri- cane, gazing in consternation from the young prisoner to the accuser; " what—what I my news- boy, my saucy little prince of patches, a girl in boy's clothes !!!" 'Yes, sir—a young scoundrel! I actually twigged him selling papers at the Fulton Ferry this morn- ing I A little rascal " "A girl in boy's clothes A girl!" exclaimed Old Hurricane, with his eyes nearly starting out of his head.</p>
        <p>Just then the young culprit looked up in his face with an expression half melancholy, half mis- chievous, that appealed to the rugged heart of the old man. Turning around to the policeman, he startled the whole office by roaring oat: "Girl is she, sir 7—then, demmy, sir! whether a girl in boy's clothes, or men's clothes, or soldieP or sailor's clothes, or, any clothes, or No clothes, sir! treat her with the delicacy due to womanhood, sir aye, and the tenderness owed to childhood! for she is but a bit of a poor, frien iless, motherless, fatherless child, lost and wandering in your great Babylon! No more hard words to her, sir—or by the everlasting—" "Order," put in the calm and dignified Recorder. Old Hurricane, though his face wam still purple, his veins swollen and his eyeballs glazing with anger, immediately recovered himself, turned and bowed to the Recorder and said:</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir, I will keep order, if you'll make that brute of a policeman reform his language." And so saying, Old Hurricane subsided into a seat, immediately behind the child, to watch the examination. "What'll they do with her, do you think ?" he inquired of a by-stander. "Send her up, in course." "Up 9—where ?" "To Blackwell's Island—to the work'us, in Course." "To the work-house—her, that child 7—the wretches! Urn-m-m-me ! Oh-h-b-h ?" groaned Old Hurricane, stooping and burying his shaggy, grey head in his great hands. He felt his shoulder touched, and looking up saw that the little prisoner had turned around, and was about to speak to him. "Governor." said the same clear voice that he had even at first supposed to belong to a girl— " Governor, don't you keep on letting out that way I You don't know nothing 1 You're in the Recorder's Court! If you don't mind your eye, they'll commit you for contempt l" "Will they? Then they'll do well, lad! lass, I mean, I plead guilty to contempt. Send a child like you to the--1 They shan't do it! Simply, they sham t do it! I—Major Warfield, of Virginia—tell you so, my boy—girl, I meant" "But, you innocent old lion, instead of freeing me, you'll find yourself shut up between four walls, and very narrow ones at that, / tell you! You'll think yourself in a coffin! Governor, they call it— The Tombs!" whispered the child.</p>
        <p>"Attention l" said the clerk. The little prisoner turned and faced the court. And the "old lion" buried his shaggy, grey head and beard in his hands, and groaned aloud. .</p>
        <p>"Now, then, what is'your-name, my lad—my girl, I should say?" inquired the clerk. "Capitols, sir.' Old Hurricane pricked up his ears and raised his head, muttering to himself—" Cap-it-o la! That's a very odd name. Can't surely be two in the world of the same. Cap-it-o lal—if it should be rag Capitola, after all? I shouldn't wonder at all I I'll listen, and say nothing." And with this wise reso- lution Old Hurricane again dropped his head upon his hands.</p>
        <p>"You say your name is Capitola—Capitola what ?" inquired the clerk, continuing the ex- amination. "Nothing, sir." "Nothing I What do you mean ?" "I have no name but Capitols, sir l" "Who is your father?" 'Never had any that I know, sir." "Your mother ?" "Never had a mother either, sir, as ever I heard." "Where do you live 7" "About in spots, in the city, sir." "Oh—oh—oh l" groaned Old Hurricane within his bands. "What is your calling?" inquired the clerk, "Selling newspapers, carrying portmanteaus and packages, sweeping before the doors, clearing oft snow, blacking boots, and so on." "Little odd jobs in general, eh 7" _ _ . _</p>
        <p>"Yes. sir, anything "that lean turn my hand to, and get to do." "Boy—girt I should say—what tempted you to put yourself in male attire?" "Sir r . " In boy's clothes, then 2" "Oh, yes--want, sir—and—and danger, *sir," cried the little prisoner, putting her hands to a face crim- son with blushes, and for the first time since her arrest upon the eve of sobbing. " Oh—oh—oh 1" groaned Old Hurricane from his chair.</p>
        <p>" Want I Danger? How is that 1" continued the clerk. " Your honour mightn't like to know." "By all means. It is, in fact, necessary that you should give an account of yourself," said the clerk. Old Hurricance once more raised his head, opened his ears, and gave close attention. One circumstance he had particularly remarked —the lam usge used by the poor child during her examination was much superior to the slang she had previously ail eat d, to support her assumed character of newsboy. "Well, well—why do you pause? Go on—go on / My good boy—girl I mean," said the Recorder, in a tone of hind encouragement.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VI. A SHORT, SAD STORY. "All! poverty is a weary thing, It burdeneth the brain, It maketh even the little child To murmur and complain." "It is not much I have to tell," began Capitola. "I was brought up in Rag Alley and its neighbour- hood, by en old women named Nancy Grewell." "Ah I" ej culated Old Hurricane. "She was a washerwoman, and rented one scantily-furnished room from a poor family named Simmons."</p>
        <p>" Oh r cried Old Hurricane. 'llaranDY. es I called her. waa verr good to Mes</p>
        <p>and I never suffered cold, nor hunger, —until about eighteen months ago, when Granny took it into her head to go down to Virginia." "Humph 1" exclaimed Old Hurricane. "When Granny went assay, she left me a little money and some good clothes, and told me to be sure to stay with the people where she left me, for that she would be back in about a month. But, your honour, that was the very last I ever saw or heard of poor Granny. She never came back again; and by that I know she must have died." " Ah-h-h 1" breathed the old man, puffing fast. "The first month or two after Granny left, I did well enough. And then, when the little money was all gore, I eat with the Simmons s, and did little odd jobs for my food. But by and by Mr. Simmons got out of work, and the family fell into want, and they wished me to go out and beg for them. /just couldn't do that; and so they told me I should look out for myself." "Were there no customers of your grandmother that you could have applied to for employment!" asked the Recorder.</p>
        <p>"No, sir. My Granny's customers were mostly boarders at the small taverns, and they were always changing. I did apply to two or three houses where the landladies knew Granny; but they didn't want me." "Oh-h-h !" groaned Major Warfield, in the tone of one in great pain. "I wouldn't have that fellow's conscience for a good deal," whispered a spectator, "for, as sure as shooting, that gal's his unlawful child." "Well—go on. What next ? " asked the clerk. "Well, sir, though the Simmons's had nothing to give me except a crust now and then, they still let me sleep in the house, for the little jobs I could do for them. But at last Simmons got work on the railroad a way off somewhere, and they all moved away from the city." "And you were left alone I" . .</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir, I was left alone in the empty, un- furnished house. Still it was a shelter, and I was glad of it, and I dreaded the time when it would be rented by another tenant, and I should be turned into the street."</p>
        <p>"Oh 1 oh! oh, Lord!" groaned the major.</p>
        <p>"But it was never rented again; for the word went around that the whole row was to be pulled down; and so I thought I had leave to stay, at least as long as the ruts did," continued Capitola, with somewhat of her natural rougish humour twinkling in her dark, grey eyes. "But now did you get your bread I" inquired the Recorder.</p>
        <p>"Did not get it at all, sir. Bread was too dear! I sold my clothes, piece by piece, to the old man, over the way, and bought corn meal, and picked up trash to make a fire, and cooked a little mush every day in an old tin can that had been left behind. And so I lived on for two or three weeks. And then when my clothes were all gone—except the suit I had upon my back—and my meal was almost out, instead of making mush every day I econo- mised, and made gruel." "But my boy—my good girl, I mean—before you became so destitute, you shou'd have found some- thing or other to do,",said the Recorder. . . .</p>
        <p>"Sir, I was trying to get jobs every hour in the day. I'd have done any hing honest. I went around to all the houses Granny knew, but they didn't want a girl. Some of the good-natured landlords said, if I was a boy now, they could' me opening oysters, but as I was a girl, they hh, no work for me. I even went to the offices to get papers to sell, but they told me that crying papers was not proper work for a girl. I even went down to the ferry-boats and watched for the passengers coming ashore, and ran and offered to carry their carpet-bags or portmanteaus; but some growled at me, and others laughed at me, and one old gentle- man asked me if I thought he was a North American Indian, to strut up Broadway with a female behind him carrying his pack. And so, sir, while all the ragged boys I knew could get little jobs to earn I bread, I, because I was a girl, was not allowed to carry a gentleman's parcel, or black his boots, or shovel the snow off a shopkeeper's pavement, or put in coal, or do anything that I could do just as well as they. And so because I was a girl, there seemed to be nothing but starvation or beggary be- fore me." "Oh, Lord I oh, Lord! that such things should be 1 " cried Old Hurricane.</p>
        <p>_..... keep I girl, they had I</p>
        <p>"That was bad, sir! but there was worse behind! There came a day when my meal —even the last dust of it, was gone! Then I. kept life in me by drinking water, and by sleeping. all I could. At first I could not sleep for the gnawing—gnawing—in my stomach; but afterwards I slept deeply, from exhaustion and then I'd dream of feasts and the richest sort of food, and of eating • such quantities ! and really, sir, I seemed to taste it and enjoy it and get the good of it—almost as much: as if it was all true! One me,ning after such a dream I was waked up by a great noise, outside. I staggered upon my feet and crept to the window! aad there, sir, were the workmen all outside, a pull- ing down the house over my head I " "Good Heaven l" ejaculated Old Hurricane, who seemed to constitute himself the chorus of this drama. "Sir, they didn't know that I or any one was in the empty house! Fright gave me strength to rtm down stairs and run out. Then I stopped. Oh! I stopped and looked up and down the street! What should I do? The last shelter was gone away from me l—the house where I had lived so many years and that seemed like a friend to me, was falling be- fore my eyes! I thought I'd just go and pitch my- self into the river, and end it all I " "That was a very wicked thought," said the Recorder.</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir, I know it was; and besides, I was dreadfully afraid of being -uffocated in the dirty water around the taut I " said Capitols, with a sparkle of that irrepressible humour that effervesced even through all her trouble. "Well, sir, the hand that feeds young ravens kept me from dying that day. I found a five-cent piece in the street, and reolved not to smother myself in the river mud as long as it lasted. So I bought a muffin, ate it, and went down to the wharf to look for a job. I looked all day, but found none, and when night came I went into a lumber-yard and bid myself behind a pile of planks that kept the wind off me, and I went to sleep and dreamed a beautiful dream of Hying in a handsome house, with friends all around me, and everything good to eat, and drink, and wear!" "Poor, poor child; but your dream may come true yet I "muttered Old Hurricane to himself. "Well, your Honour, next day I spent another penny out of my half-dime, and looked in vain for work all day, and slept at night in a broken-down omnibus that had happened to be left on the stand. And FO, not to tire your patience, a whole week passed away. I lived on my half-dime, spending a penny a day for a muffin, until the last penny was gone, and sleeping at night wherever I could— sometimes under the front stoop of a house, some- times in an old broken carriage, and sometimes be- hind a pile of boxe,, on the sidewalk!"</p>
        <p>"That was a dreadful exposure for a young girl," said the Recorder.</p>
        <p>A burning blush flamed up over the young crea- ture's cheek, as she answered: " Yt s, sir, that was the worst of all; that finally drove me to putting on boy's clothes."</p>
        <p>"Let us hear all about it." "Oh, sir—l can't—l—how can Ii Well, being always exposed, sleeping out-doors, I was often in danger from bad boys and bad men," said Capitola, and dropping her head upon her breast, and cover- ing her crimson cheeks with her hands, for the first time she burst into tears and sobbed aloud. "Come, come, my little man I—my good little woman, I mean—don't take it so to heart! You couldn't help it !" said Old Hurricane, with rain- drops glittering even in his own stormy eyes. Capitola looked up with her whole countenance flashing with spirit, and exclaimed, "Oh ! but I took care of myself, sir I I did, indeed, your Honour! You mustn't, either you or the old gen- tleman, dare to think but what I did." "Oh, of course! of course I" said a by-stander, laughing. Old Hurricane sprang up, bringing his feet down upon the floor with a resouLd that made the great hall ring again, exclaiming: "What do you mean by of course," of course,' you v llian ? Demmy I I'll swear she took care of herself, you varlet; and if any man dares to hint otherwise, I'll ram his falsehood down his throat with the point of my walking-stia, and make him swallow both !"</p>
        <p>"Order, order !" said the clerk. Old Hurricane immediately wheeled to the right- about, faced and saluted the bench in military fashion, and then said:</p>
        <p>, "Yes sir 1 I'll regard order I but, in the mean- while, if the court does not protect this child from insult, I must, order or no order " and with that the old gentleman once more subsided into his seat.</p>
        <p>"Governor, don't you be so noisy! You'll get yourself stopped up into a jug next! Why, you remind me of an uproarious old fellow poor Granny used to talk about, that they called Old If urricane, because he was so stormy l" whispered Capitola, turning towards him. " Humph she's heard of me, then !" muttered the old gentleman, to himself. "litrelL mesa Idiss—go on I" said the</p>
        <p>clerk, addressing C`apitola. "Yes, sir. Well, your Honour, at the end of five days, being a certain Thursday morning, when I couldn't get a job of work for love nor money, when my last penny was spent for my last roll--and my last roll was eaten up—and I was dreading the gnawing hunger by day, and the horrid perils of the night, I thought to myself if I were only a boY, might carry packages, and shovel in coal, and do lots of jobs by day, and sleep without terror by night! And then I felt bitter against fate for not making me boy! And so thinking and think and thinking, I wandered on until I found myself in Rag Alley, where I used to live standing right 4 l between the pile of broken brick's, plaster, and lumber, that used to be my home, and the old pawnbroker's shop where I sold my clothes for meaL And then, all of a sudden, a bright thought _ struck me: and I made up my mind to be a boy "Made up your mind to be a boy !" "Yes, sir! for it was so easy ! I wondered how I came to be so stupid as not to have thought of it before! I just ran across to the old shop, and offered to swap my suit of girl's clothes, that was good, though dirty, for any, even the raggedest suit of boys' clotheshe had, whether they'd fit me or not, so they would only stay on me. The old fallow put up his finger to his nose, as if he thought I'd been stealing and wanted to dodge the police. S° he took down an old, not very ragged, suit that he said would fit mit me and opened a door, and t?!.cl • me to go in his daugli"tea's room and put 'ern on. " Well ! not to tire your honours, I went into that little back parlour a girl, and I came out boy, with a suit of pants and jacket, with my ha" cut short and a cap on my head! The pawlahroke‘r.. gave me a penny roll and a sixpence for my hlar- ringlets." " All seemed grist that came to his mill 1" said Old Hurricane. "Yes, Governor, he was a sealer in genera, Well, the first thing I did was to hire myself to hirat at a sixpence a day, and find myself, to shovel his coal. That didn't take me but a day. Se at night he paid me, and I slept in peace behind 11, stack of boxes. Next morning I was up before the sun, and down to the office of the little pennY paper, the Morning Star.' I bought two dozea of 'ern, and ran fast as I could to the ferry-boat° to sell to the early passengers. Well, sir, in ail hour's time I had sold ont, and pocketed just two shillings, and felt myself on the high road t° fortune 1" "And so that was the way by which you came t° put yourself in male attire ?" " Yes, sir! and the only thing that made nie feel sorry was to see what a fool I had been, not to turn to a boy before, when it was so easy! And fro' that day forth I was happy and prosperous ! found plenty to do? I carried carpet-bags, td horses, put in coal, cleaned side-walks, Dlee—,,t gentlemen's boots, and did everything an honest gentlemen's could turn his hand to! And so for more xi 11. year I was as happy as a king, and should bilve kept on so only I forgot and let my hair grow; and instead' of cutting it off, just tucked it up undet my cap; and so this morning, on the ferry-bt; in a high breeze, the wind blowed of my cap sod "- policeman blowed on me !" "'Twasn't altogether her long hair, your honotir ; for I had seen her before, having known her will; she lived with old Mrs. Grewell, in Rag Alley, interrupted the officer. "You may sit down, my child," said the Recorder in a tone of encouragement. (To be continued.)</p>
