      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. LIBERAL VICTORIES.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON MUNI- CIPAL ELECTIONS. LIBERAL VICTORIES.</p>
        <p>Friday was a very favourable day tor the hold- ing of the annual municipal elections, as the weather was very fine. Consequently the voting generally Was very steady during the whole of the day. A large number of conveyances were em- ployed by the various candidates, and there were many evidences of a contested election. As is usual in elections, both sides were confident, and it was pretty evident that both sides were putting in all they knew to obtain the victory. There were pol- tinge in eight out of the nine wards, and in all but Bewsey there was a straight fight between Liberals and Tories, the only ward which was uncontested being Howley, for which Councillor Canned was re- II turned unopposed. TOWN HALL WARD (1028 VOTERS). The peiline booth in this wird was at the Drill Hall, Bath-street, the presiding officers being Messrs. John Harrison and James Bolton,and the polling clerks, Messrs. Sydney Wellhead and John Houghton. The candidates were Mr. George Clay (L) and Mr. R. Heaton (C). The voting in this ward was pretty brisk; and as it is the premier ward of the town both sides exerted themselves to capture it. There was very little mural literature, besides the addresses of the candidates, each candidate devoting his eneraies to canvassing.</p>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD. (1,016 VOTERS). The polling booth was at St. Barnabas school, and the presiding officers were Messrs. Stanley Wilson, and W. H. Woodcock, and the polling clerks, Messrs. T. H. Spinks, and W. Ball. The candidates were Mr. F. W. Monks (L). and Mr. J. Billings (C). The officials had however very little to do, for the contest was rendered farcical owing to the announcement by Mr. Bi'lingo that he did not intend to proceed with the election. As his retirement did Not come in time to be legally valid, the polling booth had to.be opened, and Mr. Monks having registered a vote for himself, the booth was closed at 9.10 a.m., and thus the contest concloded. BEWSEY WARD (730 VOTERS). There were two polling bool he in this ward, viz., at 98 Hoyle-st., and at St. Ann's school. The pr siding officers were Messrs. T. Fl. Granger, and J. R. Williams.' and the polling clerks, Messrs. J. M. Bate, and A. Clive. The candidates were Messrs. S. J. Beaman (L), and W. Rogers (I). The Conservatives had agreed to compro- nahle this ward on condition that the Liberals did not oppose Dr. Cannell in Howley ward, and the Liberals having agreed to this, it was anticipated that Mr. Beaman would have a walk over. At the last moment however, Mr. Rogers came out as an independent, and despite all the appeals of both Liberals and Conservatives, he ret used to retire, and he has prosecuted a vigorous canvass. The I contest has been purely on personal grounds and politics have been kept in the background. The.polling was ex- pected to be very Ira vy</p>
        <p>ORFORD WARD (853 VOTERS). In this ward the polling booth was in the Silver-street school, and the presiding officers were Messrs. FL Woods and E. Bradshaw, and the polling clerks, Mesrra. T. S. Steel and S. Howarth. The candidates were Mr. J. Wilkinson (L), and Mr. J. Grace (C). Mr. Wilkinson hen contented himself with canvassing and with appeals on the walls, but Mr. Grace has held a public meeting at which he received the benediction of Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P. It was, however, very evident that Mr. Wilkinson had a strong following in the ward, and the neighbour- hood of the polling booth presented a very lively appear- ance during the whole of the day. ST. JOHN'S WARD (1,543 VOTERS). This ward which is the largest in the town had one wiling booth in the old club room, John-street. The presiding officers were Messrs. F. R. Roberts,J. Sutcliffe ' and A. J. Willett, and the poll clerks, Messrs. W. T. Flood, F. Broady and J. W. Howarth. Mr. William Furnesa had been chosen as the Liberal candidate a fort- night before the Conservatives secured a champion in ' he person of Mr. J. Bolden. A heavy poll was also taken in this ward, and there was a large amount of mural literature. Each candidate worked hard during the day, and considerable feeling was displayed between the sup- porters of the two candidates, owing to a letter which was is ued to the Irish voters which Mr. Furness declares was very unfair. Mr. Furness has fought the ward previously, and it was very evident that he was making a bold bid for victory yesterday. The question of the wharf for Msssrs. Bishop and Co. has been much to the fore in this election, as the Company have their works in the ward, and many electors were influenced by it, as the retiring councillnr, Mr. Hill, had been an opponent to the proposal of the Rivers' Committee. FAIRFIELD WARD (899 VOTERS). The polling booth at this ward was in the Fairfield schools. The presiding officers were Messrs. C. H. Unsworth and W. D. Jeans, jun., and the poll clerks, Messrs. George Francis and T. G. Litton. The candidates were Messrs. D. S. Duncan (L), and R. T. Fairclough (C) The chief question before the electors of this ward was the wharf for Messrs. Bishop and Co.,as Mr.Fairclough was considered to be one of the chi&amp; opponents of the Rivers Commit- tee's proposals Neither side held any meetings but can- vassing was energetically prosecuted. Mr. Duncan had a little more literature on the walls than Mr. Fair- dough, hut neither indulged in this kind of electioneer- ing work to any considerable extent. The polling was very slow up to dinner time, but in the afternoon it grew a little brisker. At no time of the day, however, were the polling officials very busy. ST. AH,TIN'S WARD (897 VOTERS). This is the smallest ward of the town, in the point of population, but owing to its rather peculiar geography there were two polling booths,one at the Infant's school, Cairo-st., and the other at St. Luke's school, Liverpool- road. The presiding officers were Messrs. R. Henry,and E. A. White, and the poll clerks, Messrs. T. Westbrook, and J. Smith. The candidates were Mr. D. Tinnion (L), and Mr. W. Gill (C). The contest has been fought in a great measure upon the proposed cattle market which the Markets Committee wish to put in the old barracks. Mr. Tinnion is strongly opposed to the scheme, and ac- cused Mr. Gill of being favourable to it. Mr. Gill denied that he ever was favourable to it, but Mr. Tinnion re- joined that when a petition was drawn up against the proposed Cattle Market, Mr. Gill was the first to be asked to sign it and that he refused to do so.</p>
        <p>_ LATCHFORD WARD (893 VOTERS). Polling in La tchford Ward was quiet all day yesterday and a heavy poll was not expected. The Liberals stated that the ward had never been in such a hopeful condition since the time when Mr. Keen's "candle was put out" by Mr. Dale some 25 years ago. The Liberal candidate, Mr. Luke Ellison,is one of the oldest residents in the ward, having resided in Latchford 58 years, and moreover he is one of the most respected men in the district. Not a word could be said against him even by his opponents, and it is wall known that many CorFervatives " made their mark "opposite the name of the Liberal candidate. Good feeling was shown all round,and very little excitement,but plenty of enthusiasm was displayed. Two polling booths weer re- quired to accommodate the voters in this extensive ward, one being at Christ Church School, Wash-lane, and the other at &amp;James' School,Old-road. At the first,Mr.J.W. Whittle was the presiding officer and Mr. H. Lawrence clerk, while the corresponding officials at the latter were Mr. Josiah Longland and Mr. E. J. Smith.</p>
        <p>THE RESULTS OF:THE ELECTION. The results were declared at the Town Hall about mine o'clock as follows : TOWN HALL WARD. R. Heaton (C) 412 G. Clay (L) 4OB Majority No Change. BEWSEY WARD. W. Rogers (I) 297 S. J. Beaman (L)</p>
        <p>Majority Independent Gain ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>T. Wilkinson (L) J. Grace (C)</p>
        <p>Majority No Change ST. JOHN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>W. Furness (14 J. Holden (C) 460 Majority Liberal Gain</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD WARD. D. S. Duncan (L) R. T. Fairclough (C) 332</p>
        <p>Majority _ .. _ 37</p>
        <p>, Liberal Gain ST. AUSTIN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>D. Tinnion (L) W. Gill (C) 279</p>
        <p>Msjority Liberal Gain LATCHFORD WARD. L. Ellison (L) 355 L. Greening (C) 293 -- Majority Liberal Gain Mr. F. W. Monks was returned for Whitecross Ward, his opponent having retired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>AT THE LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>AT THE LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>After the declaration of the poll there was a crowded and enthusiastic meeting in the billiard room at the Liberal Club, Councillor Pierpoint, J.P. presided,and speeches were made by Couhcillors Duncan, Furness, Ellison, Tinnion, Roberts; Messrs. J. Wilkinson,jun., Marratt, and Cron.</p>
      </div>
