      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>THY, EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 9, 18951</head>
        <p>THY, EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 9, 18951</p>
        <p>TOE WORK OF THE SANITARY WORKS COMMITTEE. SPEECH BY MR. F. W. MONKS.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held on Tuesday evening in the St. Barnabas schoolroom, Warrington,in support of the can- didature of Mr. F. W. Monks for the Whitecross ward. Although the contest was rendered somewhat farcical by the belated retirement of Mr. Billinge from the contest, there was a large attendance. The chair was occupied pro tem by Councillor Roberts. The CHAIRMAN said that the situation was somewhat complicated. As they ware aware, the gentleman who was nominated to oppose Mr. Monks had practically withdrawn from the contest, but unfortunately that withdrawal did not take place in time to make it legally valid. The Town Clerk therefore advised that the poll- ing booths would have to be open from eight in the morning until eight at night. It was therefore the duty of everyone to vote for Mr. Monks. The Conservative party would no take any action in the matter, but still it was well to see that there was no surprise. Mr, Roberts ex- pressed his satisfaction with the work of the Sanitary Committee of which Mr. Monks was chairman, and said that as Mr. Beamao,the vice-chairman of the committee, was being assailed, he would go and do what he could tomake him safe. Mr. Roberts then left the meeting and the chair was taken by Mr. James Evans. Mr. Evetss at once called upon Mr. Monks to give his address. _ . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. MONKS, who was received with applause,said that he felt in an awkward position in the turn of affairs in connection with that election. He had been thinking that they would have a fair and square fight and be ex- pressed his disapproval of the action of those who had forced a contest in that ward. Referring to his work in the Council he said that during the last twelve months he had been rather under the impression that he had not made a very large attendance at the meetings and com- mittees of the Council, but from a return he had just re- ceived he found that after all he came pretty high. The Mayor had made the largest number of attendances as he had made 237 out of a possible 442. He would like to add his testimony to the large amount of work which the Mayor had put in during the year,and also to the courteous manner in which he had discharged his duties. He (Councillor Monks) stood ninth in the num- her of attendances, for he had madell3 out of a possible 193, or about BO percent. (Applause.) In that respect he thought he had acquitted himself to the satisfaction of the electors. He had sat upon six committees—the Watch, Finance, Rivers, Museum, Library, and Techni- cal Instruction, Sanitary Works, and General Purposes. In the opinion of many members those were the most !mem-tent committees which had charge of municipal business, and he thought that to some extent it reflected credit upon them that one of their representatives had been chosen to sit upon such important committees. (Applause.) In addition to that he had also acted as chairman of the Sanitary Works Cernmittee. In his opinion that was one of the most important of the six committeeen whieh he had sat. As chairman he had, of course, a good deal of power in the management of the business, and he had also to take active supervision of the work which was being dope at the Longford Depot. It had given him a great deal of pleasure to look on that work, and the results had been extremely satisfactory. He did not wish to take all the nredit on himself by any means, for Councillor Beaman, his deputy-chairman, had rendered invaluable aid in many ways, and also other members of the committee. It reflected very great credit, be thought, on Mr. Beaman and Mr.Deas that after all the struggles in the past they had managed to get the works at Longford in Its present position. It was In 1871 that work was commenced at Lengford to get rid of that undesirable material which was an essential part of their highly civilised condition. At the outset and for many years there was undoubtedly a very greet nuisance created. They would all remember the long and acrimonious discussion which took place from time to time at the meetings of the Council,and the long newspaper discuseions which followed. Still, the material had to be dealt with, and it was necessary to go on experimenting in order to convert part of it to a valnahle menure Warrington was one of the first,if not the first, town to start the plan of dealing with the con- tents Of the pails, and this was a great credit to the town. In 1891 the cost of the work amounted to £2,187 for the year or equal to a rate of 41. in the £ In 1892 it cost £2,352. In 1893 it became so intolerable that the Council declined to re-elect Alderman Burgess as alderman so that he lost his seat on the Coun- cil and with it the chairmanship of the Sanitary Com- mittee. It was felt that the work was too much for one committee, and the Health Committee was formed to takeover the management of the hospital and matters of that sort, and the Sanitary Works Committee was appointed to look after Longford and the collection of night soil. He (Mr. Monks) was appointed chairman of this committee. At the end of March, 1893, the man- agement of Lonefnrd had been so successful that it only cost the town £B7l, or a saving of £1,300 in the first year of the new arrangement. (Applause.) The re- duction in the cost came in this way. The expenses on wages and repairs were rather more, but then much more money had been made out of concentrated manure, which showed that when a man like Mr. Dees was em- ployed who knew his work and was willing to do it, and as not afraid of standing up for what he considered was the best, that that was the heat method. He (Mr. Monks) was taken by surprise when he was asked to be chairman of the committee, and when he took the posi- tion he was determined that he was not going to manage Longford like he managed hie own works. He was told that the former chairman spent one portion of every day at Lnneferd. As a matter of fact, he had not found it necessary to visit the place more than about once in two months. If the former chairman had been less a mane ger,he would have done far better,and there would have been far greater eetietaction. In March, 1894, the cost of Longford was £l,BBO which was much larger than in the previous year. But the inerease was not in any way caused by the reduced amount of manure being made, as they had to put in a new boiler and several other items had to be paid for. But for the holler, the charge on the rates for Longford would only have been Id. in the £. (Applause.) In March, 1895, the cost WAS less than ever, being enly £774, a charge of only 14 1. in the £. (Applause.) The work to-day was in better order than it had ever been before, and they were likely during the present CPA, to have still more satisfactory results, as the pop- ulation was increasing, and the amount of material they could deal with was larger. He considered thee it was ewe satisfactory that during the time he had been chairman of the Sanitary Works Committee, the coat to the rates ef Longford had been reduced from 3,k1 to 11el. in the £. (Applause.) In addition to this, the commit- tee had also altered the system of collecting the pails, and now instead of going through the streets during the day-time to the annoyance of everybody, they got that work done at night. He had only heard one person complain or this, end he lived near an intercepting depot and said he could not sleep at nights for the noise. He (Mr. Monks) advised him to leave that part of the town and he had followed his advice, and had not complained since. (Laughter.) In addition to being chairman of the Sanitary Works Committee, he was also a member of the Finance Committee, and a reform which he could claim here, was in the better control of that committee over the expenditure. Formerly, the committee did not present their accounts until the end of the year, but It WRS now arranged that estimates of expenditure be made fer each month. Some of the committees did not like thisehist it was only right that the committee which was reeponsible for the expenditure should have better control over it, and a much better control was obtained by this means. (Applause.) At the Watch Committee during the past twelve months, there had not been any exciting questions hut he would just refer to the ap- pointment of the new Chief Constable. Mr. Hunt had resigned after a lone period of service and had given every satisfaction to the Watch Committee and the town at large. Mr. Talbot who had been appointed in his place Came with a great reputation and had excellent testimonials, and he believed he would be a worthy successor to Mr. Hunt. (Applause.) He hoped that Mr. Talbot would keep an eye to the obstructions ir the streets and various other matters. Bridge-street nd other streets in the town required widening, but he thought that the ratepayers ought to have a few years to recover from the bad trade which they had suffered from of late, and he did not want to raise rates at the present time. HP had also sat upon the Rivers Committee which had done such important work in regard to the Manchester Ship Canal. He went to the Council not with the object of benefiting his firm, and .as any action which he took in those negotiations might have been mis-construed,he did not take any part in the negotiations, and, therefere, when Councillor Roberts explained them to the Council, they came to him with- out any Preparation and rather as a surprise. He was pleased that the committee had been so successful in their negotiations as they offered facilities such as the town never had before. It would be a great disappoint- ment to him if those facilities were not taken advantage of. There was one firm who wished to take advantage. and he was sorry to say the Council had snubbed them —(shame)--in the most disagreeable manner. He did what he could to convert the members of the Council, but he had not succeeded. Messrs. Bishop ond Co. ,could have had the land in spite of the Council, but they preferred to leave it with the Council, and the latter then refused them permissien. If the wharf had been placed there, things might have been brought from Liverpool or Manchester at little more than half the cost, and that might make all the difference between carrying on a business I comfortably, and doing all they could to make ends meet. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Mr. Monks then proceeded to deal with the Museum,etc.,Committee and General Purposes Committee, and at the conclusion of his speech he dent...oat-n.l the introduction into the elections of a party contest. Ho considered that when he was in the Council, he had a duty to perform to his Conservative as well as to his Liberal friends, and had he been asked to fight simply a party fight he would have refused. He hoped that he would secure their votes on Friday as untortunately a poll would have to he taken, and they must make the best of it, and he hoped that they would give him a good majority. (Applause.) Councillor PLuxyrox moved a vote of confidence in , Mr. Monks, pledging all to support him on Friday. In ; doing so, Councillor Plumpton spoke highly of Mr. Monks' work on the Council. With regard to his own work, Mr. Plumpton said that he lead promised when</p>
        <p>returned to the Council, he would do all he could for the paving and lighting of back passages in the ward, land' ' he contended that ha had caused a great improvement In these matters. Whitecross ward had received a large amount of attention, and the Wing and Sewerage Com- mittee had done a great deal of work owing to his efforts. (Applause.) Mr. I. HAYWARD seconded the motion which was car- ried unanimously, and the meeting then terminated.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL MEETING IN ST. JOHN'S WARD.-THE WHARF QUESTION.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. BISHOP AND CO. AND THE PROPOSED</p>
        <p>WHARF.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held on Monday evening in the Brick- St. School, in support of the candidature of Mr. William Furness for the St. John's ward. There was a good attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. Thomas Birchall, J.P. and there were also on the platform, Councillors Shaw Green and Roberts, and Messrs. W. Furness (candidate) D. S. Duncan (Liberal candidate for Fairfield ward), S. Mather, E. L. Fleming and Mr. A. Cook. . _ .</p>
        <p>The CREWMAN in the course of his remarks said that of all the institutions of the 'country, nothing scarcely had conduced more to the advantage, tie greatness, and the general usefulness of the country than municipal government. He was old enough to remember quite a different condition of things !torn the present, and War- rington before it had municipal government was a much worse place than to-day. Mr. Birchen then men- tioned the bad condition of the town both in paving and in sanitary matters greatly, and contrasted the present state of the town with that period. He said that a great deal still remained to be done, and could be done by sending the right sort of people to the Town Council. They wanted men of business capacity, men that had 1 shown a capacity for attending to their own business, and not men who had been failures in other depart- ments. They wanted men who wwld feel it their duty to try and bring trade into the town. They had an ill- ustration of the opposite kind which he hoped they would not forget. Messrs. Bishop and Co. wanted a wharf to bring up goods to the town, and make use of the river. For some inexplicable reason, they were re- fused. Be thought that reflected great discredit upon the municipal authorities. (Hear, hear.) Mr. T. MATIIER said that he had been in the ward for nearly 50 years, and he believed that Mr. Furness was the right man to represent it in the Town Council. He proposed a resolutioa pledging the meeting to use every legitimate effort to secure Mr. Furness' return at the head of the poll. Mr. COOK seconded the resolution. nMr.E. L.FLEMING in aupporting the resolution referred at some length to the refusal of the Corporation to gran Messrs. Bishop and Co. the puwei to construct a wharf. It was, he said solely through the representations of Messrs. Bishop and Co. that the laud had been obtained by the Corporation, and he contended that when a Cor- poration or an individual Purchased land for an express purpose,and then kept it themselves,they were guilty of a dishonourable action. (Applause.) Mr. Fleming then proceeded to show the advantage of river traffic over railway traffic. He showed that the former was by far the cheaper, and said that if the carriage of goods were taken into consideration Warrington was practically as far off Liverpool as Philadelphia as the carriage to either place was the same. He hoped that the electors of St. John's Ward would send a message to the Town Council to prevent them putting this question off any longer. (Hear, hear.) The resolution was carried unanimously. Mr. Fullness, in his reply, expressed his strong disap- proval of the course the Council had taken with regard to Messrs. Bishop and Co. He also thought that the property in the leading streets should be purchased as soon as possible with a view to widening the streets. As the town grew, the property in the centre of the town rose in value, and it was therefore prudent to acquire it without delay. He thought that much of the money spent in widening the streets would be recouped again; by various means, such as the laying of a tram line. (Hear, hear.) Councillor SHAW GREBE also addressed the meeting. Ho spoke in eulogistic therms of Mr. Furness, and said that the Council was in need of more young men, who could give expression to the wants of the town. Councillor ROBERTS referred to the wharf question,and said that the Rivers Committee did not intend to let the matter drop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MEETING OF BEWSEY ELECTORS.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF BEWSEY ELECTORS.</p>
        <p>A meeting in support of the candidature of Mr. Bea man, the Liberal candidate for Bewsey Ward, was held in Wycliffe Hall on Tuesday. The chair at the com- mencement of the meeting was occupied by Mr. T. Morris,and later on by Mr. T. Flood. Amongst others on the platform were Councillors Beaman, Roberts, and Shaw Green, and Mr. E. L. Fleming. Mr. T. MO ' RRIS in the course of his remarks,alluded in high terms to the qualification of Mr. Beaman as a 1 town councillor and to the work that he had done in the Council. He had been told that some ironworkers had declared their intention to vote against Mr. Beaman be- cause he had stopped them from working on Saturdays in the mills with which he was connected, but he (Mr. Morris denied that there was any truth in the allegation. He assured them that Mr.Bearnan had had no more to do with stopping them than any one of them in that room had. The stoppage was really due to the slackness of trade which resulted from the miners' strike. Mr. Rickman referred to the calumnies that had been circulated against hiss, and said that a great deal ' more power had been attributed to him than he pos- sessed. He was only a servant and had to take his orders like the rest of them. He had always tried to do bis duty towards his employers,and at the same time towards himself, and his libellers should feel ashamed of their dirty work. (Hear, hear.) Referring to his work in the Council hesaid he had devoted himself par- ticularly to sanitary work, knowing as he did that the workingman's health was his capital. On the Sanitary Works Committee he had supported the night collection of night soil, and at the same time knowing what night work was he advocated an increase of the wages of the workmen and got it. (Applause.) Alluding to the great improvements at Longford, he said that the depot had at one time on an average of four years cost the ratepayers £2,000 a year, but last year if there had been no sinking fund and in- terest on capital to provide for there would have been,for the first time in the history of the Corporation, a profit of £260. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>A resolution in support of Mr.Beaman was afterwards adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>THIIRSDAY.—Before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes.</p>
        <p>Cunt BY ham LABOURERS. —Two Irish agricultural labourers, named John Conniff,. and Michael Hussey, sued Thomas Kelsall, farmer, of Bank House, Penketh, for 10s. each, due to them (they alleged) as harvest money. There was a counter-claim for damages sus- tained through the men leaving the employ of Keine without giving him due notice.—Mr. A. J. Willett. soli- citor, anneared for Cunniffe and Hussey,while the inter- ests of Kelsall were watched by Mr. Browne.—The case for the labourers was that after they had been engaged by Kelsall.and just as harvest time was approaching, he promised them 10s, extra at the finish of the harvesting. When the harvest was gathered in they applied to Mrs. Kelsall for the money, but she put them off, and when they applied for the money on the Monday evening fol- lowing the end of the harvest, Mr. Kelsall drove them off the farm by threatening them with a gun, which was held by his son They accordingly left the farm and ultimately claimed the money through Mr. Willett.— The defence was that the meney was promised to the plaintiff's after they had gathered in the potatoes, and not at the end of the harvest. The men had not been frightened off the farm,as they alleged, but had been requested to remain until the potatoes were got in, and they should then have the harvest money. Instead of doing so, however, they, with two other men, eft the service of Mr.Kelsall without giving any notice, although they had been engaged ata weekly wage,and it was undersfrood that a week's notice would have to be given. Mr. Kelsall had been put to great inconvenience and serious loss in consequence of the men leaving him without giving warning, inasmuch as the whole of the farm work had to be performed imperfectly for quite two days until fresh men could he procured. For that loss he now couoter-claimed.—His Honour,in summing up, said be was afraid there was no evidence on the counter- claim,and that the only question that now remained was the claim. Though the evidence had been most conflict• ing he was inclined to think that the 10s, was not meant to be earned until after the potatoes were got in. The defendants had, therefore, gone away from their work without finishing their contract,and he (the Judge) must decide against them. "OATH AGAINST OATEl."—Robert Raeside, draper, of Manchester, sued Mrs. Gibson, of Sandown-terrace, Latchford, for £2 19i. ed., balance owing for goods sold and delivered.—Mr. Browne, who appeared for the plaintiff,explained that the defendant possessed a receipt which was disputed by plaintiff .—The defendant there• upon produced a receipt for £2 19s. 6d. signed by Mr. Hugh Ingram, but on Mr. Hugh Ingram being called, he said he only gave a receipt for £l, and added that it must have been altered since. The £1 was paid by Mrs Gibson's mother; and he had never seen Mrs. Gibson, the present defendant.—The defendant said she had never had any goods, and had simply found the receipt amongst some papers after her mother had died. She (defendant) had never seen the plaintiff, nor had any transactions with him.—Mr. Raeside was then called, and deposed that he had received an order personally from the defendant for some print, a macintosh, and twelve yards of cashmere.—Mrs. Gibson persisted that she had never seen the plaintiff and had never given ' him any order. If her mother did get things from plaintiff, they must have been paid for by her before her death. She did not remember having had any cashmere or macintosh. She admitted having had some print,but her mother had told her she had paid for that.—His 113nour remarked that it was oath against oath, and he thought it must have been a case of mistaken identity on the part of Mr. Raeside. Judgment was then entered for the defendant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>SEQUEL TO A COLLISION IN BRIDGE-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ST I tEET.</head>
        <p>ST I tEET.</p>
        <p>CLAIM FOR DAMAGES AT THE WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday week before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes,Rena Maggie Mann tobacconist, Bridge-st., sued Edward Wibberley, milk seller,linutsford-road,s nd MesBr4.Greenall,Whitley, and Co., brewers, Wilderspool, for £l5, for damage to window, fixtures, and stock, caused by a collision in Bridge-st., betweed the fl )at of the former and a dray of the latter, on August 6. Mr. C. H. Unsworth appeared for the plaintiff, Mr. A. Browne for the defendant Wibberley, and Mr. Hatton for the defendant Company. Mr. Unsworth said that the plaintiff was a married woman who was suing in respect of a separate estate and she sued the two defendants for negligence. One of them, Wibberley was a milk seller, and the other, Greenall, Whitley and Co., large brewers. She alleged through their negligence, the shop window, several fixtures and stock had been damaged. The plaintiff occupied a tobacconist shop in Bridge-st., which was one of the bu4iest and narrowest streets in the town. While Mr. Wibberley's milk float was standing opposite to Mrs. Mann's shop window on Aug. 6, a dray which was heavily laden with barrels b,longing to Messrs. Greeoall, Whitley and Co., came up the street and collided with the float, the force of the collision forcing Mr. Wibberley's horse into Mrs. Mann's shep window which was roken and great damage done. The plain- tiff alleged that the defendants Messrs. Greenall, Whit- ley and Co. were negligent in not driving their dray in such a manner as to pa s the milk cart in safety, and that Mr. Wibberley was to blame in not putting hi a float in such a position as to prevent an accident. William Ellison, carter in the employ of Wigan Coal and Iron Company, stated that on August 6th, he was two yards away from the shop of plaintiff. Wib- berley's milk cart was behind bis coal lurry, on the left hand side of the street going towards Market Gate. The cart was two or three inches from the kerb stone. He was waiting for Messrs.Greenall Whitley and Company s drag to go past. While passing Wibberley's milk float, the hind wheels of t e drag sewered on one side which he thought was caused by the channel flags in the street and collided with Mr. Wibberley's float. This lifted the pony right into the window of Mrs. Mann's tobacco shop. After the collision; the carter with the dray drew up his horse. He was sitting on the shafts at the time of the co.lision. He did not notice any one in charge of Me. Wibberlev's cart at the time of the accident, but after the accident a boy came to the float. The window was broke by the accident and the glass of the winnow had to be cut away to get the pony out. Richard Cooper Shaw, butcher,Bridge-street,said that he remembered the accident. lie was coming out of his shop and heard a collision in the street. He saw Wib- berley's pony swerving into Mann's shop window. He ran down and got the pony's head and broke the glass round its head So that it would not cut itself. He tried to back the pony and float away, but Greenall, Whitley and Company's dray had jammed it so that he could not stir it, either one way or the other. 118;did not see any- one in charge of the milk cart. William Horace Mann, husband'oft the plaintiff, and manager of the shop, deposed that he caree home at night of August 6, and then found the window broken. It was a plate glass window sft. by 6. for which he paid £5 10s. Mr. Mann then gave particulars of various fixtures which had been broken. The damage to the stock such as tobacco, pipes, Ize., he valued at 503. The ' shop was not closed but the shutters were up for three days afterwards. He estimated the damage to his trade to be about £3 in consequence.—ln cross-examination : The witness admitted the white opal letters were put up by Hignetts,and he intffered no loss thereby. The insur- ance company had put the glass window in, and he had not put the claim in. A portion of his stock was "dummy" He could not make his shop pay and was about to close business. ' '</p>
        <p>„.„„„ .... Robert Lowe, Market Gate, auctioneer and valuer, said that he was also the assPssor of a free insurance company. The glass window cost £3 113 s. id. and had been paid by the company. He then went in detail of the various articles which had been damaged. Mr. Arthur Browne submitted that his client had not been guilty of any negligence. The Judge said that he could not strike Mr. Wibber- ley's name out of the case at that stage, an Mr. Browne must prove that there was a negligence on the part of Mr. Wibberley. There should have been someone at the pony's head. Mr. 13rowne : They might have been killed. His Honour said that did not affect the case.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said that the weight of the dray would have knocked the float m one side even if some one had hold of the horse's head. _</p>
        <p>Hie Honour however declined to agree with :Mr. Browne.</p>
        <p>—.„....„. Mr. Browne, in a further statement, said that at the time of the accident, Mr. Wibberley was about a yard away from the float. Assuming, however, that Mr. Wibberley was responsible, he contended that the claim was exorbitant and got up for the purpose of the action.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hatton contended that there was no negligence on the part of his clients. He would bring evidence which he thought would satisfy his Honour that the dray was being driven with proper care along the streets and that It would have passed easily and that there was plenty of room had not the pony in the float stepped on to the parapet for some reason and caused the end of the cart to catch the hind wheel of the dray. Edward Wibberley, Mrs. Wibberley, and William BettPs, were then called as witnesses by Mr. Browne. Mrs.Wi hberley said the pony had never backed or gibbed, and that she swooned after the accident. Joseph Arnold, the carter of the dray (called by Mr. Hatton) said that at the time of the accident he was sitting in front of the dray in the usual place. He crossed ever about the length of the waggon away to the other side of Bridge-street, on the day in question to pass the float. He was past the pony's head with the horses and front wheels when he heard a shout and saw that the poney was swerving round into the window. He stopped the dray and helped to get the poney out. His lorry was straight when passing the float and was near the kerbstone. There was plenty of room to pass. He had never had any trouble with passing vehiclesin the street before or since.—By Mr. Unsworth : He did not think that it was safer to lead two horses than to drive them.</p>
        <p>. ------ Joseph Caldwell, Winwick-road, farmer and milk seller said that he saw the accident. Be was leading a horse just behind the dray. The dray was grazing the kerbstones when passing the float. As the dray was passing the float, be noticed the pony put its feet on the parapet, and this caused the step to catch the hind wheel of the dray, which would have passed the float had the pony stood still. P.C. Turner said that be was on duty at Market Gate at the time of the accident. Be at once went to the epot. The footboard of the milk float was fast in the near hied wheel of the brewer's dray. Mr. Unsworth submitted that it was negligence for a person to leave a horse and cart in a street without any- one to attend to it. He contended that it had been shown that no one was in charge. As to the damages claimed he contended that they were fair. His Honour said that he thought there was do ebauist that Messrs. Greenall Whitley and Co.'s carter was neg- ligent. Ellison's evidence had not been rebutted in the least by anyone. What Caldwell said had happened I might have been caused by the accident. He also I thought there was negligence on the part of Mr.Wibber- ley by not leaving some one in charge of the pony. The question was did that contribute to the accident. He did not think that if any one had been in charge it wmuld have prevelted the accident. He would non-suit the plaintiff with regard to Wibberlev but would not allow costs as he had been negligent in one particular. He would give judgment for £8 188.10 d. against Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TWO AMERICAN SENSATIONS.</head>
        <p>TWO AMERICAN SENSATIONS.</p>
        <p>Verdicts have been given in two trials for murder which have excited a wet amouct of interest in th United States. At San Francisco Theodore Durrant,, secretary of a Sunday school, has been found guilty, after a trial which extended over three months, of murdering a young girl whose body ha secreted in a lumberroom in a church. He had to be tried OR a aecond charge of murdering another girl. The public " cheered wildly" on hearing the verdict. At Phili- delphia a man named Holmes has been convicted of the murder of Benjunin Pitezol, with the object of obtain- ing a large sum of money for which his victim's life was insured. It is alleged that Holmes has murdered a large number of people whose lives were insured.</p>
        <p>To MoTrrEns.—Mrs. Winslow Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers for theirchildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste; it produceB natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the little cherub awakes "as bright as a button." Of all herniate. la.'l d. per bottle.—Adat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>BOARD OF TRADE AND THE</head>
        <p>THE BOARD OF TRADE AND THE</p>
        <p>RAILWAYS.</p>
        <p>The second annual report of the Board of Trade on the operation of the Railway Regulation Act of 1893 in regard to hours of labour was issued on Tuesday. It states that the phenomenally lon hours have to a large extent disappeared—giving pirticulars of various cases, —and that in the future it will be the duty of the De- partment to deal with the more delicate and difficult questions which arise on representations of overwork made by or on behalf of a class or classes of servants— not as to whether a man should be employed at a stretch for sixteen or eighteen hours, but whether his term of duty should be a tweive,an eight,or a ten hours' shift.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>PERILS OF MEDICAL MEN.</head>
        <p>THE PERILS OF MEDICAL MEN.</p>
        <p>The perils to which medical men are exposed in the practice of their profession have been illustrated by the following case. A man came under the care at a well- known operating surgeon for the purpose of undergoing an operation. Before, however, submitting to the pro- cedure, he displayed great anxiety to know what the chances were of his recovery. The assurances which were given him upon this point were apparently deemed to be satisfactory, and the operation was performed. Two days afterwards, when the nurse was arranging the patient's bedclothes, she discovered a revolver secreted beneath one of the pillows. Observing a diszreet silence, she took the weapon and put it in a place of safety in a distant part of the room. Nothing was 53id on the sub- ject until the patient became convalescent. She then inquired of him why he had placed the revolver under his pillow. His calm, laconic reply was—" That is easily answered, nurse. If my wound had beenidoing badly I intended to shoot my surgeon and after that I should have shot myself." It is needless to say that the sur- geon knew nothing of the peril that he incurred until long after that event.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL NOTES.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL NOTES.</p>
        <p>Some of the councillors at the Town Council meeting on Tuesday week declared that they were eager for electioneering and that they would much rather be in the wards canvassing than in the Council Chamber. Perhaps this was the cause of the comparative celerity with which the business was despatched, for considering the amount of matter that was contained between the two covers of the agenda the meeting was not a long one. The first subject upon which there was any discussion was that relating to the widening of the streets. Councillor Beanett seems disposed to make this question his own and with characteristic pertina- city he raises it in season and, some other members of the Council appear to think, out of season as well. II sug- gested that the present opportunity should be taken to secure the widening of Springfield-st. be- fore certain buildings the plans of which had been approved were erected. Alderman Sutton, the , ohairman of the Street Improvement Committee, I did not jump at the suggestion, in fact it would be strictly accurate to say that he was disposed to "sit " upon it. He did not enter into the relative merits or demerits of wide or narrow streets, but he made the rather extraordinary statement that the owner of the property in that neighbourhood could build in defiance of the Corporation. this statement was not received without protest, and I Councillor Woods, in particular, was proceeding to ' enlarge upon the subject of " all sorts of incon- gruities," when Councillor Roberts blandly sug- gested amid some laughter that "all sorts of in- congruities" were not on the minutes. The Town Clerk afterwards explained that as the owner of the property in question had complied with the bye-laws he could build as he liked. Councillor Bennett's proposal was then negatived, only three voting in its favour.</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the Health Committee a strong diversion was made on behalf of the poor oppressed property owner. The Health Committee proposed that before a house was closed as unfit for habitation the owners if willing to comply with the notices seed upon them should be allowed fourteen days in which to employ a surveyor and submit a report showing the alterations and improvements they proposed to carry out. On the face of it this resolu- tion seemed to be framed for the benefit of the owner of insanitary property, but several aldermen and councillors did not look at it in that light. It was strongly urged that when the Medical Officer condemned a house the Borough Surveyor should inspect the house and specify what improvements that were needed to make it habitable. This line of argument was pursued by Councillor Snaw Green Alderman Bolton, Councillor Woods, Alderman Wright, Alderman Francomb, and Alderman Hut- chinson all of whom spoke with such fervency that the Mayor was induced to make the remark, " It strikes me that a great many of you gentlemen are property owners." The other side of the question was adequately dealt with by Councillor Roberts, Councillor Plumpton, Alderman Sutton, Councillor Greening and the Mayor. Councillor Roberts warmly repudiated the idea that the borough sur- vey should be specially engaged for the property owners of the town, or that he should hold a brief for any private owner, and be ridiculed the suggestion that pro- perty owners did not, know in what respect their property was defective Councillor Plumpton spoke with indignant warmth against the condi- tion of some of the inhabited houses of Warring- ton, and roundly declared that they were not fit for dogs to live in. He was, therefore, surprised that owners of that kind of property should "stand up and defend it." Alderman Sutton pointed out that before the borough surveyor could possibly undertake the duties which property owners wished to put upon him, they would have to very con- siderably increase his staff, while the Mayor con- sidered that that would be taking the bread out of the mouths of the surveyors and architects of the town. The amendment which had been proposed by Councillor Shaw Green was in the end rejected by thirteen votes to ten.</p>
        <p>Questioned as to the proposed smallpox 'hospital, Alderman Sutton said the Health Committee had not yet adopted any definite scheme. In moving the adoption of the Museum Committee's minutes, Councillor Roberts said the committee had not carried out a suggestion which had been made to them to circulate amongst the schools boxes con- taining natural history specimens because they had not the money to do so. But teachers might take their classes to the Nfuseum, and the Education Department would allow the attendance at OP Museum to Count as an attendance for the purpose of the grant. The pro- posal of the Markets Committee to hold the half- yearly fair on the singularly unsuitable site off Orford-lane did not pass without a protest, but Alderman Blackhurst allayed all further hostility by stating that the arrangement was only a tem- porary one. The report of the Parr Hall Committee did not escape criticism. Councillor Woods was of opinion that the opening concert had been znisman- agod or it would not have resulted in a loss of nearly .£lOO. He referred rather pointedly to his own experience in getting up entertainments, and intimated that more su;table men had been left off the Parr Hall Committee than bad been placed upon It. The report was, nevertheless, adopted by a large majority, and the composition of the committee was unaltered. The remaining business included a vote of thanks to the Mayor. which was appropriately moved by Alderman Har- rison, seconded by Councillor Roberts, and sup- ported by other members of the Council The Mayor in responding to the unanimous vote did not definitely announce his decision with respect to the invitation to again accept office, but he depre- cated the introduction of politics into municipa contests.</p>
        <p>The tunnel at Schemmitz. UT T 7, is the longest ir the world. It ham a length of 10.27 miles, and coat over £1 000,000 to construct Completed in the year 1888,it WWI commenced in the eighteenth century.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>BEST PAID __PROFESSION IN THg</head>
        <p>THE BEST PAID __PROFESSION IN THg</p>
        <p>WORLD.</p>
        <p>Considering the enormous number of people who imagine that they can sing like nightingales, it is a re- markable fact that singing is the most profitable pr, zee- Sion in the world. Mr. Daniel Mayer, the well-known concert agent, has been interviewed by" Cassell'aSatur- day Journal."</p>
        <p>_ " Will you give me some idea of large fees " " Well," said Mr. Mayer," yoa must not forget that years have to be spent before a single penny can be earned. But you are right about big prizes. The largest amount ever received for one recital was £1,400 by Mr. Paderewski at Chicago." " Quite a pleasant little evening." "Yes ; but Mr. Paderewski always takes the greatest sums because he is the sole performer. At his last re: cital in London this year he netted £1,087. Of all pub- lic performers Paderewski is far and avray the greatest draw at the present moment. He has lately arracy7ed for a hundred recitals in America, from which you may be interested to learn he will net at least R 50,000." " Can singers often make a thousand pounds a day? " "I have no doubt they do at times. We know that Madame Pattie does, at all events. In reckoning fees however, not only have puhlic performances to b,;, reckoned, but engagements at private 'at homes ' have to be taken into consideration. Nowadays, 'at homes ' increase the income of popular artiste im- mensely. To my knowledge Mr. Paderewski received five hundred guineas for one number in the programme of an at home ' held at a house in the West End not long ago. Understand, that for that sum he was only required t,9 appear on the platform once. "Mr. Jean de Re•zke will never accept an engwment for an at home.' I was once commissioned to offer him five hundred guinaas to appear at an at home,' but he could not be induced to accept it." " But are these sums fairly general ?" "Most decidedly. The amounts psid to other artistes may not be quite so big, but they are still very large. Besides their regular public engagements, some of the• leading singers occasionally appear at three or four of these • at homes' a week." _</p>
        <p>' "Madame Melba is most in demand amongst ladies, and her fee is invariably two hundred guineas. To give you same idea of Madame Melba's popularity, I need only remark that she has recently signed a contract to appear at forty concerts in America. For this she has been guaranteed £12,000, besides a share of the profits after they have exceeded a certais amount." " M. Plancon, the bass at the Opera, is another singer who is in great request. For one 'at home' his fee is £l2O. Madame Bresna's fee ranges between forty and fifty guineas."</p>
        <p>. - 'As regards English singers, Messrs. Plunkett, Green, and Ben Davies are perhaps the most popular. For an 'at home ' their fee would be between thirty-five and forty guineas."</p>
        <p>"Are these huge fees paid without a murmur?" " Certainly ; otherwise there would be no getting the singers. I have known a lady pay as much as 800 gs. for the services of artistes at a single 'at home.' In addi- tion to this at least £2,000 must have been spent on towers, refreshments. "By the way, I have omitted to mention Mr. Edouard de Reszke, whose fee for an •at home' ranges between 120 and 150 guineas. For this sum he sings two, or at most three songs." "I suppose the De Reszkes earn as much as any- body ?" _</p>
        <p>"Probably; anyhow they could if they liked. Their jiint income from singing must be something like £40,000."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC. In the week ended Friday, November 1, 103 sea-going vessels, with an aggregate net registered tonnage of 33,136, arrived at and sailed from the various docks, wharves, &amp;1., on the Ship Canal. During the preceding week the arrivals and departures numbered 169, and the tonnage amounted to 43,388. The coastwise traffic last week showed a falling off of 81 ships and 8,234 re.. gistered tons, and the foreign traffic a decrease of five ships and 2,018 tons.</p>
        <p>Amongst the cargoes discharged at the Manchester docks were 20 general cargoes, two of timber, three of pig iron,and one each of green fruit and cotton. Twenty- seven vessels cleared outwards with general cargoes,six with coal, and two with pitch. A large number of coast- ing schooners which bring china clay and stone to Rai corn are now towed up to Pomona Docks to load hous coal, which is brought thither in barges from neigh. bouring collieries. A cargo of wheat is now on passage for Manchester from the River Plate per the Adele, e5l tons, which sailed from Rosario on August 17,and is now due at Queenstown. Four more sailing ships are re- ported as fixed for the canal with timber from North America—viz, the Kalstad, 482 tons, which left Mediae on the 16th ult.; the Alert,s3l tons; the W. W. M'Laugh- lan, 471 tons, also from Shediac ; and the Aureolite from Cane Formentine. In the steam department the Alert, 559 tons ; Heimdahl, 336 tons ; Sunpulp, 350 tons Urda, 440 tons ; Granada, 606 tons; and Mancestria, 981. tone, as reported as fixed or on passage for Man- chester with fruit from Spain ; and the Fez, 519 tons, with iron ore from Bilbao.</p>
