      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>W ORTH LIVING</head>
        <p>W ORTH LIVING</p>
        <p>p HELL</p>
        <p>Such was the subject of a celebrated sermon preached in Manehester,but the more practical problem ot the day is how to make life worth living on earth. One of the important tri- butaries of the river of happiness is the possession of a com- fortable home. A home made bright and cheert ul not only by the kind and loving disp fsitions of those living there, but made attractive by the good taste displayed in the furnishing. Furnishing is a very important factor in th - matter of con- tinued self-,atisfaction. So much jerry new London furniture is being rif-fw retailed by drapers that one needs to pause be- fore rushing impetuously into the first draper's because of a cheap rd ticket. High-class makers, such as Lamb,Gillows, and Garuett,still exist, to whom the wealthy can still go with a long puree but to most people this would be a too expensive experiment. Again, the average man nowadays has a strong liking for a bargain ; anything obtainable at about half or quarter the cost has a strong fascination f r him. I. it possi- ble to obtiin such an advantage in furni,hing ? Yes. and more than possible, it is an absolute fact. We cannot do better than recommend a visit to MR. WORSLEY'S FUR- NISHING PANTHEON, GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>THE Successful Establishment of any of the large ware- I houses of Manchester is always of interest to the citizens. One of the most brilliant examples o• the vabie of perseverance, eutermise, and tat Is the now busy hive of industry so well known a The Pantheon, which occupies such a prominent position in Gay thorn, near the 0 ntral Station, Manche ter The building is a massive pile of modern design, 150 feet long, six storeys high, with show- room accommodation covering an area of over one million square tet. The plate-glass windows are each 12 feet square. each sq,,are if bi oken costing nearly £3O to replace. '1 en of thee imfnense show windows fo, ma portion of the frontage. This will give a faint idea of "The Pantheon" in outward appearance, but ah l—here pen fail, alt. gel her in attempting to no justice to anythinake an adi- quote description of the interior. "The Pantheoli " was speci lly built to give the largest and mos" convenient floor space for showrooms, and cost £28,000, the floors specially constructed to carry enormous weight. To-day it stands as one of the smartest furnishing houses in England, containing by far the largest and most comprehensive ,took of any two houses in this large city. Prom basement to roof, from fl•ior to c&amp; ling, 'The Pantheon is filled to overflowing with thousands of well bought, care- fully selected bargains, to attempt description of which would Le as futile as Liverpool attempting o crush THE MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The destinies of' The Fanth ,on" are c fawned and pre- sided over by its sole absolute proorietor, Mr. W. H. Worsley, whose sole stu iy is an ever increasing desire to satisfy and please the thousands of customers who daliy patronise and support his • fforts. We invite the reader to accompany us in an imaginary visit to " he Pantheon' to see what it con- tains and how it is managed On arrtval we are at once strustc with the magnificent</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>STARTLING APPBARANCR</head>
        <p>STARTLING APPBARANCR</p>
        <p>of " The Great Pat.theon,' the immense long line of stupen- dous show windows di.playing for sale capital spemmens of high-class Second hand Furinture ny high-class makers, the sale of which has made "The Pantheon " famous,</p>
        <p>UPON lINTBRING THE BUILDING we , see at once that a far as stock Is concerned there is sufficient furuiture for nearly the whole of Manchester. We see as once visitors walking round inspecting from Oldham, Bolton. Bury, Bur ley, Blt-ckburn,Bacup, Ho, hdale, Preston, Warrington and al parts of Lanca,hire and Yorkshire, Cheshire, the Potteries. Wales, &amp;c. It Is tbe cardinal rule of "The Pantheon " to give the fullest facilities for Inspec- tion without feeling compelled to buy. The ground floor Is filled with the large specimens of bedroom furniture suitable for ttrge rooms; we are told the smaller suites are on the top floor, to which we can travel by means of the hoist, or walk. Bedroom suites on the ground floor we notice by Lemls original cost £l5O, price £35 ; by Goodall, cost £lBO, for £5O; by Henry Ogden. cost £2OO, tor £5. These are among the best suites, but one that strikes us with special attention is a fine handsome Bec'room Suite oy Behan, In solid American walnut, heavily carved tubas relief, with large wardrobe fitted with briliisht crystal bevel mirrt re ; dresbihg table unique with neatly fitted jewel drawers, sur- mounted by large swing dressing glass ; washstand fitted with statuary marble and shaving Iln rro r three chairs. pedestal table cis nuit, and towel horse ; what originally cost £6O you can ob. ain for £25. Over 200 ,droom Suites are in st I,k, on show, from 15s. to £lOO, forming a display so Impressive that wears almost</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>STRUCK DUMB WITH AMAZBMENT.</head>
        <p>STRUCK DUMB WITH AMAZBMENT.</p>
        <p>We now go up stairs to the Dining-room Showroom. Witat a sight, long avenues of sideboards is the first impression ; hundreds of Sideboards, with hundreds of Suites. also Dining Tables, Overmantels. splendid collecrion of Bronzes, Ormolu Clocks, Vases. ac. In this department we find magnificent Sideboards, original cost from £lOO to £2OO. at prices simply astounding, £lO to £2O. The next flo xabove is the Drawing- room Furniture Showroom, which is simply a show of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>DAZZLING BEAUTY</head>
        <p>DAZZLING BEAUTY</p>
        <p>dissolving one's sensitive appreciation into impressive loveli- ness. No department is so intere.ting to la !lea as the draw- ing-room slyiwroom; a keen delight is experienced in selecting weird and fantastic furniture for the drawing-room. Asthetic, Moorish, and Egyptian Cabinets, peculiar Green and eronzed Tables, Occasional Chairs of charming variety, Cosy Corners, Comfortable Lounging Settees and chairs equal to the highest class firms in the Kingdom at quarter the price. The largest showroom for Drawing-room Furniture in Manch star, containing over 100 Suites, 100 Cabinets, 200 Uvermantels, 500 Tables. suites from £3 10a Cd. to £5O, Cabinets from 30s. to £45. Marvellous value, worth in most cases three or four times the money charged. The next depaitment adjoining is cases three or four times the money changed. The next de- partment is the Be istead ind Belicling Department, which ! contains an enormous stock of Bedsteads. The brilliant- ! burnished brass displayed here is so immense as to present ! almost a</p>
        <p>SEA OF GOLD. Magnificent specimens of the brassworkers' art is Imre dis played to perfection. Brass Bedsteads may be purchased solid and complete for as low a price as 355. to the best at £35. A clear sav;ng of 50 per cent, is effected in bedsteads and bedding Brass and IrQn Bedsteads, strong and durable, my be secured from 61. Ed. to 30s. The bedding is all new, manufactured on the premises; everything Is new in this department, the work here giving employment to over 50 hands. Nothing Is allowed to be used here except it has pre- viously been washed and purified. All bedding sold at Wor- slry's may be guaranteed as of SPOTLESS PURPLY.</p>
        <p>Wool Beds complete, guaranteed all wool, from Bs. 60., Hair Mattrmses from 18s. 6d., Feather Beds from 225. 6d. The next place of interest to visit is the Bedroom l'urniture Department, which is displayed in a showroom. 68 yards by 25 yards, larger than most of the combined showrooms of most furnishing establishments. One is bound to admit that The Pantheon is certainty far ahead of any rival establish- ments in point of size and convenience. The showroom visited so far are lofty, well lighted, and of immense area. , The Bedroom F-rniture Miowroom is magnificent; 300' Bedroom Suites are oisplayed in ay.mues, the finest collets- tion extant, at prices simply incredible, astonishing every one. pulverising and exterminating all competition. Com- plete Bedroom Suites, solidthroughout, at 23105. up to £3O, magnificent in style and finish. Bnamelled Suites horn 30s. to £7. 'the next departme:st is for Kltchert Furniture, the varied stock of which is quite sufficient to satisfy the most exacting. Thousands of chairs, hundreds of tables and dressers, every requisite for the kitchen at half the usual prices charged. A department is also provided for Iron- mongery. But we must move on, our examination and in- spection is taking too long and occupying too much sp We now are shown into the Carpet and Oilcloth Department, which contains thousands of new rolls of carpets, Brussels from Is. llad., Tapestry from 'Cad., Axminster from 2s. lleds and so on. Than we see hundreds of Second-hand Carpets. If you want a carpet to beat creation, buy one of Worsley's second-haad carpets ; they knock the competiticn of Jew and Gentile into smithereens. You can get a second-hand Brussels carpet, no tapestry; no, reel, gent.ine Brussels. from 4to 5 I yards by 3to 4 yards, for under a sovereign. Rugs of ins- msmse variety, lovely Skin Rugs, Axminster, infected every 1 conceivable design, shade, and colour, forming a gorgeous display. I • RIYALLING THIS RAINBOW. Oilcloths and Linoleum, are here cut to pieces; thousands of yards are here daily slashed and cut into all shapes to fit the various houses they are intended for. The prices are also cut, in fact, out so fine that you would require a magnifying I glass to discover any profit made out of this department. We saw Oilcloths from lid. per ya.d and Linoleums from 6d. per , yard. The best Scotch Oilcloths and Linele,..ms are displayed 1 from Is. 3d. per yardlto 2s. 11d. We should like to speak of all the wonderful things displayed for sale, but we must be pre- paring to leave The Pantheon in order to catch our train. We, therefore, hu,ry through the Office Furniture Department, the Brass Feeder and ifirebrass Department, the Miscellane- ous Department quickly on to the latest addition, the Musics Department. Hark l What do we hear tJIAGNIPICHNT Bargain in Fine POLt AIM OAK 131 FUBNITUBB by James Lamb, consisting of a truly handsome Sideboard, modern design, 7ft. long, a fins massive piece of high-eless cabinet work, rich coloured pollard oak with ebony lines, brilliant crystal thiJk Bngllsh plate back; elegantly fitted throughout with modern conveniences, richly carved panels in bas-rstlef. The Suite consists of a. dining- room couch, luxuriously uphoisLered; two lounge chairs and 12 fine standard single chairs, with stuffed backs, all finished in Bnedsh morocco and curled hair. An 18ft. Pollard Oak Dining Table, on six carved supports. The lot cost nearly L 440; price for the lot. £75. A dead bargain, just removed from Newiands, Cheetham Hill. Inspect early.-- WORSLIMB.</p>
        <p>_ MISCBLLANBOUS BARGAINS.-Lot 63, very handsome DINING-BOOM SUITEI, by Lamb, cost £75 for £25, In brown oak and morocco; lot 68, heavy mahogany Dining- room Suite. by Goodall, in morormis. cost £65, for £lB 18s., a bargain; lot 75, excellent Dining-roora Suite by Ogder. in Utrecht velvet, massive earvedtrames, cost £6O tor £O2; lot 75, good substantial Dining toom Suite, in walnut,by Dyson, worth 1135, for £7 10s. a bargain; lot 79, milord oak Dining- room Suite, cost £l5O, in morocco, perfect condition, almost new, by Balfour, 1335; lot 125, magnificent walnut Sideboard, cost £5O, for £l5; lot 128, largo Sideboard, In oak, cost £45, for £8; lot l'a, fitt. Sideboard in oak, cost £`,o, for £lO 10..; lot 131, massive mahogany Sideboard. cost £6O, for £l2; lot 133, mahogany Sideboard, love,y condition, coot £B5, for £l5; lot 141, carved walnut Sideboard. early llnglish. £3O. for £7 75.; lot 142, solid heavy Sideboard. worth £25, for £.3 10s ; lot 143, a real chanoe in a Sideboard, for 355. ; lots 215 to 416, 200 Overmantels, from 108.6 d. to £5. equally as good bar- gains as the sideboards; lot 410, Drawing-room Suite, in bilk. £5; lot 411, Drawiag-rocnt Suite, in inlaid rosewood, £lO I lot 412, Drawing-room Suite, cost 80 guineas, for £2B; lot 415, Drawing-room suite, carved Chippendale for £l2; lot 425, Drawing-rem Suite, %Cant and rep, ks 105.;1,,t. 435, Drawing-room Suite, worth £lOO, for act ; lot 516. Drawirig- room Cabinet, cost £3O. for £10; 2CO Cobh:eta in Stock. Lot 715, Complete Bedroom Suite, walnut, for £5; lot 716, Com- plete Bedroom Suite, massive, east £25, for £7 ; lot 718, Com- plete Bedroom Suite. heavily carved, £10; lot 719, Complete Bedroom Suite, a marvel. for £3 10s. ;lot 720, very handsome Bedroom Suite, by Ratcliffe, beautifully carved in bas-relief, 5Ae famt vier irca .16 di, ?a:7,lb 1, nt bevel fitted with every :uxorious convenience, bril- liantra chairs,astp r, e dn and dressing ittn: ghw. et atihbreal eta I,t;twit, ens sifihsnswet asotr td h, selected wx6i toh, tt h, ! prioe 20 guineas. lAry v • H. aWbothalSeateLr.EYelosTehtoeceFnatrantlhbetaotll, Station. HOURS OF BUSIINESS EVERY DAY 9 to 7.</p>
