      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>READ WHAT THEY SAY:-</head>
        <p>READ WHAT THEY SAY:-</p>
        <p>One of those mod-rn monst:e establishments, whose re- sources ara so tioun,less, and whose stocks are so comprehen- sive, that it can, with coal care and despatch, furnish throughout a cottag-, or a mansion, is to be found in the famous house of J. GER.:HON, furnishing contractor. of 44, Oldham-street, Manche-tee. This c0...50il business was commenced in 1870, and being coyducred with remarkable ability, energy, and foiesight, anti or. sound commercial principles, it rapidly grew in public fav,ur and patronage until at. the presen time i. is the largest and most represen- tative house of its kind in Manchester. Mr. Gershon at- ' tends to every customer p..rsw.aliy. and d es not leave cus- tomers In the hands of s desmen. Operations are carried on in a magnificent block of four-storey buildings, occupying al prominent and attractive position, and h .ving a splendid frontage. They comprise shops. offices, and rapacious show-I rooms on the ground floor, and further suites of extensive showrooms on the first, second, and third fhors, together with showrooms at the rear of vast capacity, and filled to re- pletion with an immense collection of valuable Roots. Mr. Genhon is a thorough exponent of the general house furnishing, ann stocks held are unrivalled in Manchester either In magnitude, variety, or worth ; if wide experience, and fer-set log policy are absoariely requisite in proper selecting and getting together such a stupendous conglomer- ation of ml cellane us goods, It is equally true that, a great a.eount of ability of no common ord r is necessary to arrange and group them with such admirable effect, and to maintain at the samelime Bull regard for the comfort and convenience of visitors. Where «very thingthat can pessibly be considered to belong to this branch ,if business is fully represented, it is an endless task to attempt anything like an enumeration in detail, it will be 'ruffle ent to indicate the leading lines with which the house is oc-upied. ouperb ant comprehen- sive stocks are held in Drkwing, Dining and Br«akfast Room Suhes, made of all the best woods, artistically elegant in design, and magnificently and gorgeously upholste, ea in all materials of various colours, shades, and tints. Bedroom Suites made of the most dainty materials, and of the choic?st patterns. And substantial, solid-looking Library, Stu-iy, and Hall Furniture, together with inex- haustible et polies of splen.i.d couches, ottomans, and so as, duchesse stands. sid«bo Ms, book-cases, heffoniers, cabinets, Davenpor. s, easy, occasional, and other chairs dining tables, dressing. work, card 100, a d °egg:Jonal tables, whatnots, overmautels, chests of drawer., as well as a large assortment of bedsteads ot every des-ri; tion. p ttern, size and price ; bedding, matt,esses, cut «ins, fenders, fireirons and carpets, rugs, and mats of every pattk rn and design, from all the most celebrated makers Briefly. the stock embraces every article of whatever kind that can possibly be required in furnishing any or every kind oi residence, from the cottage to the mansion of the noble: and not only is each in- dividu I article to be found in these marvellous stores, hut each article is represented by a lerge codection, often unique In its kind, *Tie including a wide selection in style, quality, and prices This enterprising firm have also manufacturing premises of consider .ble extei.t, and thoroughiv equipped with pan' and machinery of the newest and best kind and a large number of skilled workmen are constantly, employed in the ma ufacture of cabinet work of all r escrlptions. Repairs, too, are executed on the premises, a little army of assistants is employed in the control of this stupendous establishment, and competent men are sent all over the kingdom to fit up private residewes. hotels, offices, public huiliiinot, end mansions, in eonjunction wi h their business. The connection of th,s representative house Is tar-reaching and most valuable, its goods have acquired the highest repu- tation in Lancashire and the neighbouring counties, and the name of "Gershon "Is an ample guarantee for everything th,t is superor to his line of business. The proprietor is a man of extensive experience and 0, great and varied know- ledge in the various branches of the business in which he is engaged. His eble and et ergetie supervirion is given to the whole of the concern, and the utmost vigilance is used that noth:ng shall emanate from his establz hment which shall tend io detract from the high and Well-merited reputation the firm has achieved. Mr. Gershon is held in great esteem in private life tor his abil.ty, public spirit, and commercial integrity. We strongly ,elvise those about firnishing to call at Mr. GIiRSHON'S Universal k'urnishing Arcade, 44, OLDHAM - SaltlitHT. MA NCH HST (Next door to the Adelphi Bank).</p>
        <p>HTTERS ARRIVE BY EVE y POST expressing thanks 14 at the;manner orders are carried out, and the following are published with the consent o the writers. READ WHAT THEY SAY. L. Samson, H.q.. the Managing Directorof the Civil Service Dental Company, Limited,. writes : " 150, Brotripton-road, South Kensington, "August 30, 1895. " Mr. J. Gershon, Manchester. " Dear sir,—l have returned your packing materials, and consider it only just to say that everything arrived in perfect condition. I am very p'eased with the goods. Should I require more, you shall have my order, and I shall be pleased to recommend you.—Yours truly, L. SAMSON. READ ON.</p>
        <p>_ Fred Bentley, Esq., writes Victoria Hotel, Manchester, September 25, 1895. "Dear sir,—l expected to be abl, to call on you to-day on passing through, but time prevented my doing so. Every- thing has arrived in a sat,factory manner, and my wife and self are pertectly sstisfied with the various suites you furnished me with, and now the work is completed the house looks exceedingly nice. I enelose cheque in settlement. Please acknowledge to Southport, and oblige yours faith- fully, FRED. BENILEY. Mr. J. Gershon, Oldham-street, Manchester. EAD ON.</p>
        <p>W.- Broadbent, lisq , writes I— " Iteynard Cottage, P, nkridge, September 27, 1895. " Dear sir,—All the goods arrived safely, and give the greatest satisfaction. If they turn out equal to the goods I had from you 14 years ago, when I lived at Blackpool, I shall have no need to complain. Cases and acking returned. —Yours truly, "W. BROADHURST. "J. Gershon, 44, Oldham-street, Manchester." MA RVLLOUS BARGAINS.</p>
        <p>IN Conaequence of Financial Difficulties and a Death in the Family, I have had placed in my hands for immediate dis- posal the contents of a handsomely furnished Villa, late the property of a solicitor, who, through the death of his wife and unfortunate speculation, has come to grief. In addition to the dining, drawing, bed rooms, and library furniture, and a quantity of miscellaneous articles. I have a number of Law Books, particulars of which will be given below. Only a br:ef description of the goods can be given. Intending purchasers are cordially invited, an upon examination will find tie goods high class and in spotless condition, they having been made to special order less than 18 months no. The hall is furnished w,th a most unborn- mon combination tail stand, which forms a seat, bench, bracket, fitted with receptacles for sticks, umbrellas, brushes, gloves, &amp;a., and the back is finished with brass pegs, and a massive bevelled-e/ge glass mirror measuring about aft. long; price £5 15s. A pair hall chairs to match, 21s. The Dlning-room is well wordy/4th° attention of those aliout furnishing. It consists of a nob lie sideboard, which is guaranteed solid walnutwood through ;it measures 6ft. long and about 9ft. high. The lower portion is fitted with every convenience, plate drawers, wine bin. and cupboards, with splendid carvsd panels, and a truly magnificent back, with a massive shield shine centre bevelled ;ease and four smaller bevelled glasses, between which are brackets, supported by massive carved pillars. Next comes the solid walnut extend- ing dining table, with two extra leaves opening out to a largv size, and resting on finely carved rated legs of rare thickness. To match are the half-dozen standard upholstered chairs, two large easy chairs and couch, all studded with brass studs, and upholster d in a rich claret-colour leather of undoubted quality. To COMplete.this remarkably handsome lot, I have the pair of real Japanese vases, standing about 2ft. high. Now for the price. Some houses would ask £9O for them. I am in a position to pack and deliver this lot for 38 guineas. The sideboard alone is worth the money: . _ .</p>
        <p>The Drawing-room really baffles description, it is so unique, , and cannot be described in the space of an advertisement. The tour small occasional chairs, two conversational chairs, pair of Eugene easy chairs, and the gondola double-ended shaped settee, are most profusely inlaid with choice and rare w,ods ; the upholstery is most uncommon, being a real broc,telle of chocolate and silver, which Is handsomely brocaded with flowers and sprays, trimmed with suitable gimp and highly finished. Next comes the magnificent inlaid cabinet, measuring about 41t. 9in. wide and nearly fift high, fitted in a most ail istlo manner with bevelled glasses, sma'l cupboards enclosed with Inlaid doors. It is simply a work of art. being complete in ,tonstruction, design, and finish. The evermantle to math is very unique, most beautifully inlaid, finished with bevelled glas, and side brackets to match the cabinet. There are two of the handsomest inlaid tables ever seen, octagonal shape, on curious shaped legs, with wine tray fitted between the legs. Now for the pries. If £72 was quoted it would not be anything like the cost. lam able to offer the complete fittings for a stylish drawing-room at the low price or 38 guineas. • The Library is fitted with 10 solid oak chairs, one large easy chair, and very fine lounge. All are Doveston's make. They are covered in leather, which is somewhat worn. When new they cost £5O. Can now be had for £lO the lot. There is a very handsome carved oak bookcase, enclosed with glass door with carved pillars running up thd sides. The lower portion is carved with curious heads. faces, &amp;c. Price, £lO. Very fine carved oak corner cabinet. 5 guineas. Superior black oak carved dinner waggon or side table, and several other oak pieces. Useful mahogany bookcase, with folding glass doors, mov.,`sla shelves, lower portion enclosed with cupboards and drawees. A great bargain; for £4 15s. A fine old Spanish mahogany dining table. No better table ever made, on bold fluted legs, five extra leaves, opening out 'to 20ft., also the lead case. A dead bargain •' for £l2. There are several Bedroom ouites. his best room is in solid American walnutvvood„ and oat £.90, price 50 guineas Ulae second room is in solid rosewood, which cost £8 for 40 guineas. The third room which is very handsome, consists of a solid American walnut suite; the w trdrobe is 61t. wide, fitted with two bevelled-glass doors, reaching f: ona plinth to cornice; between the doors are lock-up drawers and cup- boards ; pedestal dressing table, fitted with drawers in the pedestals, large bevelled glass attached • the double wish- stands, with cupboards in tue pedestals, dark marble slab,tile back; bevelled .shaving Mirror, towel airer, beside cupboard, and three chairs, upholstered in plush; a £5O suite for 25 guineas. Several smaller suites, complete with wardrobes ; 13 guineas. Solid ash suite, with wardrobe, dressing table, washstand, bedside cupboard, chairs and towel airer, for £7 10s. Several odd wardrobes, dressing tables, and wash- stands.</p>
        <p>Kitchen oddments. .Dinner set complete, 54 pieces, 1.8.5. Ed. two corner cupboards, nearly new ; wicker invalid carriage. on spider wheels and rubber tyre.; cost tour months ago, ; price 5E,3. A number of law books, among which are the follow lug William's Law of Real Property, Leading Convey- ancing and Equity Cases, " Inderm,m's ;" Leading Common Law Cases. " Indertnan's ;" Law of Companies T. Enstace Smith; the Conveyancing Acts, 1831 and 1874, with notes, forms, and precedents y E. P. Wolstenholme and R. O. Turner; Introduction to Conveyancing, H. W. Kip- hinstone ; 4 vols. Stephenb's Commentaries on the Laws of England, &amp;a.; a fine regist, red writing desk, pedestal writing desk, both nearly new ; number of bordered carpet squares, velvets, Brussels, and others. Some pleasing oil paintings and engravings, musical box, two pianos, quantity of china and glass. Several very fine brass bedsteads. Solid oak case of cutlery, never been used, a wedding present. The whole have been removed, and are Os; view at J. GERSHOWS Showrooms, 44, Oldham-street (next to Adelphi Bank). Can be warehoused till required, and delivered free. Note address ; J. (114KSHiiN5 next to Adelphi Bank, 44, Oldham-street, Manchester,</p>
        <p>:.</p>
        <p>Probabte Class • THERE are reasons for believillg Legislation. that the present government will be so largely engaged with foreigo affairs that they will attempt very little of domestic legislation. This is a way M the WO' with Toll governments and it is not likely that the garcluig" of SALISBURY will wish to depart from it. Them is, however, ono question which, ft is government will find time and opportunity ti&gt; deal with, and that is the question which DOB.° expected, the SALISBURY in his speech last week :ouphemisticalir described as the "relief of agriculture." What the Prime Minister means by that phrase Wils- made plain enough in the speech referred tc4. With him “relief of af,riculture " is synanyinono with relief of landlords at the expense of town rate- payers and taxpayers. This relief is given by th° subtle but vicious process of taking:from the. _lir' penal taxes large sums of money in aid of rates. Thismethod of relieving landlords began', it is said, so far back as 1846 when Si" Bober: PEEL, in order to conciliate the landed interes:' placed the salaries of teachers in poor-lsvr schOos) the salaries of poor-law medical officers,the salarieo of auditors in poor-law unions and the cost of tbe Imaintenance of prisoners after conviction, oil the i imperial taxes. These subsidies in aid of the lat rates have been largely increased since then auu almost entirely by the Tory party. In 18.92, amounted to no less a sum Ulan £11,846,482, gin and in 1893 they were increased 417) £13,000,000 in England alone, and, ju other words, one-fifth of the impo_aj revenue. It is argued sometimes with Vu" deal of plausibility that the system of relievitag local rates from imperial taxes is, at the worst, only taking money out of one pocket and puttinfi it in another. But when the process is analred by competent financiers it is found te result to a' large extent in the taking of money out of 00- pockets of poor people and putting it into tbe pockets of the comparatively rich landlords. It . 'for instance, calculated that out of every Ow raised by imperial taxation £59 is contributed 137 taxes on the necessaries of life or on articles that are freely used by rich and poor alike. In regarc!. to tea for instance there can be no question that by far the larger amount of the duty paid uporithi* .101 commodity paid by the poor, simply beCa they are so much more numerous than the nab The same reasoning can be applied to tObaCCO--</p>
