        <p>it has been aptly said, a docker will pro- smoke as much tobacco in the year as a id with more or less force to several other _tildes by duties on which the imperial aue is largely augmented. This being the '4lle it may readily be seen that, when imperial taxes are expend° 1 in place of local rates, the l'ekilt is that money which for the most part comes out of the pockets of poor people when they buy , - tea, tobacco or other necessaries or luxuries, 141 in place of rates which would, .for the most Part, come directly or indirectly out of the pockets landowners. Let us take a direct illustration. There are between fourteen and fifteen millions of People in this country who live in houses with tntaLs of under £lO a year. They pay annually 4,588,153 taxation on the articles mentioned, but their local taxation is estimated at only £806,908 40 that when local rates are replaced by imperial taz%tion they lose £3,781,185 by the transaction. Ihere are 22,211,919 persons living in houses of %Aar £2O a year rental. They pay on the duti- commodities Z 7,043,412, and only receive towards their rates of £1,741,192, thus loss to the extent of £5,302,220. The working of favourite Tory dodge for relieving landlord been illustrated in another and even striking fashion. At one end of the is the man who occupies a house £6 a year rent. His contribution to imperial exchequer is £1 12s. id., and he wed in his rates to the extent of Os., and . loses £1 Os. id. At the other end of scale is the wealthy parson who pays a of £6OO a year. He pays £1 12s. ld le imperial taxes referred to but receives towards his rates, so that he gains less a sum than £2B 7s. 11d. These are of the results of tho financial jugglery which Tories have practised in the past, and which : almost certain they will again attempt to Praetic- • " -</p>
