        <p>ice in the immediate future: It is to be titcl that the taxpayers will be fully alive to ve'lr own interests in this matter, and resolutely Lfuse to submit to any further imposition for the 7,ofit of an already highly privileged class of 411 downers.</p>
        <p>I, A WHAT is described as the " sym- 87a1eathetic " pathetic" lock-out of shipbuilders Lock-out. on the Clyde is naturally attract- ing a good deal of attention</p>
        <p>ing 41,40ugh0ut the country. It is now well-known the dispute which has led to these deplorable tesults originated at Belfast. The workmen in the large shipbuilding yard there voluntarily "ePted a reduction of wages when trade was 11111 cola condition that the old rate of pay should " restored when trade revivad. There has re- l.:ell, tIY been a distinct revival in shipbuilding, and Workmen having demanded their old wages '.111(1 hem refused have come out on strike. The 41hsellief did not however end there. The Itilseter shipbuilders at Belfast and those on the h‘• IYde had previously agreed to act together ° ease of a strike in either locrlity, and accordance with this agreement that the *°rltesen of the Clyde are now"locked out.”Of late 4141's public opinion has been a powerful factor in ;et iding the result of strikes and lock-outs. Strikes ,shiels have extfited a great share of public sym- , hY have generally been successful, while on the 4‘.'ller hand those which have met with public dis- proval have just as generally been defeated. here can be little doubt as to which side in the 01/resent dispute will be condemned by public .‘Phlion. Neither strikes nor lock-outs of the th%YraPathetic " kind have ever been popular with % general 'public. They nearly always appear to 11, ose who are not immediately concerned to be 17gether unnecessary extensions of the losses t" sufferings which are the inevitable ac- :l4l3animents of alllabour troubles. Lapse present Itrie the feeling will be intensified_ by one or two</p>
        <p>I 7(1</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I , 1 .... ‘444 , , the</p>
        <p>considerations. There is, for instance, the that`the shipbuilding industry is of peculiar b-7, vital importance to this country in regard to to% peace and war; and then again this lock-out 3,14 es just at a time when it will seriously check tr undoubted revival in shipbuilding of which thel.e has been ample evidence of late. The fact locat Some of the firms which have joined in the k'nut have war vessels for the British Govern- iii course of construction will not make the 1341)1. tot)ale any more tolerant in judging their , Even the Tory "St. James' Gazette" 1440 ,4a a vigorous protest against the ei-,out on the Clyde, and comments 11:24:1 the serious consequences that may from a delay in shipbuilding operations in Of warlike complications arising in the East or ). It adds that it may be necessary to the capitalists "who run the shipbuilding "that the " national work must be done</p>
        <p>ii tltslll4le4t !Ilk4where. °lavince Itlcitustry kg - I)ath, 4 `X</p>
        <p>tnust not be liable to interference out of sym- Acly." When Tori journalists write in this vein tl'uot a labour dispute it is a very good indication the Way the wind of public opinion 13 blowing. 'There was a remarkable absence of "Republican yo Plicity " from the wedding ceremony at New L. Pic on Tuesday at which Miss Conruelo Vanderbilt 781te the Duchess of Marlborough. In fact, in tite.9 respects the reports read more like an account " Pageant at the court of some semi-civilised "a potentate than of a function in the free enlightened Republic of the West. The pre-</p>
        <p>, 01()Et" . Illiplterti D‘rations kot, i ,Nete Lclrn . t'qiited</p>
        <p>J for the wedding cost, it is said, the enor- tCB 811ra of ,£BO,OOO. The interior of the Christian tkilt:eti at which the ceremony was performed `l3? glittered with the jewels worn by the invited The bride's dress is described as a" perfect</p>
        <p>in cream satin and tulle," and in at least one h`tlttd account the train is said to have been " five 14:11111 length "but probably yards was meant for 4:148." At the house of ths bride's parents the ttits:g display was on such a lavish scale that it is inaated the chrysanthemum blossoms,if piled up, eqUa: in bulk an ordinary baystabk, and the 4;4' if placed in a line Would extend over a dis- th;eB Of eight miles. In addition to these twenty ~..I.lsalad sprays of lilies of the valley were used.</p>
