        <p>Mc DOTTGALL'S PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR MAKE; THR BE-T PASTRY.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN EVENINi CONTINULTION SCROOL,</p>
        <p>The male and iPmale pupils at this school were pro- vided with an illustrated lecture on Thursday evening, describ,ng a tour round Britain in a yacht. Mr J. Browne was the lecturer, And Mr. J. Bowyer manipul- ated the lantern. The proceedings, which were greatly enjoyed by the students, closed with a pictorial representation of the poem " Curfew must not ring o-night." DISPENSING A CHARITY.</p>
        <p>The Rigby Charity was on Friday distributed to about 60 persons at Runcorn Parish Church. The recipients, who are chosen without respect to their religious beliefs, resided in Runcorn, Weston, and Halton. Tne clergy and warden + of the church distributed the gifts in Ow presence of Mr. W. E. Rigby, of Liverpool, son of the donor.</p>
        <p>ENNA</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LOCAL PRIZE WINNER.</head>
        <p>A LOCAL PRIZE WINNER.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Chester Fanciers' Association Show, held at Chester on Wednesday and Thursday, Mr. J. H. Weston, of Runcorn' was a successful exhibitor. In the open class for St. Bernard, he succeeded in gain- ing a first and special prize for his splendid animal, " Young Sir Visto." It has been exhibited at various shows, but this is the first time it has succeeded in gaining a first prize. GUY FAWKES DAY AT RUNCORN. The fifth of NoVeMbi r was celebra,ed on Tuesday with the usual bonfires ane the setting MI of fireworks. The . only incident of note occurred on the premises of Mr., Cl ugh, butcher, Regent-st. A fire was discovered about ten o'clock at night among a quantity of old papers. Immediately several persons commenced to carry the burning material into the street. Little damage was done, and the origin of the fire is attributed to the throwing of fireworks through the window. LECTURE ON ROME AT RUNCORN. At a meeting of the Brunswick Mutual Improvement Society on Wednesday evening, Mr. F. J. Norman, j.p., gave a most inter. stihg and instructive lecture on Rome. H. dealt particularly with the associations of St. Paul with the city, and traced its history from a very early date. The lecture wh,s illustrated by a number of lime- light views, and altogether a most pleasant evening was spent. Mr. T Simister was in the chair. A TOUR IN NORWAY. .</p>
        <p>In connection with Bethesda C mgregational Church, the Rev. J. S. Simpson gave an account of his visit to Norway, a trip he took from Stavanger to Trondj hem which lies in the same latitute as Ireland. Ele spoke ry eulogistically of the fjords, notably Efardanger and fivrianger, and mentionrd the waterfalls as being a special feature of this grand country. The lecture was illustrated by one hundred limp-light views, the lantern being manipulated by Mr. Henry Turton, of Frodsham. The chair was nermaiKl by Dr. MeMmeall.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN Co-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. The ernpleN 6i of the ab we society held their fourth anneal social gathering in the Co-operativc Hall, on Wednesday evening. About 120 persons sat down to an ewe llent tea, which was presided over by Mesdames Elliott. Tinton, Saunders, Clare, 1111rris,and Pendlebury, and Misses Ilnyle,,Morris,and Withington. The committee were represented by Messrs.Aston,Sayle,Dyer,and Dudley (who occupied the chair), Mr.Upton,secretary ;and LEL Chadwick, auditor of the Wholesale Society, Manchester. The Chlirman gave an address on commercial and social unity. He thought they should have a periodical confer- ence of managers and committee to remedy any little shortcomings. The committee only had the theoretical part of the business, while the employei came in touch with the practical part. Speaking of social unity, he thought they should have something in the form of a social club, which would enable them to spend many pleasant evenings together, and prevent the emplo3 éa spending their time in other less profitable ways. In the course of the evening Mr. Morris contributed a pianoforte selection, and songs were rendered by Misses Johnson and Morris, and Messrs. H. Johnson and W. E. Dudley. Mr. D. Billington gave a stump oration,and Mr. Saunders a humorous readi g, all of which were highly appre- ciated. Mr. Cann (Widnes) proposed. and Mr. Hill se- conded, a vote of thanks to the Chairman and to the committee for the use of the room, to which Mr. Dudley suitably responded. On the motion of Mr. Upton, se- conded by Mr. Ellis. a similar compliment was paid to the accompanist (Mrs. Morris), the artistes, ana to the ladies who had assisted at the tea. Games were after- wards indulged in to a late hour. Messrs. Upton and Morris acted as MO's FORTICOMING CONCERT. From a glance at our advertising columns it will be seen that the Runcorn Tonic Sulfa prize choir are to give their first concert of the season in the Public Hall, Runcorn, on Wednesday next. The special artistes en- gaged are Miss Lillie Wormald, soprano ; Mr. Tom Thomas, tenor ; and Mr. Stanley Cookson, bass. The choir since its inception has been most successful, and has won prlzes in several competitions. RUNCORN VOLUNTEER SOCIAL CLUB. The annual rePeting of the members of this club was held on Thursday night, under the auspices of Captain Ashton. Colour-Sergeant Poole, secretary, read the annual report and the balance-sheet, which showed that the club was continuing its successful career. During the year, upwards of £2OO had been spent in altering the club premises and in providing two first-class billiard tables. The report was adopted. Colonel Ashton was appointed president of the club, and Colour-Sergeant Poole was re-elected secretary and treasurer. GOSPEL MISSION CHURCH BAND OF HOPE. ' On Monday evening this society held their inaugura- tion meeting of the season. Mr.J. Robinson, assisted by Messrs. Berrineton, Mathews, and other friends, kindly gave an entertainment with his magic lantern entitled " Historic and beautiful spots in our country," with various recitations, interspersed with selections by tbe choir, &amp;c. The congregation numbered over 200, and those prsent were delighted. The illustrations were admirable, and the effect would be to confirm the con- gregation on the principle of total abstinence. SEO^EING BURNING ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A well-koown Runcorn character named Ann Corcoran of Stlkeld's Square, Runcorn, was severely burned on Saturday night. It appears that she had fallen asleep whilst sitting before a log fire. At midnight she awoke to find the log had fallen from the firegrate and set her clothing on fire. Shd ran into the square and her screams attracted the attention of roliee-ennstable Gregory, who ran to the spot, and at once took off his overcoat and wrapped it round the woman. The con- stable, who is a certificated St. John Ambulance man, and Mr. Frank Speakman, chemist, attended to the woman's injuries, the latter providing the lint, nil. etc.. necessary. A few hours later Dr. MacLennan examined the woman and stated her injuries were of a serious nature.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED TAXING OP CYCLES. At the last meeting of the Highways Committee of the Runcorn Urban District Council a circular letter from the Chester Rural District Council was read, asking the Council to adopt a memorial praying the Chester County Council to impose a tax of ss. upon cycles. The com- munication was "laid upon the table," on the motion of Dr. McKenzie.</p>
        <p>TIE TRADE OF RIINCORW. There is no fresh development to report regarding the new works the Castner—Kellner Company propose to erect. As far as can be ascertained no site has been de- cided upon. Nerotiations are in progress respecting war- leaves at Weston, but no satisfactory arrangement has it is said, been come to. The Salt Th.ion Limited are con- tinuing their investigations respecting the erection-a-4 chemical works, but their intentions are yet of a private character. With regard to the establishment of glees works at Weston which has been talked about, it is now understood that they are to be erected elsewhere. an- other unsatisfactory piece of news is that the Manches- ter Shin Canal Company have discharged between 100 and 150 men who have been employed in connection with the dredging operations, and who were paid at Runcorn. The men were working on the night shift, and this has been stopped for the next three or four months in con- sequence ofthe severity of the weather. This means a stoppage from £lOO to ,Cl5O ner week in wages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>CAN CURE ASTHMA AND HAY MITER. A NOTED PHYSICIAN OFFERS TO PROVE THIS</head>
        <p>CAN CURE ASTHMA AND HAY MITER. A NOTED PHYSICIAN OFFERS TO PROVE THIS</p>
        <p>TO ALL SUFFERERS IN WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>TO-MORROW.</p>
        <p>The majority of sufferers from Asthma and kindred complaints,after trying doctors and numberless remedies advertised as positive cures without avail, have come to the conclusion that there is no cure for this most dis- tressing disease, and these same persons will be more in doubt and sceptical when they learn through the columns of the press that Dr. Rudolph Schiffmann, the recognised authority who has treated more cases of these diseases than any living doctor, has achieved success by perfecting a remedy which not only gives immediate re- lief in the worst cases, but has positively cured thou- sands of sufferers who were considered incurable. These were just as sceptical as some of our readers now are. Dr. Schiffmann's remedy no doubt possesses the merit which is claimed for it or he would not authorise this paper to announce that he is not only willing to give free to each person suffering from Asthma. Hay-Fever, or Bronchitis in this town one free liberal trial box of his Cure, but urgently requests all sufferers to call at J. R. Young's,Chemist, 40 Sankey-et., during the next three days only, and receive a package I absolutely free of charge, knowing that in making the claim he does for his Cure, a strong doubt may arise in the minds of many, and that a personal test, as he offers to all, will be more convincing, and prove its merits. I than the publishing of thousands of Testimonials from persons who have been permanently cured by the use of his Asthma Cure. " Dr. Schiffmann's Asthma Cure," as it is called, has been sold by chemist of this town eves since it was first introduced,although many persons may never have heard of it, and it is with a view to reaching these that he makes this offer. This is certainly a most generous and fair offer, and all who are suffering from any of the above ,omplaints should remember the date and place where the distribution will be made, and avail themvlyes of the same. Persons living out of this town who desire to test the effieacy of this most wonderful remedy will receive a package free by Post by writing direct (not to the chemist) to Dr. R. Schiffmann's British Depot, 32, Snow-hill, London, E.( ~ providing their letter is received before November 13th, as no free samples can be obtained after that date. J.R. Young the wel-known chemist authorise us to announce that they have received a quantity of samples from Dr. Schiffmann, which they will distribute in accordance with his offer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EUNC'ilt., Vv 11)N E 6.</head>
        <p>EUNC'ilt., Vv 11)N E 6.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN GIRLS' FRIENDLY SOCIETY. The annual meeting in connection with this Society was held on To -sday at the Vicarage, Higher Runcorn. Several reports were submitted, and bore out the fact that good work was being accomplished. Mrs. Wood was re-elected secretary of the branch. LECTURE AT THE NATIONAL SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The Rev, H. B. Blogg, vicar, of Frodsham, gave a capital lecture on Egypt in the National Schools on Thursday evening. There was a large attendance. Owing to illness the Rev. A. Maitland Wood was unable to be present. The lecture which was copiously illustrated, was greatly apor sciated. The prooeeds were in aid of Weston chureh building .fu: d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>"RIMING WITHOUT A LICENSE.</head>
        <p>"RIMING WITHOUT A LICENSE.</p>
        <p>hancorn P lice Court on Tuesday in-Orning, before Mr. James Handley, and Mr. E. Imison, a stranfzer in the tiwn who stated that Le behoved to Manchester and named JAMeS Murray, was charged with unlawfully acting as pedlar without a certilAte.—P.C. Gregory proved the charge which was admitted by the prisoner. The magistrates discharged him on his promising to leave the town. WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Runcorn branch of the Wes- leyan Foreign Missionary Society, two sermons will be preached on Sunday next, by the Rev. Owen Watkins, superintendent of the Warrington circuit, and who was formerly a missionary in South Central Africa. At the annual meeting which is to be held on the following evening addresses will le delivered by two returned missionaries, viz , the Rev. John Grims,iaw,late mission- ary in British Guiana, and the Rev. Owen Watkins. Col- lections will be taken on behalf._ of F,reign Missions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>DEATH OF MR , DUNFIANANO-</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR , DUNFIANANO-</p>
        <p>As briefly stated in our laat issue, Mrs. Eliza Dun- bavand, wife of Mr. John Dunbavand,of Bride water-st., died on the 30th ult. at the age of 72 years. She had I resided in Runcorn all her life time, and was a member of an old Runcorn family. She had been ailing for about two years. When in health she was a regular worshipper at St. Paul's Wesleyan Chapel. She was married about 50 years age, and her husband and a family survive her. SUDDEN DEATH AT RUNCORN. Under most painful circumstances, Mr. W. Worrell, 58 years of ase.ot 31, Brook-st., Runc.orn, passed away sud- denly on Saturday efternocn. When in Devonshire Square about one o'clock, he was seized with an attack of coughing, and burst a blood vessel. He waa conveyed home, and Dr. McKenzie's services were obtained. hut despite his efforts he died in half an hour. 'l'he circum- stances are the more distresaine inasmuch ac the deceased was to have been married on Monday. He had been in the employ of the Runcorn Authority for about eight years, four of which he had been occupied as foreman scavenger. RUNCORN SANS OP TEMPERANCE. A meeging of this society was held on Saturday night in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom. The chair was occupied by Mr. Thomas J. Holt. After the ordinary business was transacted a discussiaa took place on the question " Ought professing Christians to be total ab- stainers 2" Mr. Daniel Bisbrown introd iced the subject and took the affirmative. After the ediress there was a discussion, in which the following took part :—Messrs. George Holt, Thomas Cooke,John Jones, Thomas White, and the chairman. A vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Bisbrown. Tem ELECTION OF A GUARDIAN FOR RUNCORN. As neither of the two gentlemen nominated to fill the vacancy on the Board of Guardians for Reath Ward withdrew, a contested election will take place on Satur- day. The rotes will be recorded in Greenwav-road Board School. The candidates are the Rev. John Simp- son, Congregational minister, and Mr. Thomas Reynolds, of Norman-road. Mr. Simpson is just recovering from a severe illness, and is therefore prevented from taking a very active part in the election. The interest being taken in the election cannot be described as keen, hut it is ex- pected when the result of the contest is made known that the number of votes recorded for each candidate will be vet y ch Is. DEDICATION FESTIVAL. At the Runcorn Parish Church, the dedication festive was celebrated on Friday evening, when a large con gregatiou assembled to hear a sermon by the Vicar, the Rev. Canon A. Maitland Wood, which had special refer- ence to All Saints' Day. The musical service was Stainer's Magnificat and Nunc Dinaittis, the solos being well rendered by Master Lane. The anthem " Worthy is the Lamb" (Handel), was sung. The dedication festival was continued on Sunday, on the evening of which day the choir, under the conductorship of Mr. James Littler, sang from Mozart's 12th Mass, " I will give thanks." MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN. The weekly entertainment in connection with this mis- sion was held on Tuesday evening under the presidency , of Mr. Shaw (missionary). A migcellaneons programme: of songs, duets, quartettes, and choruses was given by the members of the Mariners' Church choir. There was a good attendance. The solniste were the Misses Kite ' Foulkes, M. Bell,Mary Denning.Temple.Lizzie Breadman,l and Fenwiek, and Messrs. C. Temple, J. Williamson, and: Masters Hazlehurst and Banner. A couple of humorous sketches were given by Mr. D. Smith. At the close al vote of thanks was accorded those who had taken part, on the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. H. Cooke. ACCIDENT TO A RUNCORN PAINTER. A serious accident befel a painter named Jesse Hind, 30, King-st., Runcorn, on Saturday morning. He was proceeding to his work at Runcorn Bridge along the line when a light engine struck him, knocked him down in the four-fnnt, and passed over him. He was subse- quently picked up and conveyed to the station. Dr.Mc.- Dougall was called in, and, on his ascertaining that the man had sustained serious internal injeries, ha ordered his removal to Liverpool. He was accordingly taken by train, accompanied by railway servants, and admitted into the Northern Hospital. He had also received one or two superficial wounds on the head. He is now pro- gressing favourably. ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH. "A pleasant evening with the ladies" WAS the title of la novel hut most interesting entertainment provided in St. John's schoolroom by the ladies connected with the church and school. The programme was entirely given by the ladies, and each contributor to it went under the cognomen of some flower. Each wore the particular flower she represented with the result that a most varied display of flowers was made. The chair was taken by Narcissus, and the programme included choruses, songs, pianoforte solos, and duets, violin solo, and a recitation. reading a paperette, entitled "A chat abeut flowers." The various items were given in an excellent manner, and the entertainment was a complete success. Refresh- ments were handed round during an interval. The pro- ceeds were in aid of the building fund for the new church. THE NEW SUPERINTENDENT FOR RUNCORN. The changes to take place in the Cheshire Constabu- lary which have been notified this week, have special in- terest to the residents of Runcorn and district inasmuch as the successor to Superintendent Atherton of the Run- corn division is given. The new superintendent is to he Inspector J. O'Kell, now of Northwich. Inspector J. Dodd, of Hydeswill fill the vacancy caused by Inrpector O'Kell's promotion, and Sergeant Morris, of Hyde, will succeed Inspector Dodd. Other removals include Sergt. Thomas, of Northwich to Hyde, and Acting-Sergeant Stubbs from Brixtsn to Seacombe. Many Runcornians will be glad to hear that P.C. Hamrnetsley, formerly of Runcorn and Weston Point, has been awarded the merit badge for long and meritorious service. GUILD OF ALL SAINTS, RUNCORN. The annual tea and meeting in connection with this guild was held on Friday in the infant schoolroom, Church-street. The tea tables were presided over by Mrs. Wood, Mrs. Yates, Mrs. Tonics, Miss Smith and Miss Bate. Among those present were the Revs. Canon Wood, A. J. Jameson, W. H. Stables, H. B. Firth and H. C. Moyle. After tea the Vicar made an interesting speech on the advantage of being associated with such institutions as the one under whose auspices they were met. Connection with it enabled them to obtain assist- ance in times of sickness, in forming and carrying out good resolutions, in carrying on the work of the Church and in maintaining her continuity. The officers were afterwards appointed as follews :—The Rev. Canon Wood, warden ; the Rev. H. B. Firth and H. C. Maisie, sub-wardens ; Mr. William Coe, secretary, and Mr. Edward Rev, treasurer. FUNERAL OF TER LATE CAPTAIN BURTON, OF RUNCORN. The remains of Captain John Burton, of 3. Cavendish. street, Runcorn, who, it will be remembered, was drowned by the wreck of the steam flat Renfrew, off the Welsh coast, during the gale on the night of October Ist., were interred in their last resting place in Runcorn Cemetery, on Sunday afternoon. The body had been washed ashore at Prestatyn the previous Wednesday. The Rev. H. .1, Jameson was the officiating clergyman, and amongs those present at the funeral were the mem- bers of the deceased" family, and Messrs. Philip Burton, i :len., T. H. Burton, W. Bueton, Harry Burton, Herbert Burton, Mrs. Helsel! and Mrs. Williams (sisters of the deceased), Mr. Richard and Mr. Albert Abel, and others. A number of beautiful wreaths were placed on the coffin sent by the following :—Harry and Lizzie, Jessie and Dick, Joseph and John Hewitt, Mr. and Mrs. Williams, Mrs. Basnett, Harry and Willie (nephews). William And Alice (brother and sister), and Mr. and Mr:. Wakefield (Warrington). Efforts are being made to find the body of the engineer (Mr. Lloyd). The wreck of the vessel was a total lose to Mr. William Burton, of Warrington, the owner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, NOV,EMBER 9. 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, NOV,EMBER 9. 1895.</p>
        <p>TIIE ELECTRIC LIGHTING OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION BEFORE THE COUNCIL. At the meeting of Runcorn Council on Thursday morning, a long discussion took place regarding the recommendations of the Electric Lighting Commit- tee. They were as follows :—That Mr. leghtburn and Mr. Jones be appointed members of the com- mittee in the place of Mr. Imison aed Mr. Taylor, resigned, and that application be made to the Board of Trade fer a provisional order authorising the Council to supply electricity for any public or private purposes wiehin the Urban District of Runcorn w iteout limit as to time. Dr. Gastaldi, chairman of the committee, moved the adoption of the minutes. In doing so, he said he would put before them some facts which would elucidate the matter tea great extent. A committee consisting of Messrs. Wilson, Kirkham, Sweetman, and himself went at their own expense to visit a town which was lighted by the electric light. They had there air opportunity of seeing for themselves the beauty, and the economy to the revenue, which was produced by adopting electricity, and they came home satisfied with their expedition. With a capital of £ll,OOO they would be enabled to pro- vide two thousand lights of sixteen candle power. The cost of interest on a sinking fund of £ll,OOO, taking it at the present rate would be £750. The cost of provid- ing the electric lieht sufficient for the lighting of the public streets and also for private individuals would be £BOO per annere, making a total of £1,550. This would be the total expenditure incurred. If they used 500 illuminants for the lighting of the streets they would still have 1,500 illuminants to sell, and supposing they sold them at the very cheap rate of 10s. a lamp per yearethey would realise the sum of £?5O. Adding to that the sum of £l,OOO which was at present paid by the Council ' for lighting the streets and public buildings, they would haves revenue of £1.750. Deducting from that the , expenses of £.1,550 they wduld have a balance of £2OO , which could be devoted to the town. That was the scheme in a nutshell. The reason why they had brought the propositi.in forward so quickly was that they might obtain power from the Board of Trede this year. If the proposal was not passed at that meeting there would be a delay of a year. It would also mean another year of the bad puede.: lighting of the streets which were not lit more than 200 nights in the year, and also the loss of a year's profit.; wkich he had calculated at £2OO. The ex- perience of the past week with regard to the lighting of the streets was sufficient, for one could scarcely see whe e they were walking. If they advertised at once (before the 30th November) asking for a provisional order, by June, next year, they would be able to estab- lish three electric lights. It would be to the interests of the Council to make use of the power, and not to let it be dormant. But he must add that there were companies ready to purchase from the Council the power, if they did not make use of it themselves. He had in his possession letters from two companies which would he only too glad to purchase from the Council the order they would obtain. He knew it would be asserted by SOW" of the members that the committee were vision- aries trying to upset things which had existed in the past, striving to ruin the town, and eedeavouring to re- duce it to a state of bankruptcy. But that was not their desire. On the contrary, instead of becoming bankrupt they would be able out of the profits of the electric light, to reduce the burden on the ratepayers, by reducing the rates which ware so high, and stood like a scare-crow to those who thought of establishing works in the town. From the returns of the Board of Trade there WAS scarcely a town which had the electric light that did not pay from 4 per cent. to 10 per cent, and even 11 per cent, interest. He referred to the profits made at Blackpool and Man- chester, and in conclusion he thought by supporting the resolution they would be doing the best for the rate- payers. He sincerely trusted and hoped that everyone would dispel all thoughts of private affairs and of any other private objects, and have in view the one single object of the welfare of their own town, and if they did so, he felt sure they would be with him in agreeing that it was time they moved in the matter. Mr. Sweetmen seconded the proposition as bethought the providing of electric light would be of great benefit to the town. He felt that it would be a paying concern, and would assist in the reducing of the rates. A com- pany in Liverpool bought the power the Corporation had obtained, but now the Corporation were paying treble the amount they received from the company in order to get it back again. He did not see why they could not obtain the power in Runcorn and utilise it themeel res. Mr. Bisbrown said knowing the depressed condition of the town and the exorbitant charges of the Gas Com- pany, he had sympathy with the proposition, but at the same time he did not feel justified in supporting it,as he believed they would be taking a lean in the dark if the resolution was passed. He was not a shareholder in the Gas Company, and he had no sympathy for them in their excessive charges. They paid a dividend of 10 per cent., and be thought they might make some little sacri- fice and reduce the price of gas. But with regard to providing electric light, it was not wise to rush the thing through. He did not think the matter I had been dealt with in committee as it might have been. It had not been stated what it would cost to get the 'power ; the provisional order would coat £l5O, and that was providing there was no opposition. He thought the wealthy Gas Company would be sure to oppose it, and it would he e life and death struggle between them. (" No, no.") If they got the power, it was no use hanging it , up, they must make up their minds as to what they in- tended to do with it. The cost would have to COMP out of the rates, and they might have to par through the nose for it. lie thought it would be better if they approached the Gas Company and brought pressure to bear upon them to reduce the price of gas. Mr. Speakman moved an amendment that the cope- Mon of applying for a provisional order be deferred until the committee furnish the Council with information showing the probability of the power prepeeed to be conferred being taken a'ivantage of. He said that his experience had been that when any subject had not been well considered by the Celina, they had had time to repent at leisure. They were all delighted to see the enthusiasm Dr. Gaetalcli had put into the question, and there was no doubt he did it with the best intentions in the interests of the ratepayers. But there were two ways of looking after their interests. Suppose they went In for a provisional order, they would at once set their law clerk to work and £l5O or £2OO would he wanted. —The Law Clerk : It would be well spent.—Mr. Speak- man (continuing) said if there was opposition to the order £l,OOO might be wanted. Their rates were getting up,end they were now about Ild, in the £ more than they were 16 or 17 years ago. And at the rate they were going st.they would have them still higher. It was not the way to bring new industries into their midst. He thought they had enough on hand at the present time, and when they had the matter brought before them, in the terms of his amendment they would be better ac- quainted with it, and know how-to act. Dr. Mt-Kenzie seconded the amendment. Mr. Collier Fa;cl the question they had to deal with was not that of electric light but that of applying for a provisional order. The Council did not stand to lose a single penny. If they got the order for £2OO, and it would not exceed that sum for the (lee Company could not oppose the order, he was prepared to state that a number of gentlemen were prepared, if the Council did not use it, of taking the powers over. Many of those present seemed to think that they, the members of the Eleeric Lighting Committee, came with amateur opinions on the matter. They had come as reformers, and, for himself, he could say that he had taken the opinion of men in large municipalities on the question of electric lighting, and it was their opinion that it was the duty of the Council to get a provisional order. The committee did net want to make a bungle of the matter as was the case when the water works were purchased, for that was , conducted in a most unbusinesslike way. They did not ' want the Gas Works to get it established, and then the Council to buy it off them. The old members of the Council seemed to be afraid ef the eoung members meet- ing them in for some now thing, but if the old Commis- sioners were admired for their prescience and ability why were they not all elected? Instead of it being a leap in the dark, as Mr. Grice had said, it was a leap in the light. (Laughter and hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor was somewhat surprised that the gentlemen who were advocating for thie provisional order should do so with such little information. They had only given opinions. Dr. Gastaldi had told them that the plant could he put elowu for £ll,OOO. With ail due respect to him he did not think he had had very great experience in anything of that kind. As business men he hoped they would require to know more about it before they went in for spending two or three hundred pounds on a provisional order. Dr. Gasteldi NO reckoned on the public taking the light hut they had only his word for it. They were simply asked to CIONP their eyes and accept no state- ments at all. What new works would be established in the town when manufacturers knew there were such people managing the affairs of the town? Mr. Jones May I ask the Law Clerk whether the Gas Company have power to oppose the order? The Law Clerk said he was instructed that no opposi- tion by them weld he listened to by the Board of Trade as they had no locus standi to oppose it. Mr. Wiswall said he was as anxious as any mem- ber of the Council to get a better artificial light, but to accomplish it they mast go about the matter in a reasonable and sensible manner. It was a leap in the dark, and as a member of a society which went into these subjects and who knew a little mere about the matter than perhaps they did, he would warn them that if they did get the provisional order they would gravely repent it. The scheme would suit the Law Clerk no doubt, but not the ratepayers. He was surprieed at these skin-deep ecenomists bringing the matter forward in PI linh a manner. Mr. Wilson said that at the last meeting a gentleman spoke of the matter ending in a farce, and he thought some of the members of the Council were endeavotaring to make the work of the committee a farce. Be joined ' the cemmittee with an open mind, and he found that the old members of the Council who had voted on the committee had resioned. The committee was cense- quently composed of young members, and it was dig-</p>
        <p>t nctly stated that they should not spend a lof the ratepayers' money. What information could they obtain when handicapped in that manlier? 1 hey ar- ranged for an engineer to meet them,aad he did so. He went into the question of the cost of the installation,the income of the outgo, and he (Mr. Wilson) took it down from the lips of the engineer. It was not Dr. Gastaldi's estimate at all, but that of the engineer who stated that the cost ef the installation would be £ll,OOO. The Coun- cil now paid £9OO per annum for street lighting, and in addition they paid men for going round, and others for doing other work, brirwing toe total eogt to £l,OOO. 1r they got the order and they decided to go into the mat- ter they would be all that to the good, or they could sell the orier. He believed in progress,and he could not see any liklihood of the venture being a loss to the ratepayere The interest of the ratepayers should he paramount over self interest when considering such a matter. He declared it was a business thing and it the Council wanted further information they would have to relax the resolution which they had passed. If it had been in connection with some big thing with an excurson to London, there would not have been a committee appointed. (Laughter.) Mr. resnett said a good many of the members were severe on the committee, stating that they had given no information, and yet they had given the committee no help or money. Mr. Kirkham in supporting the proposition quoted statistics showing the profits which had been made in a number of places where electric light itad been adopted. He must say that he never saw a business man go into a business without spending money. The remarks which had been made about the members of the committee that morning .were certainly ungentlemanly. . . _</p>
        <p>Mr. f7ritchard did n'ot think the members of the corn- mittee had made out their cases. He woald like to have known the number of years it would take before they arrived at anything like the revenue which had been statod. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Dean said the committee had had very short time to get information. As a member of the committee he would be in favour of again considering the matter pro- vided they were able to spend some miney in getting more information.</p>
        <p>On being put to the vote the amendnient was carried by eight votes to seven. It was decided that the com- mittee should consist of the whole of the members of the Board.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN bISTRICT COUNCIL. A meeting of this Council was held on Thursday morn- ing in the Council Chamber. The chair was occupied by Mr E. Imison, J.P. and there were also present : Rev. Dr. Gastaldi.Dr. IVleKenzie, Messrs. P. Speakman, J.P.' C.C., S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., H. J. Griee. J Prit- chard, W. Collier, J. Jones, H. J. Kirkham, J. Wilson, R. H. Posnett, F. Wiswall, D. Bishrown, M. SweetinAn, T. Dean, and Mr. Lake (law clerk). Mr. E. Marshall (secretary), Dr. McDougall (medical officer), and Mr. M. Barker (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE TOWN.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall, medical officer,reported that the num- ber of deaths registered during the past month amounted to 29. Two were inquest cases and of the remaining 27, two were due to cancer, two to debility, two perito- nitis, two to heart disease, four to convulsions, three to diarrhcea, four to tubercular disease, and one each to premature birth, pneumonia, paralysis, natural decay, tee hing, gangrine, and one to bronchitis. The 29 deaths showed an average death rate for the twelve months of 18.3, and exclusive of the inquest cases of a fraction over 17 per 1,000 of the estimated population. The total births for the month were 39.—The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>_ HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS COMMITTEE. It was recommended by this committee that iron rail- ings be fixed for 12 yards on each side of Victoria-road at the west end of the Viaduct ; that a strip of land in li.wland•road be purchased from the trustees of the late Duke of Bridgewater for the sum of £7 104. ; that the offer of Mr. Howland to contribute £lO on condition that the Council flag the footway on the south side of Mough- land-lane co-extensive with premises owned by him be accepted ; and that the plan of Mr. Arthur Caygill for the erection of seven houses in Queen's-road be ap- proved sutject to the obstruction in Queen's-road being removed before building.—Mr. Taylor moved the adop- tion of the minutes.—Mr. Walter Collier objected to the acceptance of the offer made by Mr.Rowland,and moved an amendment that the recommendations of the committee he accepted with that minute excluded. He did not see why the Council should be put to the expense of £3O for flagging the footwa•-.—Mr. Sweetman se• conded the amendment. —On being put to the vote, the amendment was Inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ENALTH COACHITTEE,</head>
        <p>ENALTH COACHITTEE,</p>
        <p>The Sanitary Inspector reported to the committee that there was insufficient or improper privy accommo- dation at 1,3, 5, and 7 High-st., and the committee re- commended that it be remedied within 28 days; also tlfat the account of £l3 10s. for the two Well's lights procured by the Sanitary Inspector be paid. That the tender of Mr. J. Sefton for the re-construction of privies at Mill Stone-yard be accepted ; that the chairman be authorised to have the defects in the ventilators at the hospital made right, some pointing of walls done and a coal place constructed—Mr. Wiswall moved the adop- tion of the minutes.—Mr. Collier wished to know whether the money for the Well's lightshad been already spent.—Mr. Wiswall explained that they had been bought at a sa,e.s.t half their original cost. The minutes were then passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WATER COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The committee reported that three analyses of water had been received from the Runcorn Rural District Council of samples taken at Ffsltnn and the committee found that they did not materially vary from theanalyses obtained at the time of the purchase of the waterworks undertaking, th, eater being pronounced a first class water as regards oreanic matter but contained chlorides of calcium and magnesium.—Mr. Pritchard stated that there had not been a single complaint in Runcorn respecting the water. There had been complaints from Weston and Balton, and the authority had been put to some considerable expense in overhauling their mains and plugs, but he thought the most fastidious person in future would hive no rea-ion to complain.—Mr. Wilson seconded, and the report was accepted without further comment.</p>
        <p>. ........ - MR. WILDING S SERVICES TO THE TECHNICAL INSTITITTE.</p>
        <p>The Technical Inatruction Committee recommended that Mr. James Wilding be paid the sum of £2O for extra services in respect of the preparation of the specification and the carrying out of the contracts for the furnishing of the Technical institute.—ln moving the adoption of the minutes Mr. WiSwall stated that for the equipment of the Institute it wig necessary to have certain specifica- tions and quantities, and if the architect had been al- lowed to prepare them it would have been an expendi- tnre of from £5O to £6O. The Committee thought that Mr. Wilding was able to do the work, but when they appointed him to undertake it, it was understood that there should he some acknowledgment for his services as the work would have to bed ne in his own time. Mr. Wilding wont to a great deal of trouble, and visited several places in order to gain information. The work had been done exceedingly well, and they had been complimented on the admirable manner the Institute had been furnished and equipped.—Mr. Dean seconded the proposition, and said that by engaging Mr. Wild- leg the Committee had caved about £4O —Mr. Jones moved an amendment that for the wards " That Mr. James Wilding he paid the alum of £20," the following should be substituted " That this Council tender its sincere thanks to Mr. Wilding." In doing 84 he said there was a great feeling of opposition against this grant- ing from time to time of slims of money for extra ser- vices. Mr. Wilding, he supposed,was now a permanent official of the Council at a certain salary for which he was supposed to devote his whole time. He 'could not see the reasonableness of paying for this extra work to a permanent official. He did not know whether he had applied for extra payment, he should be glad to know that ha had not. He thought they should deal with the ratepayers' money as they should pay their own. Ile erongly opposed the payment of £2O —Dr. Gantaldi seconded the amendment.—Mr.w ilson said Mr. Wilding had spent most of the time in preparing the specifica- tins in his own time. He had been up till two o'clock in the morning doing this work, and he did not think I t weg fair for him to get nothing for it. It reflected ' 'upon the Council that the work had to be done at night. fle was of the opinion that it ought to have been done la the day. Under the circums`ancea he thought they were compelled to acknowledge Mr. Wilding's services. —Mr. Grice thought it would be a most unfortunate thing if they refused to reward Mr. Wilding for the work.—Mr. Speakman said the interests of the town were best served by true economy,but it happened that the money it was intended to .give for extra services would not affect the rates at all. He thought they ought to congratulate themselves that they had an official in their midst who could tackle the work of drawing up such a specification.—Mr. Bishrown said he spoke against the proposal in committee. He did not think it was wise to indulga its those extra grants from time to time. As they had two permanent officials it was thought that they could do the work. He never remembered any agreement being made that they were to pay extra for the work.— Mr. Sweetman and Mr. Posnett also spoke. The latter gentleman was of the opinion that when they engaged an official they ought to pay for his entire services. It should he distinctly understood what his duties were. They were now paying more for their surveying than they had ever paid, and he thought they should have some definite idea as to what they were paying, and as tea the definite work each officer was doing.—Mr. Wiswall having replied, the amendment was put to the vote, and lost, only five voting for it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>PINANCJI.</head>
        <p>PINANCJI.</p>
        <p>Tha Finance Committee recommended that the offer of Mr. W. T. Hardie, Northwich, to lend the SUM of £7OO for seven years at 3i per cent, per annum interest be ac- cepted. (Private Street Works) ; that the tender of Mr. Joseph Moseley and others to lend the sum of £1.200 for seven years at 3i per cent, per annum interest be 11C, cepted. (£B5O for Technical Institute, £350 for Private Street Works) ; that, the sum of £3 be paid to Messrs. Ashworth, Mosley and Co., Manchester, for commission in respect of the aforementioned loan of £1,200, and that an embossing press be obtained from Messrs. John Esson Liverpool, for the sum of £4 17s. 6d.—The minutes were passed. A report of the proceedings regarding the question of the electric lighting of Runcorn win be found in adother column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held in the Board room in Tuesday evening, when the chair was occupied by Mr. J. Pritchard. There were also present: Rev. Dr. Ga,taldi, Dr. McDougall, Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., Mr. E. Imison, J.P.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>LATE Ma. HANDLEY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE Ma. HANDLEY.</p>
        <p>A letter was read by the Clerk from Mr. T. Brookes Handley, of Fern Cliff., Higher Runcorn, conveying the sincere thanks of the family for the Board's kind ex- pression of sympathy in their bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>.. The minutes of this Gann-uttee showed that 72 per- sons had been summoned. 62 appeared, and proceedings were ordered to be taken against 17 parents.—These minutes were paqsed on the motion of the Rev. Dr. Gas- taLip,econdeci by Mr. Tay'or.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SCHOOL MANAGIKMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL MANAGIKMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>I This Committee's !Mout. s rec-ommended that the re- signation of Mr. W. Kerman, as assistant teacher at the evening school be accepted, and that Mr. Joseph Brown, of Brunswick-street schr4ol succeed him at the same rate of payment. The Clerk reported that on October 21st, Der Majesty's Inspector of schools had visited Green- way-road school the first of several visits to be made during the year in lieu of the annual inspection in July. In the log book of the girls' school the inspector made the following entry " Visited sre,oll under Art 1884, (b) 930 a.m., numbor on book i 268, present 244. Accom- modation for 218 girls cfly at desks. Average all last quarter 229. The planning ot the schools is quite out of date and it is hoped that the new school will be better arranged. Every class-room should empty into the cor- ridor, so that it may be emptied without disturbing the children in the main room. The main room and end class nom have no side lights." In the log book of the boys school the following entry had been made "The offices are insufficient and the cloak rooms are crowded with lumber."—The minutes were confirmed in the motion of Dr. McDougall, seconded by Mr Imison.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED EXTENSF,N OF SCEIOOL ACCOMMODATION.</p>
        <p>The Chairman speaking with reference to the plans for the extecsion at Greenway•road Schools, said there was no definite recommen i tion to be made to the Board. The committee had had the architect down, and it, was de- cided to have some alterations made to the plans laid before the committee. At present, they were somewhat incomplete, and he did not know what advantage there would be in discussing the subject at that meeting. There was no resolution on the agenda with regard to them.—Dr. McDougall stated that the resolution for bringing the matter forward at that meeting was moved by the Rev. Canon Wood, seconded by the Rev. Dr. Gestaltli.—Dr. Gastaldi said new plans had now come from the architect, and as there were alterations made which needed to be examined, he thought it would be better for them to be considered by the committee. e moved that the plans as a whole be submitted to the committee for further consideration.—Mr. Taylor was of the opinion that that was the best course to adopt. He understood that the plans had been altered, and it would be better for the crimm.ttee to consider them nein. He seconded the proposition.—The Chairman stated that the original pl ins had been modi• fled. They had had a sketch sent them by the architect hut it had tint been modified.—Mr. Taylor : I think the Board had better meet in committee to go through the amended plan,—Dr. McDougall said perhaps the gentle- men who moved the resolution at the last meeting in- tended that the question of plans as they were, or as they were altered, might he submitted at that meeting , so that the ratepayers could be made aware of what was proposed. It would be within the recollection of the Board that there was a divided opinion with regard to the relative merits of the plans, and he on that occasion was in the happy position of being in a minority of one as to the course that should be taken. It was import,- ant to the public.—The Chairman : Excuse me, there is really no motion before the meetine.—Dr. McDougall stated that .there was a proposit ion that the plans should be referred again to the committee. Rethought he was entitled to speak on thegeneral merits of the resolution. He believed he was perfectly in order, and he should be sorry to have to move an amendment that the plans be considered now.He wishe Ito say that at the last meeting of the committee when the architect was present, the members were practically unanimously in favour of the suggestion that the caretakers' house should be aban- doned, and that that portion of the building should be added to the school accommodation. That was the only alteration made in the plans, and bethought it would prove beneficial. He had always objected to the caretaker's house being inclucted,however advantageous and necessary it might be, in the school buildings. If it was thought better that that qiiestion should be dis- cussed by the Board resolving itself into committee so that every member's views might be fairly brought be- fore the public, he had not the slightest objection to sucn a course. It was important that where important , changes were about to take place, and where there was a consensus of opinion As to certain alterations being beneficial to the schools, and in the interests of the pub- lic,that it would be wise to discuss the subject with the greatest freedom, and that could be done bettor in corn- ! mittee. He hoped that the plans would be brought before the Board and decided uyon at the earliest opportunity, , for it was of the utmost importance that no unnecessary ' delay should be allowed to interfere with some form of plans being adopted, and the speedy execution of tke work for several reasons theylwanted additional labour in the town, and the sooner the additional accommoda- tion was provided the bette:.--The motion was then adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>VACANT SEAT ON THE BOARD.</head>
        <p>THE VACANT SEAT ON THE BOARD.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall said he wished to give notiz..e that at a special meeting of the Board he would prop se the elec- tion of Mr. F. J. Norman as a member of the Board. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FRODs HAM.</head>
        <p>FRODs HAM.</p>
        <p>- - - ----- WESLEYAN MEN'S BIBLE CLass.—The open meeting in connection with this class was held on Sunday afternoon, when Mr. C. A. Dawson presided. The address was de- livered by the Rev. Robert Renton. The programme con- sisted of sacred songs and solos. There was a very large number of friends present. POLICE NEW9.—At Fr4dsham Police Court on Tuesday evening, before Mr. A. Thomas, two St. Helens men named Daniel Gattridge and Joseph Gill were charged with begging at Netherton, the previous night. P.O. Breeze proved the case, and the prisoners were com- mitted tnprison for fourteen days. FREEMASONRY.—The festival of St. John in connection with the Cholmondeley Lodge, Frodsham, was celebrated on ruesday evening. Bro. T. D. Timmins, the worship- ful master, installed the following brethern in the re- spective offices i—I.P.M., Bro. S. Davies ; S.W., Bro.' F. A. Smith; J. W., Bro. J. J. W. Potter; treasurer,Bro. J. B. Holland ; secretary, Bro. H. Tiles, ; D.C., Bro. Harrison ; S.D., Dr. Julius Raschen ; J.D., Bro. J. G. IKydd ; organist, Bro. J. Walwyn White ; char. rep., Bro. H. E. Heywood ; almoner, Bro. J. K. Digges ; 1.G., Bro. Dr. T. J. Selby; S.S., Bro. Dr. MacLennan; J.S., W.B. Frith; tyler, Bro. John Roberts. Visitors were present from Chester, Liverpool, Runcorn, and Widnes. A ban- quet was afterwards held when patriotic and masonic tosts were duly honoured. FRODSRAM BRIDGE P.S.A.—The quarterly open meeting in connection with the Union Church P.S.A. Society at Frodsham Bridge, VMS held on Sunday, and was largely attended. The success which has attended this move- ment at Frodsham Bridge has been most encouraging to the promoters, and it was announced on Sunday that the membership was 154. Mr. William Hickman, of Warrington, delivered an address on "The three gospels preached at the P.S. A.' " stating them to be the gospel of Christ, of work, and of giving. The Rev. A. H. Sayers, the pastor of the church, presided. The musical part was a special feature of the service, and included a solo entitled " My bud in Heaven," by Mrs. Temperley, of Runcorn and also a duet "Fierce raged the tempest," by Mr. Robinson and Mrs. Temperley. SMART CAPTURE BY THE POLICE.—At Frodsham Police Court on Monday afternoon before Dr. Steele, Samuel Burkhill, was charged with breaking and entering the lock-up shop of Charles Hulse and stealing a quantity of goods of the value of 2s. 55., at Frodsham Lordship at 1 a.m. on 3rd Nov.—Charles Hulse, of Delph Cottage, Frodsham, stated that his lock-up shop at Stone houses was about 300 yards from his dwelling-house. On Saturday night when locging up the shop he placed a piece of paper on the top edge of the door, as he sus- pected someone went into his shop duriag the night. From what he was told at 2.30 a.m. on Sunday, he went to the shop in company with P.C. Weatherley, and saw that some of the things in the shop had been disturbed, and the piece of paper he had placed on the top of the door was lying en the counter. He identified the articles Produced as his property, and valued them at 2s. sd. P.C. Weatherley, stationed at Five Crosses, Frodsham Lordship, deposed that he was on duty in Kingsley-road at 1 a.m. on Sunday, opposite the complainant's shop, when he noticed a light inside. He went and tried the door, and noticed a key in the lock on the outside of the door. He went inside the shop which was then in darkness. On searching round he found the prisoner who was without boots concealed behind the door. Wit- ness asl,ed hins what he was doing there, and he re- plied " You have copped me." On searching him, wit- ness found the articles produced. Prisoner made no re- ply when charged.—The magistrates committed him for trial to Chester Quarter Sessions, at.i admitted him to bail, himself in £2O, and two sureties in £lO.</p>
        <p>LYTAM. WESLEYAN MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.-011 Saturday, by the kind invitation of Professor Boyd Daw- kins, F.R.S., the members of the above society paid a visit to) Owens College, Manchester, where the Professor gave then an interest rig and practical lecture on the treasures of the College museum. A Met. explaining the arrangements of the museum, he pointed out in a most masterly manner the best methods of approaching the , study of geology, botany, mineralogy, or zoology. fie then went on to show how each geological era was repris- sented by its plant Rad animal life. The Professor called special attention to the recent splendid addition to the museum of Egyptian antiquities collected by Mr.Flinders Petrie. A cordial vote of thanks having been accorded to the Professor, on the motion of the President (11r. J Dodds), seconded by Mr. J. Tonge, CE., and supported by Mr. T. Morris, F OS., the members were entertained to tea by the president, and a very pleasant day was pent.</p>
