      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ODDFELLOWSHIP IN RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWSHIP IN RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>QUARTERLY MEETING.</p>
        <p>At the Castle Hotel, Hilton, on Monday evening, the quarterly meeting of the Halton and Runcorn district was held. The chair Whg occupied by Bro. W. Lea, D.M., and there were als ) present toe trustees, P.D.M's Thomas Brian, Robert ilu4hes, and G. Hazlehurst ; P.D.M. T. D, vi-s : treasurer. W. Massey. D.S., P.G.T, L. Ashcroft, auditor; P.D.M's J. W. Bosworth, J. Douglas, George Rutter, and W. Moston ; P. G's W. Wilkinson, W. M9ors, R. M rrreli, and W. Lambert, D.D.M. The Setretary's report fur the eparter showed a loss of four members by death, arid six owing to other causes ; 13 members had been initiated, leaving a net member- bership of 962, or a gain of ' ()rec. The cash received '! during the q rarer was £167 123 9il. and expenditure I £136 03. 41d. The funeral find 11.rw stood at £1,013 18s. and management fund at Ils. lit , or a total worth of £1,014 93. Id.—Brother Ashcr.ott reported that he had examined the vouchers, return 3, and books, and found them correct as pre.ented.—Trie accounts were adopted. A discussion took place un the question of allowing members to subscribe for extra be. efits, or double those at present paid. I-. was proposed by Bro. Moors, se- conded by Brn. Davies that the subject be adjourned until the next meeting. Bro. Missey propesed as an amendment" If any member of,this district, between 16 and 40 years of age or any proposed mem tier desire to receive extra benefits for sickness and death, he and his wife shall before applyins to contribute for such extra benefits be examined by the medical officer of his Lodge, and if his health and that ot his wile be satisfactory, and the Lodge be agreeable to his subscribing for the extra ben-fits as per scales in L.rdge and district rules according to his age at the time of his first contributing on account of them, but he shall not be charged twice for management expenses aud shall be considered as only one member." Ic was pointed out by the secretaly that if this were adopted it would at once enable the memt era to insure themselvin es the lodges for double their present benefits, at a saving of about threepence per fortnight on what they would have to pay if they petted two separate lodges in this or other societies. It they could be such means as proposed induce the members to stick to their own society in addition to increasing the efficiency of their society, they would to a great extent be the means of the members providing themselves with a sufficient amount of sick-pay to enable them to keep clear of Ihe Poor Lure in their old age. The time might soon come when they would be able to provide a graduated scale of payment for sickness,but until that time arrived, it would be wise to adopt the amendment, the working of which would be simple.—Bro. Douglas seconded the amendment. Many of their members had been compelled to join tontines and other societies in order to secure sufficient benefits to keep them in decency during sickness.—Bro Moors thought it was a serious question and there should be further consideration before the amendment was adopted.—Bro. Davies said the question of the management expenses especially .hould be further considered.—Bra. Hughes hoped the amendment would be adopted. Be had known cases where men in their old age or continued sickness had not been allowed to continue their memberships in tnutine societies, and in some cases the secieties han broken up,and as a result the men had only their lodge to fall upon at the time they most needed assistance.—The Secretary answered several questions, and on being put to the vote the amendment was carried. Bros. W. Moors, and W. Moston were appointed audi- tors for the year. Bros. Thomas Davies and J. W. Bos- worth were appointed to represent the district at Cardiff A.M.C., and Bros. G. Rutter and W. Moors as sub- stitutes.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN FOO NALL UNION. The first Comnaittee meeting this Union waS held , at the George Hotel, Eluddersfipld, on Thursday even- ing. This being the first formal meeting of the Commit- , tee the election of office bearers was proceeded with, the following being elected :—President, Mr. H. H. Weller; vice president, Mr. J. E. Warren: !ism rary secretary, Mr. J. Platt; and honorary treasurer, Mr. D. F. Burn- ley. The transfer of C Wildiog from Bradford to Wigan was confirmed. Permission was given to the hon. secrc- tary to grant provisional transfers, if in order, between meetings of the Committee. J. H. Sharp (Halifax) and A. Ray (Leeds) were reported by Mr. G. Wilson (Man- ningham), referee, for rough play in the Leeds v. Hali- fax A team match. Sharp was suspended up to and including November 9, and Ray was suspended until he appears before the Emergency Committee. W. I. Favr. cett, of Manningham, the well-kuown three-quarter player, of Yorkshire County fame, appeared before the Committee along with Mr. J. Freeman, president of the Manningham club, and asked to be &amp;Hewed to play under the Northern Rugby Union. After a full state- ment of his case the Committee granted him permission to play under the auspices of the Northern Rugby Union. It may be remembered that Fawcett was declared a pro- fessional because of his having rec Jived a money gift on his leaving Manningham for Manitoba, and his reinstate- ment hal been refused by the English Rugby Union. WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLAM—At a meet- ing of this class on Monday evening,the Rev.T.Bracken- bury gave a second homely talk on his holiday in the South of Ireland, it being. a continuation of one pre- viously given in Runcorn. The "talk" was greatly ap- preciated by the Isrge audience prePent. Mr. D. Somer- ville occupied the chair. Votes of thanks were passed at the close.</p>
        <p>BURGLARY' AT RUNCORN. At Chester Ass;ze•+, on Sat urday, before Mr. Justice Lawrence, George Garvey, Waterman, pleaded guilty 0 breaking and entering the house of Richard Bagley, and stealing therefrom one elc-ic and other articles, the pro- perty of Richard Ragles and others at Runcorn, oat the 27th July, 1895. He was further charged with breaking and entering the dwelling-house of Thomas William Dar- well,and stealing therefrom a pair of boots,his property, at Runcorn,oa the 26. h July.—Mr. E. H. Lloyd,who de- fended,said the prisoner had given way to drink. Up to that time be bad always ben] a steady mm—Prisoner was sentenced to three months' imnriaon.nent.</p>
