      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>BEFO T 11E M A.GISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFO T 11E M A.GISTRATES.</p>
        <p>• A MUSIC AND DANCING LICIINDI REFUSED. On Monday morning at the Warringt•n Borough Police , Court, George Shakeshaft,applied for a music and danc- ing license for a room in Bewsey-st., Foundry. The ap- plicant informed the bench that the room would be used for a select quadrille class. Several improvements had been made in the room ; the oil lamps had been taken away and gas substituted, the ventilating system had been impro.ed, and the panic exit had been put right. Answering questions by the Head Constable,Mr. Shakes- shaft said that he had not drawn up a set of rules, but if the license was granted they would be similar to the rules in existence when he had a class in the Co-opera- tive Hall. The reason be had left the Co-operative Hall was that the room was too large and did loot pay. He admitted that the room in Bewsey-st., was larger than the one in the Co-operative Hall,but the terms would be better. When he held the licen-e for the room in the Co-operative Hall he had a membership of more than 40 He expected to have a membership for the Bewsey-s'. room of between 30 arid 40. There were so many classes in the town that he did not expect to get more. The room which was on the second floor, was let to him by Mr. Smethurst and the alterations had been made to the Satisfaction of the Borough Survevor.—lnspector Spinks sold that the room was 601 t by 31ft. and lift. high. There were 20 windows and 17 ventilators. A barricade had been put outside the steps leading from the room to the ground floor, but the steps themselves were not wide enough for two to pass abreast. Outside the building there was a large lumber yard which was not lighted at all The report of the Borough Surveyor was then handed to the Bench, Inspector Snicks remarking that the Sur- veyor viewed thf: building in company with himself.— Councillor Pierpoint said the report was totally uncalled for and ought never to have been presented to them.— Mr. Hunt said that the Borough Surveyor, when he exa- mined the morn, was not aware exactly what he was doing.—Councillor Pierpoint said that the Borough Sur- veyor had no cause to examine the building and ought never to have done so. A private architect should have been consulted.—After very little consultation the magis- trates ref used the license. No reasons were given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>MIN olt CASES.</head>
        <p>MIN olt CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman). Dr. White. A VIOLEET CIISTe,MER rs THE OLD CROW. —John Keenan. Dial-street, was charged with assaulting and bating Thomas and Fanny Mather on the 26th °etc,' er ;he was further charged with committing wilful damage to a glass door.— Mr. Browne represented plaintiffs and Mr. Davies the defend- ant.—Mr. Browne said Mr. and Mrs. Mather occupied the " Old Crow" and some weeks ago a case arose in which Keenan was prosecuted by his wife for assaulting her and it was then said that Mathers harboured the defendant in the " Old Crow," and they had since then refused to serve him ! with any beer. On the night in question re. Mather was nutting out the gas when she heard the defen 'ant asking for beer and ordered that he w,is not to be served and he then called her some filthy names Mr. Math.r came up and de- fendant got hold of him by the throat and struck hon. He seized a jug of beer and struck at Mrs. Mather with the jug. Instead of hitting Mrs. Mather the jug wen through the bar window and broke two pence of glass. When the police came the man I.olted.—Mrs. Mather have corroborative evidence and her statement WM supported by Mr. Mather who added that he hlmsell brought the police, and did net refuse to give him his change, as suggested by Mr. Davies. Mrs. Waltnsley said Mrs. Mather when ti.e defendant called her names. took the jugof beer and emptied it. Defendant put h fist in her face.—P.o Rviatt stated that Mr. Mather came o him and complained tif the damage. Witness went end found the windows broken and Mrs. Mather was crying. No complaint was made of the assault.—Mr. Davies said Mr. Browne hut evidently been misinstructed and be submitted that hl</p>
        <p>case had fallen to the gronnd as regards the assault. The officers were called to the scene almoot Immediately after the supposed once and Mot one single complaint was made about the assault. Defendant was a man of good character but unfortunately he had been harboured in the h use and the'Mathers had allowed him to sum up an account of nearly n for drink. and Mrs. Math r hal taken his change as part payment for It.—Sarah dmith stated that she saw defendant take bold of Mr. Mather.—The magistrates lined defendant Ss. and costs (Ms.) but, as the amount was so large modified it to El including costs. Several minor cases were adjourned. os SATlTitnay. Before hours. J. Burgess (In the chair) and •</p>
        <p>Wright. Mm Ccosectritcs INTERFENICD TOO LATE.--.7ohn Perry we charged with stealing from the shop of Henry Burrows, butcher, Butterrearket-st., ltlb. of mutton, value 100.—John Hyaes said he saw the prisoner ow Thu sday afternoon stand- ing with his back against the window of Mr. Burrows' shop. He saw him take a piece of mutton from the window, and then wall( towards the shon of sir hue. He then saw pri- soner return t the shop, enter it, end put the meat upon the scales and weigh it.—An employe of Mr. Burrows identified the meat produced as his master's property.—Detective- Sergeant Monaghan deposed to apprehending prisoner in con- sequen eof • comolaint from MT. Burr ms.—ln pleading to the Bench prisoner sail he was in drink at the time, or he would not have been guilty of such ac offence. After he had taken the meat his conscience told him he was doing wrong. He then walked back to the shop pot the meat upon the scales, and as he had not money enough to purchase it he left the shom lie was sorry for what he 'Ad done, and hoped the magistrates would dal leniently with him.—Prisoner was committed to gaol for fourte it days with hard labour. NON-PAYMENT OF ARREARS. William Massey was sum- moned to show cause why he should net be committed to prison for non-payment of arrears due to his wife. Hannah. under an order for maintenance —Mr. Crowns, who repre- sented the wife, aild that the order was made on the 13th September. and though he had paid several amounts, defend- ant had not set paid the court cos s, which together with the arrears of maintenance amounted to P-3 8s Id. The defend- ant hard bee in good work at Chorlev, but tie suddenly left that town without notifying him (Mr, Browne). Some time afterwards it trmspired that he was working at Wigan.— Defendant remarked that he would receive his wages that day', ago would pay an instalment out of the money.—He asked for a month in which to pay the arrears.--lhe Bench decided to give bin a fortnight, at the end of which time, if he had not paid the arrears, he would have to appear in court again, so that the Case might be disposed of. moimiy,—llefore the Mayor (Councillor James Valiclough), Alderman Harrison, Councillor Pierpoint, Messrs, Fleming and Hayward. TRANSVER OF LICENcES —The magistrates granted the transfer of the following licences : —Lower Angel Vaults, Butterrnarket-street, from the late Juglar Woodhouse. te John Howard ; 2, Watkin-street, off-ti mice, from Richard Cunningham to Lawrence Whit field ; off-licence in St. James's buildings. Winwick-road, from Ch tries Augustus Les to Henry Taylor; 159, NV inwick-road (beerhouse).from Frederick Jacob Roberts to Bridget Cunningham. wrepow Gtiffin, 10, Stanley-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly f.n Friar's greet' : also with having broken two panes of glass on the me *lion v lue is., the property of Thomas Lee.— same said 'hat he did not remember anything about the window,—Police Sergeant Smith testified to ending the</p>
        <p>• 1 1</p>
        <p>prisoner drunk and creating a disturbance in Friars-green at half-past nine on Saturday night. He received a complaint that the prisoner had broken two panes of glass and afterwards took him into custody,—Thomas Lee, shopkeeper, deposed to the prisoner breaking the glass by kicking the window frame When asked to explain his cundwit Griffin told witness that he Was looking for a man and woman whont he thought ta be , in the house at which he was kicking.—A fine of 10s. and costs was inflicted, the alternative being fourteen days in gaol. THEFT FROM A CHIPPED-1s YEATO SHOP.—Mary . Jane Hamblen, a voung-Moking person, residing at 15, Morrie- yard. was charged with stealing from the shop of Martha Jones, Orford-lane, coppers to the amount of is 6,l.—De- fondant said she had only taken four balf-pennies.—The Head Constable (Mr. Hunt) informed the Bench that the somplainant kept a chipped-potato shop at the end of Pinner's-brow, whilst the defendant was a fustian cutter by trade. On Saturday night she and one cr two more cam- passions entered the shop, and had some chipped-potatoes. The defendant was seen to lift a bag down from the wall three times and to take something from it—the complainant had said that the b ise contained from is. 6.1. to 2s. in coppers • little boy named Walter Siddalt happened to see this, 1111. i ran home and told ire mother what he had seen. Mrs. Siddall told him to run hack and tell Mrs. Jones, which he did. and shortly afterwards a constable was sent for. Haring been to,cl what had eccurred the officer arrested the defend- ant et her house, and on being charged she soid she had only taken 2d.—The complainant mid there would be quite le. 61 . .</p>
        <p>In the bag, for n A long before the ocmrrence she had tsken about £8 out of it, and she had then noticed that there were several coppers left in it —Walter iddal and Katherine Kimblin both said they had seen defmdant take the bag d iwn and put her right hand into it on two occasions. The last witness remarked that .tetendsut had shown het four half-pennies which she bad taken. —Other testimony was given, and the prisoner was committed to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS-A YANKEE ANXIOUS TO RETURN HOME.— William Taylor. 36. Winwick-road, wss proved to have been drunk and disorderiy in Horsmnsrket-street, oa Satarday night, but was dischsrged, as he had been locked up since that occasion.—George Curtis, who averred th it he was a New Yorker, and who was described as "of no settled abode," was found guilty of hsvin !been drunk in Progh di- lane on the previous day. It WAS stated by P.C. Corcoran, who apprehended him. that the prisoner first complained of being injured In his right arm. He had, however, been medically examined and h-s stat.ment found to be false. There was nothing at di the matter with him. Prisoner ob- served that he was trying to get hack to America. and Li the magistrates would overio k his offence he pr'dnised ti leave the town Immediately and nev,r return. The Bench took him at his word and discharged him. UNLICENSFD DOGS—For keepine clogs without having licenses for them th, following persons were lined the costs 2s. 6d. : -James l.awrinson, 9, Appleton-street : was Lawrinson, 72, Xerloot-str4et ; and Arthur Rostern, 126, OrfOrd-lane.</p>
        <p>"A DANGER TO Mum., AND THE POBLIC."—John Walton outer, of 103, Church-street; was summoned foe being drunk whilst fa charge of e hors, and cart on the 25th October in Bridge-street—P.C. Corcoran proved seeing the defendant in a drunken condition driving a horse and cart towards Bridge- foot. When he reached the approach to the bridge he fell out o tne cart on his head, and very narrowly esc^ped being killed Witness picked him up, and after having bAndAged his head, had him conveyed home. He was in she pleas state of drunkenness at the time.—Elefen +ant denied being drunk, saying that he fell out of the cart through the wheel of the conveyance ;ping over a stone, and his legs were so bad tb he could not walk.—Councillor Pierpoint remarked that toe defendant ought to be very much ob iged to the offiler for bandaging up his wounds ,nd ,or seeing iim home. The constable had saved him a doctor's bill, and he thought defen- dant should pay him the medical fee. There was no donbt that he was drunk and such men as he were a dancerto them- se.ves and the public. Tha defendant was discharged on payment of costs, 3s. 81. BREACH or THE PEACE —John Longshaw, greengrocer of 18. Foundry-street, and Herbert Stokes. forge labourer. of 4, Warburton-street, were eaeh bound over in sureties of £5 to keep the peace for six months for having fought in Parker- street on the 29th October. They were further ordered to pay thP costs, 1.03. each. ORSCIENE LANGUAGE.— Three boys, named Daniel Rowlands (14), 28, Sehoo,-brow ; *Samuel Lomas (13), 11. Crossley-street; and Walter Hankey (10), 24, School-brow, were each found guilty ol using oto,ceoe language to two "omen on the 20th October in Sixp.nny-fiel is, and the m .gistrates ordered all of them to receive six strokes of the birch.</p>
        <p>TUMIDAY.—Before Mr. It Garnett On the cha,r), and Dr. Bml' h.</p>
        <p>_ TRIMS MONTHS FOR WIFE ASSAULT —Richard Warburton. of Tanners-lane. was charged on warrant with having assaulted and beaten his wife, Klizabeth Warburton. n the 17th October.—Com,dainant said that she d 44 not live with the defendant, who had previously assaulted her. On the day In question he came into the house where she was living and demanded a supply of be-r and tobac'o. She refused to accede to his request, and he then struck her in the mouth. On the following Saturday he again entered the house, and getting possession of her cogs he cut them to pieces. He had recently obtained emplovm mt at Whitecro.s W irks, but given it up. Quest oiled by the Beneh, defendant said t. the work was too hard for him.—Mrs. Glover corrlboraterl. - Defendant, who plead d guilty, was sent to prison for three months with hard labour WEDMISDAY.—Before Mr. Johi Pairclough (chairman) and</p>
