      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. Moirtux.—Before Mr. 0. fi*xlehurst and Mr. James Handley.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. Moirtux.—Before Mr. 0. fi*xlehurst and Mr. James Handley.</p>
        <p>Scwoot. kryitynawcz COng.—The following persons were fined 6d. and 4. 64. costs for neglecting to cause their chil- dren to school regularly :—Thomes Jones. qurrey-st.; Philip Handley, Heatt-rond ; Henry Roane. Rutland-et. ; Bernard Plannagan. Lock-st. ; Henry Burton, Cavendish-et. ; and George Morrison, Long ROW, Halton. UNLICENSED PEDLARS.—WiIIiam Mcnermat, and William Robinson, strangers in the tewn.were charged with having on the 2nd November at Runcorn, unlawfully anted as nediars without haying In force certificates.—P.C. Gregory stated that at 10 n m on Saturday night he Was in comnanv with Sergeant Gill in plain clothes. and when in Penketh lane.the prisoner Robinson. asked him to buy sot e laces The other prisoner was also selling laces On being questioned ag to their licenses they admitted having none,and were looked up. —Superintendent Atherton stated that the wakes had at- tracted the men to Runcorn.—They were discharged on pre- mising to leave the town.</p>
        <p>CNA.P.62 OF TNT:FT.—Jane Cooke, 60 years of age, was charged with stealing 20Ibs. of coal, value 2d . th. property ef th. Wanchester Ship Canal Cunpany.—Mr Knowles, solici- tor, widnee, appeared toe the fief mee. and peodueel a medi- cal certificate. stating that the defendant was suffering from shock, and was unable to be ves nt. He asked for an ad- journment until Friday.—The magistrates grlnted the ad- jecrnrnent.</p>
        <p>A WARRLICT lasurn.—john Moores, was summoned at the instance of he wif, Hannah Moores, Trinity-st., with as. senlting her. Defendant dl4 not apreser. -The enmoleinant stated that owing to her husbano's conduct she derermined to Ilveaartfromhtm Re. howey.r. kept vi.itlng her house and abosinz her. She stated thst 'shout a month in he Wnt hold of her by the head, and threatened what he worild do to her. Had not a young man been there at the time, he would have done her injury. She was (desirous of a generation ordor.—The magistrates Issued a warrant for the defendant's t.</p>
        <p>THE MeV. A. M. MITCHP.LL AT THE LIVERPOOL DIOCESAN CONPERENCE.—A. SCENE. At the sitting of the above conference, on Tues- day, the Rev. A. M Mitchell asked permission to discuss the report just presented on the tern peranre question.—The Bishop said it was not usual to dis- CUSS reports. but Mr. Mitchell could, if he desired, move that the report be not accepted.—The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said he could not go se far as that, R 9 be had the greatest appreciation for the work of the society and the deepest affection for Mr. Hirst, I tbe secretary, but—The Bishop: I must rise to a ipoint of order. We don't usually diACUSA reports. —The Rev. A. M. Mitchell (warmly) : I think it is unfair not to be allowed to discuss matters at, the Liverpool Diocesan Conference. ("Chair. chair.") I have often tried to catch your lordship's eve, and having caught your eve—(vigorous cries of " Chair " and " Sit down") and wishing to speak, I iam ruled out of order. (Uproar.)—The Bishop : I I must appeal to the conferenre to support me. (Ap- plause.) During the whole fourteen years I have been chairman WA have never once discussed a re- port. If anyone likes he can move that a report be not received—The incident then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 9, 195:</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 9, 195:</p>
        <p>NEW BOOKSIMACi-AZINES, ETC.</p>
        <p>, MACMILLAN AND CO.—Under the fostering wing of these publishers the "Century "Magazine iBSUOS its 25th anniversary number and commences with this month, a new volume. The only notable change in this magazine from those previously issued by Mr. T. Fisher Unwin, is that two well- matched colours are used on the cover. Inside, is presented the usual budget of art, literature, sketches on politics, religion, etc. "The Century never commenced a year with brighter prospects or better reading matter,and among the contents are, first and foremost, the opening chapters of a new serial by Mrs. Humphrey Ward, entitled "Sir George Tressady," which promises to add consider- ably to the fame of the popular novelist ; The Devotion of Enriquez," by Bret. Harte ; "Equality as the 13aeis of Good Society," by William Dean; the continuation of the "Life of Napoleon Bona- parte," by William M. Sloane ; " Eleanore Duse," J. Rankin Towse ; and the "Armenian Question," by the Right Hon. James Bryce, M.P. Another feature is an autobiographical sketch of the painter, Vibert, which serves as an introduction to a series of repro- ductions of his canvases, each of which, will, like the one entitled "The Missionary's Story," be accompanied by a brief story from the pen of M. Vibert. The illustrative beauty of " The Century" is maintained and Messrs. Macmillan and Co., have a fair chance of seeing the magazine from America into its 50th year—" St. Nicholas,"--good old St. Nichelas—also enters on a new volume and that its 23rd. The original standard has been adhered to in its production and with its almost inexhausti- ble fund of stories and articles "St. Nicholas," has every chance of enslaving new readers and finding fresh tasks for the old ones. Two serials of great promise are entered upon, the first from the pen of William 0. Stoddard, being entitled "The Sword-maker's r-on," a story of the year 30, A.D. and full of incident. " The Prize Cup," by J. T. 'fro wbridge,is the name of the second,which though of i far different character, possesses equal attrac- tion. Mary Mapes Dodge still stands at the helm of "St. Nicholas," and is prepared to pilot it successfully through the future as in the past. Such an old friend 118 "M a cmi I lan's Magazine" needs little explaining or detailing. Departures are seldom in connection with it,and the November No. shows none. "A Bride Elect" is the title of the new serial. Editions of everything "for the People "are the rage just now, and worthily so. The latest we have received are the first two represents- 'Alves of an edition in sixpenny volumes of Lord Tennyson's poetical works, to be published fort " Juvenilia" and "The Lady of Shallot,&amp;c." ••• • two of the prettiest little books it has been our fortune to possess for some time. They are small —a decided advantage—and most tastefully com- piled, while the design on the cloth back is elegant in the extreme. Thick paper and clear type are used for the inside pages, and as it will take 23 volumes to complete the series, a charming shelf- full will be provided by them.—A eimilar series,now well-known, le the "Kingsley Series," and "Here- ward the Wake" is the last iesue,and is complete in one volume of over 500 pagee.—Still another series, which has now assumedlreePectable proportions,re- mains to be dealt with, viz., The Illustrated Stan- dard Novels, of which the last edition is " Popular Tales," by Maria Edgworth. Miss Chris Hammond is the illustrator, and Anne Thackeray Ritchie is the writer of the introduction. Both these ladies have filled similar capacities in the previous issues of \Hee Edgworth'e works, and have given every satisfaction. Though "Popular Tales" was pub- lished so early as 1812 there are many who have as yet not made its acquaintance, end who, with this opportunity will be glad to do so. FREDERICK WARNE AND Co.—rhe Rev. Silas K. Hocking's latest work "The Heart of Man " has just emerged in book torm from the press of these publishers. We say in book form for the story has already achieved notoriety as a serial in the "Christian World," therefore it is not entirely new nor has it its" spurs to win." There is always an advantage in being able to read a book straight off, and with such a one as "The Heart of Man," the reader appreciates this privilege to the full. The story ranks as one of—if not the best, which has emanated from the pen of Mr. Hocking. Briefly described, the novel deals with the forced exile of ths, hero through having in a struggle thrown an enemy ever a cuff. While exiled, Arthur Lewellyn (the hero) forms an attachment with a girl in the fens which is returned, and centinuee through the lives of both. The trustful, gentle character of the girl wine the esteem of every reader. A considerable sojourn in the fens brings Lewellyn into acquaintance with some odd personages, Mr. Hocking's conception of whom being fine. Dartmoor ultimately engulfs Lewellyn and the description of life in that great prison is of exceeding interest, proving that the author's acquaintance with the subject is from more than heresay. Release eventually comes from a sudden discovery and transpiration of circum- stances entirely unexpected by the reader, and therefore the more appreciated. With so many ex- cellent qualities, can it be wondered at that " The Heart of Man "is so early in possession of popu- larity. The book is handsomely bound, well printed, and capably illustrated, while the price is 3s. 6d. _ _ - -</p>
        <p>T. FrsHEE Utswirf.—Mr. Unwin has introduced by Guide's " Toxin," a library of novels named after the " Century t' magazine. In one thing only have they a resemblance to that periodical, and that is in the superior fineness of tneir illustrations.' These latter strike the eye at once as being out of the common, and we doubt if anything equal to them has vet graced the pages of a paper-backed novel. The latter is, itself, also out of the common, and has a weird, deep, fascination. There are only three characters in it, and two of these are of the light-hearted, butterfly order ; the other is entirely original—a man of profound surgical skill, but ab- solutely heartless, as the word is understood. Not that cruelty is his delight, but the pain he inflicts is merely in the service of science, for which he would commit any crime, without being in the least disturbed or realizing that it was a crime in the eyes of man and God. This man's influence is groat and like his nature, unfathomable,so that to other persons he is an object ot fear. Though practically without feeling, he shows himself to be susceptible to love,and through its influence obtains his end. The price of the book is Is. 61. CASSRLL AND Co.—With the current month there commences a new volume of Cassell's " Magazine of Art," whose predominant feature is a full page original etching of "The Silvery Thames" by Francis S. Walker, R.P.E.; this itself is worth the is. charged for the magazine and is a beautiful specimen of light and shade in sepia. Mr. F. G Kitton contributes an article on "Some Portraits of Sir Walter Scott " which is illustrated by six repro- ductions of portraits of Scott by the most promi- nent artists of his day. Some 7or 8 other articles are also given and everything in the magazine en- titles Wl° a continuance of the favour which it has so long received,—" The Quiver" for November also 'commences a fresh volume and presents a" bill of I fare" very tempting to the literary palate. Not I only are the articles good but a pictorial supple- ment giving particulars and portraits of leaders of the Church and views of some fanaous churches and memorials. Two new serials are commenced,one, a story of Cambridge life. "Andrew Clay's Awakening," by Alan St. Aubyn, and the other " The Junior Partner at Moreton's " a story of a girl's mistakes, by F. Axtens. Particulars of a prize scheme in which .£2OO in book prizes for Scripture Knowledge are offered, are given. F. V. WHITE AND Co.—The annual Autumn novel by John Strange Winter, and which is issued by these publishers, has been received by us. The title is odd but striking, being " I married a wife." The story is quite up to Mrs. Stannard's average and quietly interesting through- out. No plot is made out, and the story is of a purely social character, and based on the love of "slumming "by the wife referred to in the title. An untoward result cures her of this somewhat in- convenient fad.—The novel is illustrated in a creditable manner.</p>
        <p>In accordance with the arrangement come to between the master engineers on the Clyde and at Belfast twenty five per cent of the Clyde engineers wore locked out on Tuesday night. The District Association of Engineers met in Glasizow on tha same evening and decided to meet the action of the masters by withdrawing their members from all the yards in which the lock-out had taken place. By this decision between 5,000 and 8,000 men will be thrown idle.</p>
        <p>' THE SKIN IS COMPOSED of three layers. and con- tains muscles, nerves, blood VeSSPIP. sebaceous glands, sweat glands, etc.., while the hair and nails are its appendaves It is most complicated piece of machinery, and sobj..e.t, like any other organ, to health and disease. To preserve the skin,scalo and hair and to restore them to a condition of health. when diseased, nothing is so agreeable or so speedily effective rig CIITICIIRA SOAP, assisted in the severer forms with mild applications of CIITICIIILA '(ointment), the great skin cure.</p>
      </div>
