      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RUGBY. NORTHERN RUGBY UNION. WARRINGTON v. WIGAN.</head>
        <p>RUGBY. NORTHERN RUGBY UNION. WARRINGTON v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>These old rivals played their first match this season on Saturday at Wigan. The local team were accompanied on the train by some 600 enthusiasts, and as the railway company had only provided accommodation for about 350 it may readily imagined that the journey to Wigan was not performed in all cases under the most comfort- able circumstances. It was said that as many as 23 got in one compartment, while in another the tickets had been collected and as the collector opened the door at Golborne he had seventeen thrust in his hand, his con stetnation causing some amusement amongst the travel- ers. However, Wigan was safely reached, but it was not until twenty minutes after the advertised time that Warrington were able to putin an appearance on the field. The weather was really' charming. The match attracted about 6,000 people. and the game was watched with exceptional interest. The play of the new players was favourably criticised, and it was generally predicted that though Wigan succeeded in winning on this occasion they would in all probability have to play second fiddlel in the return match. The game was well contested from, start to finish,ana that there was very little in the teams was shown by the fact that in the first half there was no score on either side. In the second half. Wigan managed to get three tries, but they were in positions from which it would have been difficult to convert them Into goals. Warrington had hard lines, but though they failed to score it cannot be said that on the whole they were overplayed. The Warrington backs were quite as strong as the Wigan backs, in fact the two halves were the best on the field. The, Wigan forwards, however, appeared to be a little stronger, and had a way of getting the ball out of the scrimmage and passing to the backs, which gave them an advantage on several occasions. The teams 1 ; were as follows :—Warrington: Heesom, full back; ; Barber (Captain), Cross, Burton, and Broady, three- quarters; Peden and Houghton, half backs; Burrows, Saunders, Wilcock, Fairhurst, Barrett, Rill, Thornton. and Andrews, forwards. Wigan : Winstanley, full back; Rearm, Flowers, Jag°, and Wilding, three-quartere ; Walker and Forster, half-backs; Unsworth, Rigby, Web- ster, Yates, Perkins, Brown, Blackburn, and Donnelly, forwards. Warrington were short of Nevins at half-back, his place being taken by Foden. Wigan played their three county men, Winstanley, Unsworth, and Brown. Warrington won the toss. Wigan kicked off, and the ball went into touch at the Warrington quarter flag. Several scrimmages followed the throw out, the Wigan players getting the ball out on each occasion and passing to the backs. Little advantage, however, was gained, Cross intercepting one pass, and Railton having put in a kick frcm another Heesom nicely returned, and War- rington took the ball to centre. where Widen made his mark. Following the free kick, the Wigan forwards made a combined rush, and carried the ball into dan- gerous proximity to the Warrington lines, Broady ulti- mately stopping its further progress. Fodea, shamming a pass, got past the Wigan forwards and put in a splen- did run,finally passing to Broady, who was not grounded till he reached the Wigan quarter nag. Warrington now made a determined attack on the home lines, and some good passing was witnessed between Houghton, Paden, Barber, Cross, Burton and Broady. Wigan, however, gradually relieved the pressure and in turn attacked the visitors' lines, Heesom once or twice saving in capital style. Wigan, however, continued to press, and twice the ball was kicked dead. Heesom just previously hav- ing been compelled to touch down. A few minutes before the call of half time, Warrington looked likely for scorieg, the result nf a smart run by Barber,but nothing came if it, and half time was called without either side having •c,r do Hill restarted the game and Railton returned and for a time play was pretty even. A fine dribble by Hough- ton, Broady and Barber was stopped by Railton before the ball was taken far into Wigan lines. The dribble was more than contelacted by a reply by the Wigan forwards and their progress was stayed by Houghton kicking in touch at the quarterflag. A gassing movement,initiated by Walkden, and taken part in by Wilding, Jago and Flowers, was spoiled by the latter mulling the ball when he had a very favourable chance of scoring, and Cross staved off danger by a flying kick in touch. Wigan con- tinued to press, and at length a minor was conceded to them. After the restart, exchange kicks took place between Broady, Winstanley and Burton the latter find- ing touch. From the subsequent line outand scrimmage, Webster dribbled down the field and was very nearly in. Another touch down was immediately afterwards con- ceded. Flowers was again wanting tn his reply for the kick out, and thus afforded relief to Warrington. Wigan however, came down the field again, and Cross stopped a grand rush just in the nick of time. At length from a scrimmage near the line, Wilding accepting a pass from , Walkden, managed to get over the line at the corner. Winstanley failed to land a goal.. Wigan w •re alino3r in again immediately afterwards, Broady just tackling Wilding in time. Another spell of attacking ended in Wigan scoring a lucky try, for Cross attempting to kick dead sent the ball against the bottom of the goalposts, and upon it cannoning on to Raillton's feet he had simply to drop on it. Webster failed at goal. Feden made his mark after the kick out, and by a big kick found touch past half way,for almost the first time after the interval, Warrington had crossed the line. Wigan, however, quickly returned to the attack and Warrington bad a very hard time of it. They, however, kept Wigan out until the last minute of the match, when Flowers got over again. Winstanley failed at goal, and the whistle blew immediately after with the result that Wigan bad won the match by three tries (9 points) tomil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN ‘.. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>- --- The Huddersfield team visited the Irwell-lane enclosure on Saturday. after an absence at four years. The home team could not place their full strength in the field, Robinson, Farmer, Reece and Johnson were absentees threuett Injuries; whilst the Tykes mustered their fullest available strength, Smith again taking up his position at centre. The following players, under the supervision of Mr. El. Underwood (Wigan), took up their positions as follows Runcorn: J. Jolley', full back ; 0. Dunhavin, Warder, H. Myers, W. Gayter, three-quarter backs ; S. Abrams and J. Faulkner, half-backs ; W. Faulkner, A. Taylor J. Langley, H. Williams. F. Gayter, S. Walker, G. Moores and C. Pendlebury, ' forwards. Huddersfield: F. Lorrimer, full hack; Pearson, Smith, G. Lorrimer and Boothroyd, three-quarterbacks ; J. Lorrimer and Littlewood, half backs; Lodge, Sutcliffe, Dickinson, Steveason,Chambers, Wilson, Brooks and Foley, forwards. Runcorn acing the toss, gave Huddersfield the) advant- age of the wind, which blew from goal to geal. W. Faulkner commenced operations, and an exchange of kicking ended in the first scrimmage being formed in the visitors' 25. It was soon evident that the home for- warda were the smarter lot,and heeling out, Abrams and J. Faulkner soon set the backs at work and In the first five minutes the Tykes' lines were hotly wailed. Dunbavin had a shot for goal from a " mark " by Warder, but the ball just sailed wide. F. Lorrimer got in some neat returns,but the home forwards quickly had the ball back, and still the warfare was waged in close proximity to the visiting team's lines and alter Warder being held up on the line Abrams initiated a round of passing which culminated in Myers scorieg a clever try in a favourite position, but Dunbavin's kick failed. From the kick out,iluddeesfield by long kicking invaded the home half for the first time, bet Myers checked their progress and then the home forwards went off with a big rush which Smith stayed in a plucky manner. Following this,anether passing movement saw Warder I making a fine run, and on getting to F. Lorriman he I passed to Dunbavin,who had no one to pass,but he failed to hold the ball and a certain try was lost, a rather bad finish to what otherwise would have been a piece of bril- liant work. After this the game was contested more evenly,and from a pass by Littlewood, Pearson made a good run along the touch line, but Myers safely ac- counted for him. A moment later, Boothroyd made a I capital attempt to force an opening, but was overhauled by Warder,before he ceuld reach the home custodian. A lot of mid-held play followed, until Abrams and J. Faulkner started another movement, and the ball was passed and repassed to one another in a lively way, but the Yorkshiremen defended in a most stubborn manner and at times their play was very vigorous, and &amp;even,' stoppage.' had to be made for" repairs." However, the Runcornians completely overran them,and it seemed al- most miraculous how their line escaped disaster time after time. Taylor got over on one occasion, but failed to get the right side of the referee. Abrams and J. Faulkner made some splendid openings,but luck seemed dead against them, and the interval arrived with the score, Runcorn, one try ; Huddersfield, nil. Lodge re- started the second half, and Myers replying the home forwards were soon making tracks for their opponents' lines. C. Pendlebury head- ing aeldribble, whilst the Huddersfield custodian robbed Taylor when in the act of dribbling the ball over the line. Myers and Warder were prominent with some tricky play and from a line eut Taylor !secured, and throwing to Abrams he transferred to Warder, who gave to Dunbavin, and he when challenged repassed to Warder, who finished a grand piece of work by scoring behind the posts. Dunbavin placing with success. The restart saw Runcorn again in the ascendant, but F. Lorrimer and Smith saved time after time. At length, after some cnnsidorable pressure, Sutcliffe made 5a fine dribble, and for the first time during the second half the ball reached t ,e heme quarters, but its stay was only brief, the Cestrians soon forcing therm bask. After a lot of passing Warder made a futile drop at goal. The drop out brought no relief, the enthusiasm of the spectators being raised to the highest pitch, when Abrams and J. Faulkner with their inimitable combination, got going. which baffled the visiting backs completely, and the former scored in a favourable position. It was a brilliant</p>
        <p>movement, and worth going a long way to see ; Dun-1 bavin, however, failed to augment. Runcorn still kept ' up the attack, but no further addition was made to the score, a one-sided game ended in another victory for Runcorn by one goal, two tries (eleven points),to Hud- dersfield .nothing. WIDNES v. TYLDESLEY. This match was played at Tyldesley on Saturday,befor about 1,000 spectators. Widnes had only a weak team. Tylfiesley won the toss, and Drummond started for Wid- nes 25 minutes after the advertised time. Scriminaging ensued at the centre,and Widnes were penalised. Berry got over near the posts, Lawton failing at an easy place. From the Krimmage Berry passed wide to Miller, who ran over at the corner, Harris making a miserable attempt at goal. Final result—Tyldesley, three tries ; Widnes, nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON" A "V. IN 16101 "A."—There VMS a good gathering to watch the above teams play at Warrington on Saturday. The game started about an hour late, owing te the visitors not arriving in time. Teorniley kicked off for the homestere, the ball being quickly returned to the Warrington 25 line, where Evans saved neatly. The visitors ru‘hed the ball closet, the home goal, where a short streegie took place and finally Evans broke away with the ball, but was upset though not before he managed to get the ball to midfield. From a throw out, Carey got possession and passed to Lawless, who passed to Forster, the latter missing. Lawless rushed in and recovered the ball,but he was tackled and grounded before he could run. Price attempted a long pass, but the ball fell into the hands of the visitors frora whom Lawless captured it and ran down the field. Higham,the Wigan full back, attempted to stop him but did not succeed in doing so until the ball was passed to Carey, wt o scored behind the posts. Evans, from an easy position, converted the point into a goal. On resuming play, the visitors kicked the ball up the field, but Lawless was quick to reply and Carey broke away and after a splendid run passed to Moores,who scored after a desperate struggle. The try wasunconverted and soon afterwards half time came wi h Warrington leading by 1 goal, 1 try,to nil. During the second half the passing was better than during the first part, although several good passes were missed. Soon after resuming, the War- rington men got possession of the ball and kept it for a short time, and Lawless got off and scored another try. The kick for goal failed. Moeres broke away and it seemed as if he would score anottier try,but Ise was held just as he was about to ground the ball near the posts. The visitors, with a combined rush then took the ball down to the home 25, and got it dangerourly near the home line, but Price was on the alert and tke visitors soon found themselves back in their own quarters. Dur- ing the last few minutes the play was rather loose and the game ended in Warrington being the victors with a score of one goal, two tries, to nil. The following were the teams on the field :—Wigan : Full back, Fligham ; three-quarters, Samuels, Austin, Seddon. and Fairhurst ; half backs. Mitchinson and Lowe; forwards, McNamara, Collier, Kearsley, Derbyshire, Peet, Pres- cott, Newsome and Fairclough. Warrington : Full back, Evans; three-quarters, Moores, Lawless, Carey, and Fenster , half-backs, Clarke and Price ; forwards, Swift, Higbee', Savage, Royle, Schott* Thorniley, Monks, and Hampton. WARRINGTON Sr. MARY'S v. OLDHAM "A."—The above teams met on the ground of the former. The visitors won the toss.and Thomas kicked off for the Saints. The home team at once pressed, and after some scrimmaging were await:lel a free-kick,'which resulted in a minor. Tall kicking by Lawton put his team ea the attack, but Roughley was safe, The Oldham backs passing finely, but Warburton tackled Knotts when becoming danger- ous. From the ensuing scrimmage Lawton got well away, passed to Fillingham, who scored near the cor- ner, Bolton failing at goal. On the re-start Warburton made a grand run, Lamonby holding him on the line! H Bennett and Higginbottom, who were playing a good game, tried hard to get over,but to no purpose,the tackl- ing being too good. Half time—Oldharn one try, St. Mary's nil. Whittle kicked off, and the Saints at once pressed, Roughley dropping at goaLthe ball rolling dead. Not to be denied the home team again attacked, and rushed over at the corner, Hampson making a grand bid for goal, the ball grazing the cross-bar. Higginbottom met the drop out and kicked across to T. Bennett. who picked up smartly and scored, but the referee ruled him off side. Oldham now had a turn, but Hampson and Siddall defended well. A rush by the Saints, headed by Barrows, Foy, and Caldwell, took play well in the visi- tors' half, but Lawton stayed their progress. Flampson worked hard to score, but all to no purpose, and just be- fore time Lawton got clear away, passed to Fillingham, and this speedy three-quarter scored,thus giving Oldham the victory by two tries to one. The home team played well to a man, and Lawton, Filliagham, and Lamonby were the pick of the visitors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S V. CHESTER COLLEGE.—PIayed at Wilderspool Causeway. The home team were unable to put a representative lot in the field, as Tickell and Dug- dale were away playing in the district team against Barnton Rovers, and A. Duckworth was also unable to play. Robson, although still unwell, took his old posi- tion between the sticks, and Valles also assisted in the I forwards. The home team kicked off, and the visitors n! lying with the wind had the best of matters, scoriae from a scrimmage iii goal after ten minutes' play. The ICollege were still the better team up to the interval, but, failing to again score, half time arrived with Chester one goal to nil. After the re-start St. Elphin's had the measure of their opponents, but through bad shooting failed to score, several splendid chances being allowed to pass bv, a very good game resulting—Chester, 1; St. Elphins, 0. All the winning team played well, and it would be unfair to individualise. Per St. Elphin's,Tur- ner made a good snbstitute for Dugdale, and ancong4 a good set of backs, Robson, R. Colley, and Thomson de- serve mentien. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. CHERRYTREE WANDERERS. —St Mary's received a visit from the above club on Saturday. Owing to the late arrival of the visitors,2 35's only were played. It was four o'clock when hostilities were commenced, both teams at once settling down in real earnest, St. Mary's having the best of the game for a period, St. Mary's forwards being in good form, E. Mather scoring after 15 minutes' play. From the restart the home forwards again got possession, which resulted in the right wing getting down and centreing, the re- doubtable "Teddy " again putting the ball through. Cherrytree wakened up after this, and meeting with a half hearted defence scored. St. Mary's,however,scored again before half time through T. Mather. The second half proved very even, each side striving to gain the upper hand. No score, however, was registered until elose on time, Mahoney running out to clear his charge enabled Cherrytree to put on their second goal, the whistle shortly after being blown with the score—St. Mary's, 3 ; Cherrytree Wanderersa. HAVDOCK WESLEYANS V. STOCKTON HEATH.—Played at Haydock. The homesters won the toss, and elected to play wi'h the wind at their backs. A smart combined movement on the part of Haydock forwards carried play , towards the visitors' goal,the Heathens' custodian clear- ing brilliantly. Play was transferred to the other end, where Hallows had a shot at goal, the ball going wide. Play continued very even for the first twenty minutes, after which the homesters notched the first point. This was quickly followed by two more, half time arriving with the score in favour of Haydock by three goals to nil. With the wind in their favour in the second half the visitors attacked, but all their efforts to gain any- thing tangible were lost by inaccurate shooting. From a break-away by the Haydock forwards the home centre still furthir increased the lead. With four goals against them the visitors played up pluckily, Johnson heading through five minutes off time. The Heathens continued to press, and with a long shot Probed added a second goal. With the visitors still on the attack the whistle sounded for time, Haydock thus winning by four goals to two.</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY V. WARRINGTON OLYMPICS.—PIayed on the Olympics' ground on Saturday, and resulted in a draw of one goal each. ST. PAUL'S V. STOCKTON HUTH RICSIIRVR.—A.t Stockton Heath, St. Paul's arrived short, good substitutes being found. The homesters soon got the best of matters, and pressed continually, but could not score. Close on half- time Andrews shot the first goal for the homesters. On turning round, Stockton Heath:again took up the run- ning, and some good combination by the forwards ended in Biggs adding the second point. The homesters soon after forced a corner, and Titchett in attempting to clear kicked through his own goal. Goals were also scored by Kimberley and Attewell, the game ending in a win for Stockton Heath by five goals to nil. ST. ELPHINS CHURCH LADS BRIGADE V. CHESHIRE LINRS Woruts.—A match was payee! between St Elphins Brigade and Cheshire Lines Works, on the ground of he former and ended in a win for the homesters. Re- sult, St. Elphins Church Lads Brigade 5 goals, Cheshire Lines Works 1 goal. RUNCORN ASSOCIATION V. FARNWORTH (Rasnavn Tgams).—This return engagement was played at Run- corn on Saturday. Burrows won the toss and Martin set the ball in motion, and the Farnworth forwards regis- tered their first point, through Boyle. Restarting, the Farnworth eleven were making their way to the Run- corn goal, when Burrows relieved with a long kick to touch. The Farnworth front rank again got possession, and took play to the Runcorn territory. Runcorn were now penalised for an infringement of the rule at close quarters. The free kirk resulted in Roberts shooting wide. The Farnworth front rank again received, and Leathwood getting hold scored a beauty for the visitors. Restarting, the Runcorn guintette made tracks fcr the visitors' goal,the visitors' custodian being obliged to concede a corner. Nothing resulted. Half time ar- rived with the score—Farnworth, 2 ; Runcorn, 0. Cross- ley restarted, and from a goal kick the home team got possession, and set to work in earnest,putting in a grand forward movement, which resulted in Parry scoring the first point forlithe homesters.:Final—Farnworth, 2 ; Run- corn, 1.</p>
        <p>At the Manchester County Court on Tuesday, Miss Annie Rimington, a milliner, was awarded £l5 damages against Mr. Buckley ilarrop, a shopkeeper, as compen- sation for a dogbite.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CROMWELL HEAD.</head>
        <p>ThE CROMWELL HEAD.</p>
        <p>AN INTERESTING PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>The "Daily Chronicle" publishes a gruesome sketch of the mummy head reputed to be that of Cromwell, together with an interesting story of that !relic : Oliver Cromwell was buried in Henry VlL's Chapel at Westminster. After the Restoration, his body and the bodies of Ireton, his son-in-law, and Bradshaw ,were dug up, taken on sledges to Tyburn, and hanged on the three several angles of the gallows until sunset. They were then beheaded, the trunks thrown into a deep pit, and the heads set upon poles on the top of Westminster Hall. That, and the fall of Cromwell's head one night from its I dreadful perch at Westminster, is all history. Now begins the record existing in reference to the reputed head—a wholly traditional record necessarily, in the early past. The tradition runs that on a stormy 'night about 25 years after the heads of Cromwell, Ireton, and Bradshaw had been set on Westminster Hall, that of Cromwell was blown down. It was picked up by a sentinel, who hid the mummy in the chimney of his house, only revealing its presence there to his wife and daughter on his deathbed. By this time the mystery of the disappearance would have been more or less forgot- ten, and the wife and daughter were able to sell it to a Cambridgeshire family named Russell. This family was intimately connected with the Cromwells by marriage, and so would naturally have desired to preserve so important a part of the remains of the Lord Protec- tor. It continued in the possession of the Russells for long years, the last to own it being one Samuel Rus- sell. With him, however, the head fell on evil days,and was exhibited, at so much of a charge, in the vicinity of Clare Market, James Cox,the proprieter of a museum coveted the head for his place, and offered Samuel Rus- sell £lOO for it. Russell refused the offer, but later was indebted to Cox for a loan amounting to that sum. In consideration of the loan and a further amount he even- tually handed over the heirloom for Cox's museum— perhaps in the year 1780. Thus there was a forced sale of what was worth Sellißg and worth buying only be- cause it was believed to be Cromwell'a head. But this was hardly a greater dignity for the head than to be often exhibited by Russell while—as the tale goes—that worthy was drunk. When Cox retired from his museum just off Bond-street, he in turn sold the trophy,there be- ing this time three buyers. They were gentlemen of democratic views,and the period was early in the French Revolution, but the head was still kept on show. The price they gave for it was £230, and it is ominously added that they all died sudden deaths. The last sur- vivor of the three was a friend of Mr. Josiah Henry Wil- kinson, and the grandfather of the present owner, Mr. Horace Wilkinson, of Sevenoaks, Kent, bought it from his dead friend's daughter, to whom it was left. This would have been about 1812, and the head was then in a strong oaken box, in which it is still kept. Indeed,the box, like its contents, came from the Russells, and both are now enclosed in a second case of polished wood. An oil painting which Mr. Horace Wilkinson bought some years ago at a sale in Sydenham gives a very good re- presentation of the head. The artist in one Cianch, and on the back the picture is described as " the Crom- well head."</p>
        <p>Having described the head, and shown its points of resemblance to the acknowledged portraits of Cromwell, even to the historic wart on the nose, the writer goes on to say that the point of great doubt with one of our pro- minent politicians who has seen the head several times is its general state of preservation, assuming it to have been exposed for more than a score of years to the weather. To this somebody else might reply that the head—an embalmed head, always remember—while no ill-preserved absolutely, yet bears ample traces of a long vigil in the open weather on Westminster Hall or else- where. Individual impression necessarily goes forla ' great deal in such a case. Any investigation as to the ' genuineness of the head would have to proceed on two main lines, the historical and the physiological. How ' does the story of the dead fit into accepted history ; and how does the head itself accord with our information as to Cromwell' head? These are pretty much the lines on which the problem was tackled by old Mr. Josiah Henry Wilkinson in the memoranda, which, with the relic, he bequeathed to his heirs. He finds that there has been no record in this country—anyhow no record affecting the period which must bo in question—of a head having been embalmed and spiked, except Cromwell's head. Iretcn's was not embalmed, neither was Bradshaw's, so granting the " Cromwel's head "was one of the three exposed on Westminster Hall, it must be Cromwell's. He discusses the possibility of a head being spiked as a deliberate de- ception, but rejects it for several reasor s. This include the great resemblance between the head in all its cardinal feature—nseasurements which establish that having been made —and the authenticated details of Cromwell's head Flaxman, the sculptor, had confirmed him in informa- tion he had already gathered, touching the peculiarities of Cromwell's head. The forehead was low and broad, the orbits of the eyes large and prominent, the cheek- bones high, the end of the nose fleshy, the chin not pro- minent, the lower jawbone particularly short and straight, a wart close above the right eyebrow at the angle nearest the nose. And so on. " If the bead has one peculiarity," said Flamm n, " it goes for a very great deal. That is a short, straight lower jawbone, a characteristic which belonged to all the Cromwells, and especially to Oliver." Mr. Horace Wilkinson's grandfather was able to point to such a peculiarity in the mummy, and, as yet one evidence more, to the chestnut hair. Perhaps the strongest point made as to the hair relates to that on the chin and the upper lip. For some little time before his death Crom- well refused to be shaved, and the cast of his face taken after death shows the hair which had grown on it. Set head and cast beside each other and see if they do not give evidence which interlinks. Then any smallness of the framework of the mummified face, in contrast to the broad countenance of the death-maak, is explained by the fact that Cromwell had a very fleshy face. That, , indeed, anybody can see from his pictures, and when the face was reduced to the bonework the contraction in 11 breadth, and height even, would be most marked. 1 The gaping hack at the back of the head is adjudged as certainly a wound given to a lifeless and embalmed bead—not to' living head or a had from which the life had only shortly gone away. Going upon that, one might find a ready enough explanation of the mangled nose. Suppose Cromwell's body on being cut down at Tyburn was thrown on the ground, or on to a stone, and beheaded, not placed on a block. The tremendous and ill•directed stroke which hell clove the head would have destroyed the nose, providing it drove against a hard, flat substance. " Now, don't you think it is really Cromwell's head ?" the late Professor Rolleston, of Oxford, was asked on one occasion. He had examined the relic, studied its history, noted the mark where the wart is supposed te have been, but he gave no definite reply to the question, and neither can anybody else at present. Is it always to be so •is an absolute solution of the question of identity impossible? I. the last word said on a matter which is linked with the personality of one of the int Englishmen of all time ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>VALUABLE BOOK FREE.</head>
        <p>A VALUABLE BOOK FREE.</p>
        <p>One of the authorities on questions scientific—the " Lancet," says : " Overwork of the stomach, liver, or kidneys is vastly more damaging to a man than over- work of the brain or muscles, since so long as the stomach is intact, overworked muschs may be easily repaired ; and so long as the liver and kidneys retain their integrity, the consequences of extra brain work are easily removed by the elimination of the resulting poisons from the body. This verifies the contents of a little explanatory free medical pamphlet on health, recently issued by the Vendors of Warner's Safe Cure, 86, Clerkenwell-road, London (obtainable by sending post-card), in which they prove clearly that if the liver and kidneys are in a sound healthy, and consequently, active condition, health is ' ensured, and the experience of thousands in the United Kingdom, equally as satisfactory as the one given below, shows that Warner's Safe Cure is the best liver and kidney medicine in the world. Mr. G. Pate, of 14, Brook-road, Eloper Holloway, London, N., says : "My experience with Warner's Safe Pills is so satisfactory that I cannot refrain from making a I üblic acknowledg- ment. For years I have been trying different kinds of laxatives and pills, and never found anything that suited me until I tried Warner's Safe Pills. My occupation oftentimes exposes meth dangerous and poisonous gases, which escape from drains and pipes, and I require, not only a laxative, but a medicine which will at the same time increk se the circulation and keep the blood pure. These pills are easy to take, leave no bad taste (being sugar coated), and never cause griping. My son-in-law ' says, if one has a headache he should never retire with- out taking one or two of these pills at night, which will ensure a disappearance of the headache by morning. iI have frequently recommended these pills to others, and , shall continue to do so whenever opportunity offers. I alight add that my sister-in-law received so much benefit from the use of Warner's Safe Cure, that she con- siders it an invaluable tonic, sate,pleasant and effective- for ladies.</p>
        <p>_ AMBULANCE LECTURE AT DA.RP.SZURY.</p>
        <p>A class in ambulance work, under the auspices of the Daresbury School Board Technical Commit- tee, was commenced on Monday evening by Dr. Bower, of Stretton. About 27 students attended and much interest is being taken in the course of lectures. The lecturer commenced by stating generally the subjects the class would be expected to learn, and pointed out the inestimahle value of first aid to the injured in many Cases of accident, drowning, &amp;c. A general outline of e s'ructure and functions of the human body, IC. h a short description of the bones, joints, muscles, action of heart, veins, &amp;c., was then dealt with in an inter- esting manner, illustrated by diagrams. &amp;c.. speci- ally prepared by the St. John Ambulance Associa- tion. The circulation of the blood and respiration was afterwards treated in an exhaustive manner, illustrated by suitable diagrams.</p>
        <p>SEWING MACHINES Hand £2 188.64. Treldle £44s. with handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. Durvos 16 and 18. Sankey-at.. Warrington. —Advt.</p>
        <p>llousErrorm Immm,hommed and marked free of charge. —and W. Dutton, Sankey-et., Warrington.—ildnt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>WARRINGTON FIReD CLAM Last evening week Mr. George sae'"--13 s exhibited a fine rough-legged buzzird (Asehlbut! lagopus), which was shot on the 26th Octoberha Mr. Taylor, at Rixton. This beautiful ; t measured 52 inches across the wings,and is the It of its species recorded in our district. The Buckton, of Childwall, read a paper on I;)ees, da°ll% chiefly with their life history. The subjeot.l7o.. treated in a most interesting way, and "si,i+oun- trated by an admirable series of slides. Mr " • us cillor Monks on this as on many previous ocsasl° anger manipulated the lantern, and the club is fort in numbering him among its members.fo n r he is elTa ready to help in this way. The paper was listeAwto to with much attention, and will probably lea', a, further study of this group of insects, since it 'a study which can be carried on with financial 1004 as intellectual profit.</p>
      </div>
