      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>N.B 63„ _ I 25.,145"37-a2</head>
        <p>N.B 63„ _ I 25.,145"37-a2</p>
        <p>THE UTTERING OF COUNTERFEIT COIN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>PRISONER COM HITT ED TO THE ASSIZE</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on 'l'hurs- day week, before the Mayor and Alderman Sutton Margaret Wilson, aged 30, was charged with wil- fully and knowingly uttering a false and counter- feit half-crown and three false florins, on October 23. . .</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Doncaster, assistant to Thomas Tongue, confectioner, Bridge-street, said that the prisoner came into the shop on Wednesday evening, and asked for 3d. of Eccles cakes, and a Id. of toffie. She supplied them to the prisoner wrapping them in the bag produced. She gave a 2s. piece to pay for the articles, and witness gave Is. Bd, change. She did not know that tie money was bad until the same evening, when P,C. Newsy came to the shop and asked her to come to the police station where she picked the woman out of five other women as the one whom she had served. _</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Lythgoe, confectioner, 35 S a nkey- street, said that he prisoner cane to her shop at seven o'clock on Wednesday evening and bought three penny cakes, and also gave a 2s. piece receiving is. 91. change. She went out, but witness was suspicious of her by her manner. She ex- amined the florin but could not see that it was bad. About half an hour later, P.C. Newey came to the shop and she then gave up the florin. Prisoner almost ran past the window oif the shop after going out. Clare Tilling, said she managed a confectioner's shop for her father at 113, Sankey-street. The prisoner had also passed a bad florin at her shop on Wednesday. Jessie Boeth, assistant at Mr. John Tuft's shop in Bridge-st., said the prisoner came into the shop at 7.15 on Wednesday night and bought some butter, putting 2s. 6i. on the counter. Witness gave prisoner is. 101. chaLge and put the half-crown in the detector and found that it easily bent. She then ran to the door and saw the prisoner looking up Bridge-at., and she asked her to come back to the shop as she had given a bad half-crown. She did so, and Mr. Toft asked her if she had any more money like the half-crown. She said she had not. She offered a 2s. piece and asked Mr. Toft to take pay out of that, but he would not and she then threw the butter across the counter and ran out taking the 3.8. 103 change. Mr. Toft then followed and gave her into cus- tody. She was shortly afterwards brought back by P.C. Newey. Alfred Woods, silversmith and jeweller, Sankey-st., said that en Thursday, he examined the coins produced. The half-crown was made of Brittania metal, and found that it weighed si dwts. instead of nine dwts. Two of the florins had been made out of tht," same mould and weigh si dwts. instead of seven each. The third florin which had the best impression weighed 5 dwts. and 6 grs. It had a defect of having the" v" in " Victoria " below the level similar to other counterfeit coins he had examined previously. Mary Varley Torbitt, female warder at Strangeways Goal, Manchester, said that she knew the prisoner as Bridget Merriton. She was committed for six months at the Manc'ester City Sessions on April 20, 1893, for uttering counterfeit coin. P.C. Newey stated that the prisoner was given to him in custody by Mr. Toft at 7.20 p.m. on Wednesday. The half .crown produced was also given to him. She was then carrying a bag containing a large number of articles. Be took her to the police station, and on find- ing the various bags with names of shops, he went to make inquiries. He received the florins from the three witnesses who had given their evidence. When he asked prisoner for her name, she said that she had neither name nor address. She said she was only a tramp from town to town and had stopped at Bacup the previous night. He then charged her with uttering four counter felt coins knowing them to be counterfeit. She replie- that she knew nothing about them. She had ss. 7d., in good money in her possession. Inspector Spinks produced the certificate of conviction against the prisoner for a previous case of uttering counterfeit coins. On being asked if she had anything to say, prisoner said that she came into the town with 4s. and she met a gentlemen who gave her the money produced and she did not know it was bad. She did buy the three bags and bought the rest of the articles with the change. Yrisener was committed for trial to the next Liverpool Assizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WHOLESALE POISONING IN GEItMANYI</head>
        <p>WHOLESALE POISONING IN GEItMANYI</p>
        <p>A remarkable murder trial concluded on Tuesday at Prenzlau, in Germany. Hermann Springstein, a black- smith, and his sister, Auguste Bock, were charged with the murder of the male prisoner's wife, but the evidence revealed a series of crimes of the most horrible kind. The prisoners, it was alleged, had almost annihi- lated their family. The lives of their father and mother, the male prisoner's wife and brother-in-law, and the fe- male prisoner's husband and son had been heavily in- sured, and all these persons died somewhat suddenly. Springstein had dabbled a little in veterinary surgery, and while so engaged be gained a knowledge of poisonsin his house large quantities of arsenic, strychnine,and sul- phuric acid were found ,and,according to the evidence,his wife, his parents, and his sister's husband and son had been poisoned with strychnine. A single woman named Fiebelkorn, who had incautiously declared that the elder Bpringstein had been poisoned, died herself from the effects of poison two days after she made the statement. The jury found both prisoners guilty, and they were sentenced to death.</p>
      </div>
