      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Ittttro to tot Mittor.</head>
        <p>Ittttro to tot Mittor.</p>
        <p>TILE BAD ROADS OF W ABBING (ON: A CYCLIST PROTEST. Sir.—Would you kindly allow US through the medium of your valuable paper to enter our protest against the disgraceful condition of the following three roads situ- . ated in the borough of Warrington :—viz , Liverpool Road from Sankey Bridges to Bank Quay Bridge, Wil- derspool Road from Warrington Bridge to the Brewery, and Knutsford Road from Warrington Bridge to the swing bridge over the Ship Canal at L i•chford. The National Cyclists Union (with which we have the honour to be connected) as most people are aware, have the in- terests of cyclists and cycling at heart and do their ut- most to further those interests, aud as complaints almost innumerable are continually reaching us shout the con- dition of the roads referred to, we reel justified in bring- ing before the public generally, this matter. These three roads are used by thousands of cyclists annually,and we have not yet met one who c uld give them a good name' The surface is lumpy and full ot holes, and after a shower of rain they are almost impassable for wheelmen owing to the large accumulations of mud and pools of water which remain on them. Can nothing be done to . remedy these defects? Surely the rat, s of Warrington are high enough to ensure the proper maintenance of the roads and streets. If not, it is high time that the lavish expenditure of some of the departments be reduced and the difference placed to the credit of those committees who are responsible for keeping our streets and roads in proper condition. Atiologizlng for thus utilizing your valuable space and hoping that this letter may baster the needed reforms, we rern lin Yours truly, J. NELSON, Birkenhead, (Hon. Sec. Liverpool Centre National Cyclists Union). E. PICIKAVANCE, Junr., (Hon. Sec. Roads Committee, Liverpool Centre National Cyclis,s Union.)</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED WARRINGTON CATTLE MARK ET.—A CGGESTION. Sir,—As the question of deciding upon a permanent site for a cattle market at Warrington is still under con- sideration. may I be allowed to suggest one? I have been struck with the adaptability for such a purpose of the triangular-shaped piece of gr und in Froghall-lane which is at present boarded off. A portion of it is, I be- lieve, under cultivation, and the remainder is used as a storage yard. The argument which has been urged against other suggested sites—that of the cattle market being a nuisance to the residents in the vicinity—could not be used with the same force against this one, as it is not surrounded by dwelling houses. One side is bounded by the Cheshire Lines Rail way, another by a passage running parallel with the London and North Western Railway, and the third site by Froghall-lane. Another important point in its favour is its nearness to the cattle pens of the London and North Western Rail- way, a passage from which leads to the site, and within a stone's throw, there is a wharf, known as Whitecross Wharf in cont.ection with the Great Northern, Cheshire Lines, M.S. and L., and Midland Railways, at which cattle could be conveniently landed. It would also be entirely away from the main streets of the town which is another consideration.—Yours respectfully, J. H.</p>
        <p>Whitecross Ward.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt was with feelings of considerable pleasure that the inhabitants of Orford-lane and Winwick-road heard that the Corporation of Wa.rineton had purchased an open space in their district for the purposes of a park or recreation ground. Visions of trees, fl ,wers, and other things of beauty arose before their eyes, and they imagined that they were living in a favoured spot. A short time since,however,thev experienced a mild shock for they beheld on the ground hobby horses,swing boats, ire., accompanied with loud and harsh "music," holeg highcarnival until a late hour of the night. Elowever,they did not care to mike a strong objection and felt inclined to put up with some noise and inconvenience, in view of the nice park and recreation ground that was about to be made or them. But, alas, for human hopes and Warrington Corporation pr, raises ! This park is now to be used as a cattle market ! Only" tempor- arily" it is said. Bat for how long 2 is the natural question of some of the inhabitants. This ;Ten space in Orford-lane and Winwick-road is not only one of the most thickly ropulated ones and therefore it will be most unwise and unbusiness-bke to bald it there. If the inhabitants of Wilson-Patten-street could successfully object to its being held in their neighbourhood, surely the inhabitants of VVinwick-road and Orford-lene have as much right to say it shall not be held in their midst. There is also the question of the lighting of Orford-lane. How is it that the lamps in this road are not lit until a late hour in the evening 7 I have observed that this has gone on for several weeks. Other streets can have their lamps lit at a reasonable hour, but the residents of this road are de- aied the use of a lieht until a late hour of the night. Perhaps Messrs. Wilkineon, Rogers, and others who represent these districts is the Council will have some- thing to say on these inatters..—Yours faithfully, E. ENGLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CAN WE KNOW 111 T TRUTET</head>
        <p>CAN WE KNOW 111 T TRUTET</p>
        <p>-- Sir,—May I ask your readers to patiently wade through the following lines, as the writer has given considerable thought to the subject, and has a strong feeling that it is the greatest thing in the world. Long years ago, I came across a copy of Page Hopp's " Truthseeker,' and from it I soon learned how to get hold of a mass of freethought literature.the end of it all being the making of a strong supporter of the late Mr. Bradlaugh. Twenty years ago it was no light matter to be known as an atheist, or infidel in such a Conservative town as War- rington. Papers that were" Neutral in all things reli- gious and political " closed the door completely against anything that touched in the least upon religious con- troversy, and even a Radical paper like our own " Examiner " did a light tremble when a criticism of the good old book was attempted. A change has come over the spirit of our dreams, editors have bet orne liberal in their views, and not so anxious to please the few narrow minded ones among their readers. This is as it should be,and while it harms no one it affords an opportunity to the public, and especially our dear friends Mr. John Peers and Henry Esmond, to draw out once now and again the gushing admirers of "our dear curate." Still, chaff, nor yet violent iconoclasm, does not land us any nearer to the truth,or enable us to solve the old query of "whence, and whither," and we keem on beating around the still older question, "can man by searching find out God? " Are we nearer the answer to-day then we were a hundred years ago, or fifty years ago? I think it possible that we shall never be able to find out God, and that we shall also have to remain in ignorance as to the location and composition of the soul,and its immortality. But admitting these difficulties as among the insur- mountable, are we not justified in callin4 upon the world known as " Christian" to be at least honest, and confess at once that the Bible is not the inspired word of God, and does not give us a true, nor yet a reasonable account of the genesis of man, nor yet even a plausible description of the "life after death." I think it fair to charge upon theologians unfair dealings with their con- gregations, and with the English language. Prof. Bonney, Canon of Manche•ter, said at the re- cently held Church Congress, " I cannot deny that the increase of scientific knowledge has deprived parts of the earlier books of the Bible of the historic value which was generally attributed to them by our forefathers. The story of creation in Genesis, unless we play fast and leose either with words or with sciencc, cannot be brought into harmony with what we have learned from geology. The stories of the find and of the Tower of Babel are incredible in their present form ? " Pro- fessor Drummond in his book, "The Ascent of Man," says, "It is certain th t the materials for man's body have been brought together from an un- known multitude of lowlier forms of life," and that "the ultimate origin of mind is as inscrutable a mystery as the origin of life." If we do not play fast and loose with our language,what do these words mean ? Simply this, that the biarden of Eden episode is all a legend and that a God did not create &amp;dam out of dust, nor Eve out of a rib, consequently no serpent did any talking, nor any be- guiling. No curse was ever uttered, nor yet any atone- ment foretold. We must go even further than th]s, and insist upon it as Christian men and not " Iconoclasts have knocked away the foundstions of Christianity, the whole of the superstructure must go along with it. If ministers of all religions could step (foam from their pulpit., could leave the select circles they move in, and for a time mingle with the myriads who fill our factories, mills, and mines, their eves would be opened and their ears shocked at the opinions held and in currency among the masses. It is a pitiful true statement that the Chris- tian's religion is not accepted, nor yet is the minister's honestly above suspicion. The charge is micle that social position, loaves and flesh and an indolent life are pre- ferred to an active worlling life devoted to the uplifting of the common rabble. The people generally do not any longer look to minis- ters of religion for light and leading. It by no means follows that infidelity is gaining ground among us. I believe otherwise. While the number of the indifferent Is increasing, it is still true that the hope of immortality Is arranging itsel lin order in the evolutionist's philosophy. While the mind of man cannot think or fashion a treat ruler, active or passive, it can still long for a religion without the supernatural and a theology that does not depend upon the personification of good—God ; Devil— evil, for its fundamentals —Yours truly. COLIN CLOUT. 249, Mission-st., San Franc., Cal.</p>
        <p>--••• e EXAMINATION FOR LABOUR CERTIFICATES AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. These examinations were rontinuerl in Wycliffe School on Saturday. when 262 children presented themselves, namely, standard three, four; standard four. 45; and standard live, 213.</p>
