      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>EARLESTO WN AN D NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTO WN AN D NEWTON.</p>
        <p>SONS OF TEMPEUANCB.</p>
        <p>The usual .meeting in conneetiun .with the Samuel</p>
        <p>Morley Section of Cadets was held on Wednesday evening in the Co-operative Hall, Bar,lestown, Bro. A. Smith in the chair. At 8 o'clock the Britain's Need Division met in the same room, Bro. C. Bros- ter, W.P., in the ct,air. After opening, one member was initiated The Secretary reported that he had received the interest from the Earlestowu Building Society which i was ordered to be divided between the Benevolent Fund and Management Fund. The meeting closed at 9.15.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning d well-known shop-keeper of High-street, Newton, named Robert Knox died very suddenly after a short illness. The deceased retired to rest on Saturday night apparently in good health, but during the night became ill, and Dr. J. W. Watkins and Dr. Harold Watkins were summoned. Despite their attentions, however' he died at 7.30 on Mondiiy morning, death being due to apoplexy. iNDRPRNDRNT LABOUR PARTY (EARLESTOWN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Busxciat).</head>
        <p>Busxciat).</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the above a lecture was given in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown on Sat- urday evening by Miss Caroline E. D. Martyn, of Lincoln. There was a fair attendance, and the lecturer's remarks which were intently listened to were founded on the social teaching of Jesus. The lecture was most interesting and Miss Martyn sought to demonstrate that (Jurist was a social re- form who did not confine his actions to the good of the soul without the body.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GUY FAWKES D.LY,</head>
        <p>GUY FAWKES D.LY,</p>
        <p>The successful ceiebratiou of the sth of Novem- ber was this year altogether marred by the heavy downfall of rain, and the otherwise boisterous weather which prevailed on Tuesday night, At- tempts were made in several parts of the insilict to kindle bonfires, but they were only of a half hearted character, the rain playing sad havoc with the flames. The youngsters however managed to eke out some small measure of enjoyment in spite of the rain, and here and there could be seen miniature pyrotechnic displays. Up to the time of writing we have not heard of any mishaps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>SffOoTING MATCH AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>SffOoTING MATCH AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday at the iligtdieid Moss range, a shoot- ing match took place between the members of the •• (Newton) troops of Lancashire Hussars, and and the Newton detachment of the 6th V.B. K.L.R. of volunteers. The challenge emanated from the Hussars, and the conditions were 10 shots each. After five shots the carbines of the hussars were taken over by the volunteers, and the latter's rifle,, handled by the hussars. The volunteers came out ' victorious by 40 points. The contestants afterwards adjourned to the Pied Bull hotel where they sat Idown to a substantial knife and fork tea, and a pleasant evening was spent in a social manner. SUDDEN DEATH IN A WAGGONETTE. _ _</p>
        <p>On Friday evening Nirs. Johnson, of Boston Cottages, Haydock, suddenly expired while riding in a waggonette. Deceased, in company with her husband, left Haydock in one of the waggonettes which run to Earlestown. She complained of feel- ing unwell, and on the advice of her husband remained seared in the vehicle on its arrival at Earlestown. She gradually became worse, and Dr. Valentine was summoned. After prescribing for her, he ordered her to be conveyed home at once, but on the way it was found that she had breathed her last, death being due to apoplexy. ROBBERY FROM THE TILL AT EARLEEITOWN.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Bailee Station on Monday morning, before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, John Smith alias Thomas Holden of Farnworth. near 8.,:t0n, was charged with stealing three shillings from a till in the smallware shop of Mr. S. Harvey, 9, Bridge-st., Earlestown, about nine o'clock that morning. Mrs. Barvey had left the shop for a few minutes, and Mr. Harvey coming in while she was out caught the prisoner behind the counter, and on being asked what he was doing there said, he was looking for his cap which had slipped off the coun- ter. He handed 3s. to Mr. Harvey, and begged to be let off, but he was given into the custody of the police. He was remanded until Saturday next. DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday morning, before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Jane Whit- taker ("Bonnie Lady") of no fixed residence, was ' charged with being drunk in Grafton-at. on Satur- day night, and in default of payment of a fine of 2s. 6d. and costs was sent to gaol for seven days.— William Jones of no fixed residence for being drunk in Grafton-et., on Saturday night, was fined 2. 6d. *DA costs, or seven days.—Thotuas Davies of New- ton Common for being drunk and incapable in Market-st.' was fined 28.61. and costs.—At the Police Station on Tuesday morning before Mr.J.W.Emmett, Henry Mortis, a travelling showman, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Earlestown the previous day, and was fined 21. 6,1. and costs. EARLESTOWN CONSERVATIVE _CLUB.</p>
        <p>The first of the winter series of smoking concerts promoted by the Committee of the above club Wile held in the large billiard room rm Friday evening. Mr. C. B. F. Borron J.P. presided and there was a large attendance of members. The following pro- gramme of songs Stc.,w as sufficiently rendered,glee: "The Huntsman's farewell." Wargrave glee party ; song" Red. white and blue," Mr. G. Wright ; whistl- ing solo. "The lost chord," Mr. J. Howard; clarionet solo; "house, sweet home." Mr. T. Jukes; recitation. "The dying soldier," Mr. W. Sutton; glee " The Soldier's chorus," Wargrave glee party ; song" Ay Sentinel am I," Mr. W. Kay ; humorous song " Mr. lluddleston, clarirmet solo Mr. Jukes, song and chorus" Sweet Marie," Mr. C. H. Lea, song" Blow high,blow low," Mr. G. Wright; whistling solo" Kil- larney," Mr. J. Howard; song "The skipper" Mr. W. Kay; humorous song, Mr. Huddleston ; glee " Ex- celsior:" Wargrave glee party. Mr. E. Leyland acnmpanied.</p>
        <p>1.0 G.T. (EBENRZICR LODGE). The usual weekly session of the Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge was held in the Co-operative Hall, on Tuesday evening. Bro. W. Hatton, C.T.,presided. The sick committee gave their report,and the mem- bers were pleased to hear that Bro. T. Dean and Bro. Dickens were improving rapidly. A good number of candidates were then proposed for in rn bership. The election and installation of officers was proceeded with and resulted as follows :—CT., Bro. W. Hatton; V T.,Sister Mrs. Taylor : secretary, A. Morris; F.sec.,S.Williams ; treasurer, VV. J Taylor; chap.,Sister Mrs. Woods ; sent. George Taylor J. Jones; P.C.T.,W. Brown ; D.M ,Sister Mrs. Owen ; assistant sec., T.Topping. Afterwards a new set of rituals and regalias was presented to the lodge on behalf of four members, who were thanked by the lodge. The report of the retiring officers showed that the past quarter had been a success numeri- cally and financially, and it was hoped that by a little more energy still larger success would be se- cured this next quarter.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLAILS. The quarterly session of the Hope of Earlestown lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Monday evening. Bro. J. liattork pre- sided, and there was a large attendance of members. Several candidates were proposed tor membership. The digest of business for No. 1 Convention, was read and adopted. The report of the District Juvenile Council of Southport was given by Bro. E. Newman. The question of a Christmas carol party was referred to a Committee. The reports of the various officers were received. Bro. Barker, secre- tary, in his report, showed that there were 150 members in good standing, being an increase on the quarter. Several public meetings had been held, including No. 1 Convention. The lodge anniversary was successful, as also had been the open sessions at which cood programmes were given. He urged more active work. Bro. Holliday, treasurer, re- ported the finances in a good position with balance in hand. The officers for the ensuing quarter were then installed: Bro. Q.M. Sergeant Roberts, CC T. of Warrington, who gave an encouraging address, assisted by Bro. J. T. Singleton and Sister Mrs. Singleton, as follows :—C.T. Bro. J. Hatton ; V.T. Sister Miss Ord ; Sec. Bro. Barker; F. Sec. Bro. An- derton ; Treas. Bro: Holliday, Chap. Bro. C. Drew; Sfarshall,Bro. J. Hopkins; A. Sec. Sister Parker. An address was afterwards given by Bro. J. Miller. An interesting programme of songs, &amp;c., was also given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>SOCIAL ETENING AT EARLIISTOWIC._</head>
        <p>SOCIAL ETENING AT EARLIISTOWIC._</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening in connection with the Primitive Methodist branch of the Young People's Sniety of Christian Endeavour a social evening was spent in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom, Earlestown. The Rev. E. R. Davies presided and gave an address bearing chiefly on the work of the society. Songs and other musical selections were given by several of the members present, after which supper was partaken of, 44 members sitting down to an enjoyable repast. The evening was wound up with games of various kinds, a very pleasant time being spent. OITA—GARLAND, Importer, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEY GUILD. The weekly meeting of this guild was held in the \ Lecture Mall of the Wesleyan Chapel, Earlestown, on Wednesday evening. The Rev. ..T: Bartley pre- sided,th e programme being "Gentlemen's Evening," the idea being that the gentlemen should entertain theilady members. The room was decorated. to an ex- tent sufficient to enliven the proceedings, which consisted of a variety of table games, interspersed with vocal and instrumental selections by Messrs. W. T. and F. P. Clare, Mr. A. Howarth, and Mr. H. Taylor. Refreshments were afterwards ptovided, a very pleasant evening being spew.. A vote of thanks was accorded the entertainers,on the motion of Mrs. Battersby, to which Mr. T. Torrauce res- ponded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 9, 1895</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 9, 1895</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>MEEFING AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting in connection with the Wesleyan Foreign Missionary Society, was held in the Wesleyan Chapel, Earlestown, on Monday evening. Mr. James Moon, of Liverpool, presided, and he was supported by the Revs. J. Bartley, H. S. Sandford and A. Triggs, Missionary of Kandy, Ceylon. After the usual opening the Chairman called upon Mr. Parr (secretary) to read the report which gave a brief outline of the missionary work abroad. In the connex- ions under the immediate direction of the Wesleyan ' Committee, there were, central and principal stations I 328. missionaries and assistants 349, other paid agents 2,537, unpaid agents 5,207, full and accredited church members 41,000. on trial for membership 9,100, scholars 80,791, contributions £7,309 19s. Bd., of this sum St. Helens and Prescet circuits raised by subscriptien and collections £l3O BA., Juvenile Missionary Association and Chr litinas offerings, £79 9.+. 2d. The Earlestown breach of the Juvenile Missionary Society contributed £6O, one third ot which was devoted to Foreign Missions. The Chairman said he could truly say that it afforded him very great pleasure to visit Earlestown once again on such a peacetul mission as that which brought them tegether that night, and to spend an evening with his fellow worshippers of Earlestown. There were some things that the best of Methodists might have a differ- ence of opinion about,and rightly so, because Methodism was broad enough for it. But he did not think there could be two opinions about Foreign Missions. In these days he was afraid there were a considerable number of people who considered it the right thing to speak slight- ingly of much earnest work and effort ; to be enthusias- tic about anything, was, he was afraid, to incur their: smiles and sometimes to be called faddists, and to be I enthusiastic about Foreign Missions when there was so ' much scope for mission work at home, was to them an anomaly. Not that they supported home mission work themselves, because he was afraid they had not enthus- iasm enough for that, or to help anything but their feeble selves, and his observation had led him to the con- eu,ion that the very people who helped Foreign Missions e. the very people who supported Home Missions— (hear, hear)—and not only support Home Missions, 1)4 have to support them in addition to maintaining their own church organisations at their own expense. There were very few people in this country could complain that the gospel of Christianity had not been offered to them, because in this country he did not think there was any class which had not had it offered to them, but unfortunately the stumbling block was not the lack of opportunity, for they had rejected it when it had been offered to them,and he did not think that was any reason why they should not put an effort forth in order to send it to the heathen who would ac- cept it. (Hear, hear.) In sending it to the heathen, they were not simply sending the gospel and nothing be- side. They had abroad in every part of the world, Hospital and Medical Missions to relieve the suffering. Ihey had a large bomber of Homes for boys and girls, and he saw by the report there were some 60,000 or 70,000 boys and girls who were receiving training and boarding at Industrial Schools, so that they were not only sending them the gospel,but showing them that the gospel was practical. If they all acted more up to the teachings of the gospel the degradation and misery of this country would diminish by leaps and bounds, and it was not the fault of the churches that there was so much at home,and in his opinion the church was doing a neble work in sending the gospel into foreign lands, and most of them were aware that it was only some thousand years ago that their own country was steeped in heathenism, and missionaries from Rome and Ireland came over and travelled the country in order to convert the people to Christianity. The Rev. H. S. Sandford and the Rev. Arthur Triggs also spoke, dealing with mission work in Ceylon, their remarks being exceedingly interesting. During the evening solos were sung by Miss L. Barr, Migi Ethel Collinge, and others, and votes of thanks to the speakers concluded the meeting. On Sunday the annual sermons in connection with the mission were preached by the Rev. Arthur Triggs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO MR. S. HUNT, THE CHIEF CONSTABLE.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. S. HUNT, THE CHIEF CONSTABLE.</p>
        <p>On Friday, the Mayor presented Mr. Samuel Hunt,the present head constable of the borough, with the cheque for £5O passed to him by the Corporation on his retire- ment. The Mayor referred to Mr. Hunt's long connec- tion with the town as head constable, and spoke of the high esteem in which he is held by all classes of the com- munity. He expressed a hope that he would be spared for many years to spend the remainder of his life in quietness and happiness. All the aldermen were present with the exception of Alderman Holmes, and they en- dorsed what his Worship had said with regard to Mr. Hunt, who sooke at great length, contrasting the town as it was when he first came to it with what it was now. In his opinion, it was, on the whole, one of the "best- conducted and law-abiding towns" in the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NEW HEAD CONSTABLE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW HEAD CONSTABLE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Talbot, the new head constable of the borough, and who will shortly enter upon his duties in Warring- ton, was the other evening presented by the police officers of Kendal with a portmanteau, as a mark of the respect in whicn he is held there. Sergeant Kitchen spoke in very complimentary terms of Mr. Talbot, who, he said had always been ready and willing to give good advice in all cases of difficulty. fle was very kind- hearted in eatery respect, giving great privileges to t'he men serving under him, and the advantages cf partici- pating in athletic pursuits, which they had very much enjlved. He was sure all the police greatly felt his de- parture, and they all joined in wishing him and Mrs. Talbot health, strength, and happiness in their new undertaking.</p>
        <p>Mr. TALBOT, in reply,remarked that when he informed the Watch Committee of his resignation, he told them that one of the most poignant regrets in leaving the old town in which he had been so happy was that he could not take his police force with him. He had served in the Ilradford, Lancaster.and Kendal forces over a period of 16 years, and he never came across a set of men who knew their duties more thoroughly, and did them better or more willingly than the Kendal force. They were smart,and h defied anyone to take 14 officers at random from any large police force who could surpass them in the knowledge of their duties and the manner in which they executed them. Five and a half years ago' when he came to the town,he was not much struck with it,but he could assure them he was leaving it like a man who left his home. He was not struck with the police force at first,but he was not long in finding it was surrounded with loyal and kind hearts,and men who were treated like men were willing to act like men ; and so he had always found them. When he addressed them the first time he expressed the hope that he might some day call every member of the force his friend. He believed that night he could do so. Tnere was not a member of the force in whom he did not take an interest—also in his family and his home. (Hear, hear ) That was a very nice feeling to have,and be felt in going away from them he was leaving a number of frlends. He considered he owed them all something for the loyal way in which they had rallied round him whenever they had had any- thing to do. At the last election they went about their duties patiently, willingly, bravely, and well—he never saw men do better. The rank and file were all that could be desired. Of Sergeants Winn and Kitchen he cold say that two better sergeants did not exist in any force. They were lost in a small town, and he only wished they had had the ambition at the proper time, and done what he had done, sought fresh fields and pas- tures new. With regard to their handsome present, he had not had much time to examine it, but he could say they had given him the bag in earnest. (Laughter.) Whenever he and the portmanteau travelled together he would ever remember the kind and loyal hearts he hal left behind in the Kendal police force. (Applause). Other compliments having been exchanged the gather- ing separated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>.4p. THE UNITED STATES ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>.4p. THE UNITED STATES ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>REPURLICAN VICTORIES.</p>
        <p>The result of Tuesday's elections in 11 States was a great victory for the Republicans, who have carried all the doubtful States with sweeping majorities, held their own with increased strength in the Republican States, and materially reduced the DemGcratic majlrity in the States where the ascendency of that party was generally regarded as certain. In Now York the Republican majority for State officers is 89,000, and they have elected dearly four-fifths of the State Senate, and more than two-thirds of the Assembly. Maryland, generally a Democratic State has elected a Republican Governor and Legislature, and there has been a similar result in Ren- tucky. In New Jersey the probabilities were thought to be in favour of the Democrats, but the Republicans have won wich large majorities. In New York City the Tam- many candidates have been elected, and a New York correspondent says it is ths German vote which has given them their victory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <p>..----.....-,--.</p>
        <p>HOUSEHOLD LINENS. hemmed and marked free of chair —3 an W. Durrov, iankey-st,.. Warrineon.--Advt. SEWING MACIII!kIES Hand 22 10s. Treadle £4 45 with handaome wAlnut coven—J. and W. DurroN, 16 and 18, Baakey-st., Warrington.—.Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>(BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR).</p>
        <p>When recording the doings of the last monthly meetiag of the District Council, I alluded to the fact that the councillors had gone in for late sitt- ings, having on that occasion sat and deliberated tor over two hours. This month I have to go on another tack, as on Monday the councillers pro- duced a record, having sat for the shortest period known, viz., 23 minutes. Now that is what some people call business, but I am afraid they can do very little in such a remarkably short period. Fancy, 23 minutes to transact the business of a ' district like Newton-in-Makerfield, but then it must be remembered that practically all the work is done in committeP, and the Council have only to confirm the resolutions of the committee. Still it does not give the aspiring orators their usual prac- tice, and the election day is fast approaching.</p>
        <p>An unusual fact has also on this occasion to be chronicled. The Law-Clerk was absent, and accoid- ing to all accounts this is only the third time be has been away from the monthly meeting for 20 years. This is a record, and lam inclined to think it cannot be beaten. It is evident the Law-Clerk takes an interest in his work, and he deserves every credit for it.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer's report was submitted,and to far as can be judged the health of the district is fairly good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRE DIVISION NEEDLEWORK GUILD.</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE DIVISION NEEDLEWORK GUILD.</p>
        <p>The seventh annual exhibition of clothing under the auspices of the South Lancashire Needlework Guild was held in the Town Hall, Newton-le-Willows, on Friday atternoon. The room was packed with a profusion of useful articles of apparel of all descriptions,and a couple of garments made by H.R.H. the Duchess of York ex- cited a fair amount of interest. Mrs. Clare Royse, presi- dent. gave an address on "The Progress of the Guild," the Rev. H. Monk, vicar of Newton, and Mr. Be Tayl4r, police court missionary, being also present. Mrs. Royse, in the course of her address, mentioned that the South Lancashire Needlework Guild was started at Newton in 1888, having for its first president its ever lamented foundress, Lady Wolverton. Since her death the Guild had the very high compliment paid it by H.R.H.the Duchess of York becoming their general pre- sident. From H.R.H. Mrs. Royse had received a letter, in which she expressed pleasure in reading the last re- port of the excellent work done by the branch, and she hoped the efforts of the vice-presidents and associates would be no less hearty in the future. FI.R.H. noted with particular satisfaction the large proportion of clothes sent to the different hospitals, orphanages, and homes in the county. Such a destination was one of the objects of the Guild, as it enabled all who were willing to devote a little time and trouble to contribute to the wants of those who were unable to help themselves. H.R.H. was also glad to see there were several juvenile vice-presidents and associates, and thought it would be a very good thing if the number of these could be. increased. In conclusion, she urged the vice-presidents not to despair or lose hart in the Guild when members fell off, as she knew, was apt to be the case, but to let it increase their de- termination not to diminish their exertions to keep up the Guild to its present excellent standard. H.R.H.would send to Mrs. Royse in a few days two petticoats made by herself for the exhibition. To prove the special in- terest which as president she took in the South Lanca- shire branch of the Needlework Guild. (Applause.) Mrs. Royse, continuing her address, dealt with the objects of the Guild,and pointed out that it was not a substitute for such commendable institutions as clothing clubs,mothers meetings, sewing classes, Dorcas club, &amp;c., which were desirable in every parish, but was a supplement to such 'parochial efforts. Dealing with statistics, she said the number of articles received this year was 3,217, against 3,137 last year, the juvenile vice-presidents and commit- tee of distribution having sent 215 and 207 in the respec- tive years. In conclusion, she moved a vote of thanks to the vice-presidents,and the committee of distribution and also to Mr. G. Clarke, the hon. auditor. (Applause.) The Rev. H. Monk seconded,and the vote was heartily passed.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Taylor said in his capacity as police court mis- sionary he was brought into contact with the necessity for work of that kind. There was a tremendous amount of poverty and distress in the district. He had been able to adserve the amount of good done by the Guild in sending gifts to the various institutions, and was glad to notice that articles were sent to the various institutions in Liverpool in which he had a special interest. He trusted the associates would persevere. (Hear. hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held at the Earleatown Hall on Monday, when Mr. C. B. F. Borrou presided, and there were also present M“ssr.i. J. w. Emmett, P. MacMahon J. W. Gilmour, R. Stone, J. Simpson, .1. Smith, W. Thompson, jun., J. Slee, T. Ormand, W. Beattv,E.l. Evans, F. J. Woocl,R. Brierley (clerk and survevor).</p>
        <p>ABSENCE OF THE LAW CLERK. The Chairman read a letter from Mr. Worsley,the law clerk,whichlstated that he would be unable to be present owing to the death of his sister,Mrs. Venables Williams. The Chairman said that that was only the third time the clerk had been absent since he had been clerk to the commissioners. He had held that positien for a long period, and he (the Chairman) very much regretted the cause of the cl rk's absence. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>TILE READING OF THEO ALINUTES. The Chairman said that it was a customary thing to take the minutes as read, and he therefore moved that they be taken as read. If anybody objected to that course he would have the minutes read.—Mr. Gilmour said that be would rather have tho minutes taken separ• ately.—The Chairman said that his motion was simply to take the minutes as read. Discussion on the minutes would be allowed when the adoption on the minutes was meyed.—Mr.Thomoson seconded the motion which was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>HIGHWAY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>HIGHWAY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith moved and Mr. Stone seconded the adop- ticia of the minutes of this committee which appeared in our last issue.—Mr. Gilmour asked the surveyor why the minutes with regard to the paving of Nelson-street were not on the minutes.- Fle thought that it looked like bad management to take and level the street and then pave it again.—Mr. Brierley said that he gave an answer to Mr. Gilmour in committee and he could not give any further answer.—Mr. Gilmour rose again, but the Chairman ruled him out of order, and the minutes were then passed.—Mr. Ormand then explained that Mrs. Ratcliffe wished some alteration to be made in the street and said that she w.s willing to pay for it herself. He thought it only right that it should be recorded on the minutes so that the puhlic shoold know that it was not they, but Mrs. Ratcliffe was paving for it —Mr. Gilmour expressed himself as being satisfied with the ex- planation. GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.—A " BREEZE."</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith moved the adoption of the minutes which appeared in our last issue. He said that with reference to the resolution for fixing gas mains and lamps in different dis*ricts, he took it that the committee in- tended that the work should not be executed immedi- ately. That would he quite impossible. They would have to go steadily with the work and not rush it by any means. He did not think that it would benefit the rate- pavers aenerally if it were rushed at all. He had no objection to the resolution being c-,nfirmed with that ex- planation.—Mr. Evans seconded and asked whether the work would be done in the order it was put down in the minutes.—Mr. Smith : I think so.—Mr. Gilmour</p>
        <p>thought that the lamps sheuld be lit in the early morn- ing as well as at night.—Mr.Emmett said that m acad a m had been put down to such an extent that foot passengers could not walk on the road. He wished to know whether the steam rollers went over it.—The Chairman : This question should have been asked on the minutes of the Highways Committee.—Mr. Emmett : I see that it ought,but I should like an answer.—Mr. Brierley : Then is a path left en either side.--;Several questions were then asked about the lighting of certain streets, he answers to which did not satisfy Mr. Gilmour who said that the minutes relating to them had not been passed in committee.—Mr. Smith : I beg your pardon, they were.—Mr. Thompson : They were all passed.—Mr. Gilmour : Why are not the mover and seconder's names put upon the minutes. We should know who does these things.—The Chairman: That is a matter of detail.— The minutes were carried after which Mr. Gilmour de- manded an answer to his question.—The Chairman : You are out of order, Mr. Gilmour.—Mr. Gilmour: I am not out of order. It is very easy to shout " Chair, chair, chair." Give me proof that! am out of order. The Chairman: You are out of order, and you must sit down.—Mr. Gilmour : I protest against business being done in this manner.—The Chairman: I call upon the 11 chairman of the Finance Committee to move the minutes of that committee.—Mr. Gilmour: All right. If you are so stupid, you must have it in another way. (Cries of " order, order.")</p>
        <p>PINANCH Cousurrms. The minutes of this Committee were passed unanim- mush,, on the motion of Mr. Outland, seconded by Mr. McMahon. SCHOOL ATTENDANCH AND TECHNICAL INSTRDCTION COMMITTEE. The minutes of these committees appeared in our last issue, and were Passed unanimously, the former on the motion of Mr. Wood, seconded by Mr. Gilmour' and the latter on the motion of Mr. Wood seconded by Mr. Thompson. Nem); OF' MOTION. Mr. Gilmour : I give notice that at the next meeting of the Council, I shall ask for the mover and seconder of each resolution in committee to be placed upon the minutes.</p>
        <p>The proceedines then terminated haying only occupied 23 minutes. This was the shortest meetine which has been held since the formation of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RITGBY FOOTBALL. Warrington and Wigan have tought many battles together, and formerly the Warrington team was almost as well-known in Wigan as the Wigan team. On Satur- day, however, which was the date of the first meeting this seasoci, there was scarcely a man among the War • ringtonians recognisable by the Wiganers. There was no player with the exception of Heesom who had played with the first team at Wigan more than once before. Many were the remarks and questions of the spectators about the missing laces in the Warrington team." " Wuere was Turner, Smack Bate, Da.kin, and the rest of them ?" Burton was the only one who was remembered from last year. Notwithstanding the altered team, a good game resulted, and it was not until about a quarter of an boar before the finish that the Wiganers really overpowered Warrington, with their powerful forwards.</p>
        <p>Wigan were without doub.i, tue best team on the day's play, but this was simply on account of the superior strength of the forwards. The Warrington halves and three-quarters were much superior to Wigan, but Winstanley kept up his reputation as a full back,and his kicks were something tremendous. Had he been pressed iike Heesom, he might not have come off with such fly- ing colours. Heesom, though not in the best of health, defended manfully, and considering the large amount ot work he had to get through, he came through the ordeal well.</p>
        <p>Among the forwards Saunders, Wilcock, Thornton, and Fairhurst were very prominent, but the whole body of forwards were DO match in strength for the Wiganers, and unfortunately the latter were in their very best form on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Foden played a splendid game at half back, and re- minded one of Smack Bate. One bit of play in particu- lar was very fine and deserved a score. He obtained the ball from the scrimmage in midfield and Walkden, the opposing half back at once rushed to tackle bim. He, however, pretended to pass to Broady, at the same time giving the ball a knock. This knock made Walkden think that Broady and aot the ball, and he made a rush at him. Foden had however got the ball safe enough, and before he had to pass to Broady he was beyond the quarter flag. This effort was very,deservedly applauded by the spectators. Houghton was not so bkilliant as Fodian, but he got through a good deal of work, his picking up being very clean.</p>
        <p>Burton and Cross were the most prominent three- quarters, the two wings, Barber and Broady being too well looked after. Barber had once an excellent chance of scoring,for he had passed everyone but the full back, when he unfortunately slipped ,and thus the opportunity was lost.</p>
        <p>Wigan had quite reconstructed their team on Satur- day, owing to the former team being so partial to draws. One of their threequarters, Walkden was put half back, and their wing threequarters were placed centre. After his play on Saturday, it will be strange if Flowers is again placed centre, as he WAS a complete failure. Walkden's place at threequarter was taken by Wilding, late of Wales, Leigh, and Bradford. He is therefore a rolling stone, but as he scored a try on Saturday, and this was the first of the match, and also played a good game, Wigan will probably keep kina in their team until he wishes to go somewhere else.</p>
        <p>Leeds, 0130 of the leading Yorkshire teams, will be suet to-day, and as this is a home fixture a win ought to crown the efforts of Warrington. Leeds are not doing very well in the competition and last Saturday, they bad to suffer defeat by 15 points to nil at the ha rids of Hali- fax. They are however a team of surprises and come off sometimes where they are expected to be beaten. They are not to be trifled with, for now, since the ruling of the N.C.A.A., with rsgard to the position of players who do net accept broken pay, they have their two cracks, Summersgill and Walker, playing with them again. Summersgill is not playing in his usual place of three- quarters to-day, however, as he will be half back. Leeds have a good team to meet Warrington, and there ought to be a good gate, provided the weather is favourable.</p>
        <p>The following is the team selected to do duty to War- rington: Heesom, full back; Barber, Cross, Burton, and Broady, three-quarters ; Foden and Houghton, half backs : Nevins, Burrows, Saunders, Thornton, Hill, Andrews, Fairhurst, and Wilcock, forwards.</p>
        <p>The matches on Saturday were more decisively in fav- our of the home teams, than they were in favour of the visiting teams a week previously. There was one pointless draw, neither Broughton Rangers nor Stockpert being able to get through the defence of the other. St. Helens also for the fifth time in their last six matches with Leigh, played a draw. It is very singular how St. Helens and Leigh are so unable to beat each other. St. Helens have done the trick ogee, out of six matches, but Leigh's turn has yet to come.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday, Bradford were the only team to be I beaten at borne, just as by a remarkable coincidence, the previous Saturday they were the only team to lose a way. They had the worstof luck on Saturday however for they pressed Batley almost the whole of the game and yet could only scores try, while at the last moment of th match, Batley by a sudd6n rush got over and landed a goal. This is very hard on Bradford.</p>
        <p>Brighouse Rangers who are the leaders of the competi- tion, failed a: Hull on Saturday, and were beaten by a goal to nil. This is the second time they have been beaten this season, and as they hardly ever have won at Hull, they will not be very much surprised especially considering the improved play of the latter.</p>
        <p>Halifax have now the best record in the league for they ,have only dropped four points, and by their deci- sive victory over Leeds on Saturday they have further established their position. Widnes and Runcorn are very near to each other geographically, hut on the League table they are very far apart. Runcorn beat Hulders- ti•3ld on Saturday by 11 points to nil and are now fourth on the table, while Widnes suffered defeat at the hands of Tyldesley by three tries to nil,and are nineteenth.</p>
        <p>Rochdale Hornets in their return match with Liver. sedge were beaten by 10 "points to 3, and are the first to have more than a hundred points scored against them Liversedge on the other hand have almost scored 100 points. Oldham polished off Manningham on Saturday in the most approved style,and scored a dropped goal, a penalty goal, and a try against them. Wakefield added one more to their long list of defeats and have now only one point less than the century scored against them.</p>
        <p>It will be interesting to know which team will be the first to score their 100 points. Oldham are the nearest with 96, while Bradford have scored 93, Livorsedge 92, and Runcorn 89. The feat ought to be accomplished to-day by one or other of them.</p>
        <p>The following is the present position of the Northern League table :</p>
        <p>~.. Score. Score. Pts. Pld. Won. Lost. Dm. For. Ag'ist. Br'ghouse Ran. 10 ... 7 ... 2 ... 1 ... 56 ... 11 ... IS Bradford ll .. 7 ... 3 ... 1 ... 93 ... 39 ... 15 Halifax Runcorn.... Liversedge lO ... 6 ... 3 ... 1 ... 92 ... 37 ... 13 Leigh St. Helens Oldham Wigan lO 4 2 4 36 ... 21 ... 12 V anningham 10 ... 6 ... 4 .., 0 ... 53 ... 49 ... 12 Hull Leeds ... .. . _</p>
        <p>. . ... - ... ... ... ... Tyldesley Hunslet Batley lO 3 5 2 35 60 8 Warrington Stockpoit Br'ght'n Ran lO ... 2 ... 6 ... 2 ... 30 ... 48 ... 6 Widnes Huddersfield lO ... 2 ... 8 ... 0 ... 36 ... BO ... 4 Wakefield T lO ... 2 ... 8 ... 0 ... 22 ... 99 ... 4 Rochdale H lO ... 1 ... 7 ... 2 ... 19 ...106 ... 4</p>
        <p>The Lancashire and Cheshire section is as follows : Score score Pts Pld. Won. Lost. Drn. For. Ag'st. Runcorn St. Helens 7 4 1 ... 2 ... 30 ... 18 ... 10 Wigan Tyldesley Leigh Stockport Rochdale H 5 ... 1 ... 2 ... 2 ... 9 ... 38 ... 4 Warrington Widnes ... ... 3 Oldham 2 1 ... 1 ... 0 ... 16 ... 11 ... 2 B'g'ht'n Ran 7 ... 0 ... 5 ... 2 ... 7 ... 34 ... 2</p>
