        <p>HALTON. Tan Wystav GUILD—In connection with this guild,Mr. F. J.Norman J.P. of Runcorn, delivered a very interesting lecture, in the Wesleyan Church on Tuesday night, on the subject of " Rome past and present." The lecture was illustrated by limelight views, the lantern being manipulated by Mr. G. H. Pickthall. In spite of the inclement weather, there was a large audience, who thoroughly appreciated the lecture. MISSIONARY SERVICE OF Sow.—On Sunday afternoon, a'service of song entitled " Missions," was admirably rendered by a choir of boys and girls, in the Wesleyan church. The reader was Mr. Gamon. Mr. Moors con- ducted, and Miss Boys played the organ. There was a good congregation, and a splendid collection was taken ' on behalf of Foreign Missions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRADES' COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES' COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The twentieth annual meeting of the Warrington Trades' Council was held at the Wagon and Horses Inn, Buttermarket at., • n Tuesday evening. Mr. T. Malkin, president, occupied the chair, and the other officers pre- sent were Mr. Battersby (vice-president), Mr.W. Bullock (secretary), and Mr. ()Aden (treasurer). PRESIDENT'S ADDRESS. The PIZESIDBNT, in his annual address thanked the members for the high honour they had conferred upon him by electing him their president for the past year,and he hoped that the coming year would be productive of much goad, and that the conduct of themselves as dele- gates would afford another evidence of the judgment and commonsense exercised by workmen in discussing questions of importance, whether of trade, or with p li- tics. During the past few years the cause of labour had made such progress that even their most sympathetic friends had looked with amazement at the gigantic strides which it had made. Twenty years ago the labour question was regarded of little importance, but the workers were becoming more keenly alive to their interests, and to the benefits which could be obtained by trades unionism. The principles of combination were being seized upon by nearly all the workers bcth skilled and unskilled. The unorganised industries had become organised' with the result that better wages, less hours, and improved working conditions had been obtained. This revival of trade organisation was gratifying to the friends of labour,and without a doubt marked a distinct step in the advance of the workers. It was surprising whet organisation could do in regard to improvement lathe every-day life of the people. The benefits of edu- cation, the cheap Press, and free libraries were doing much for the workers in creating a desire for greater knowledge, in removing some of the narrow-mindedness and jealousy which was the result of ignorance and in- experience. Everywhere,workingmen were admitted to be a most valuable addition on such bodies as Town Councilsetc. The question was, how were they to solve the ever-growing problem of the unemployed. They could not emigrate them. The only thing to be done was to secure English land for English people so that they might steadily apply their labour to their native soil and thus add to the real wealth of their couatry. The cry of all economists was " back to the land." All ad- mitted that to labour, and to labour only, man was in- debted for the possession of everthing containing exchangeable value. Yet the position of labour on land was one of great inequality. IC Itt. in subjection by the squire and the parson, his life was very little better than that of the slave. The question of old age pensions had been before the Council irom time to time for discussion. He regarded with pleasure, the rapidly increasing in- terest of leading statesmen in this question. He was convinced that Some method of dealing with that im- portant subject, radically different from that now in force must soon be adopted. The present poor laws, as at present administered had served their day, and the time had now come for the question of pauperism to be fully considered in all its aspects, and some satisfactory change made in the system of providing for the comfort of the respectable aged poor, the widow, and her young family, and for the orphan and the outcast. How many decent hard-working people were brought face to face with want in consequence of the change in ths methods of production and the alterations which were constantly taking place in the busy hives of the factories and work- shops. In the present poor-house, the decrepid old man must live out his days away from the society of the true loving wife of his early manhood. 'The aged wife was separated from her husband. There :he poor widow was sundered from her beloved children. What a millstone round the necks of the children, was the fact that they were brought up in the Workhouse. How cruel and how hard it seemed that that taunt should be flung at them at any moment, when they considered that under con- ditions a little aid, and a trifle more outdoor relief at the time it was needed, would have prevented the possibility of such a reproach. In conclusion, he begged of all to try and preserve their independence. Let them respect each other, and be loyal to their fellow workmen, and the year would be a bright and happy one for all. RESIGNATION OF • TRADNI3 SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>A letter was received from the secretary of Operative Painters and }lumbers resigning membership of the Council.</p>
        <p>TIS 71188 LABOUR ASSOCIATION. In response to a letter from the Parliamentary Com- mittee of the Trades Congress, the Council moved that the president of Board of Trade be requested to inquire fully into the bonafides of the Free Labour Association, who had arranged to meet that gentleman—Mr. Batter- sby said that be did not think that the members of the Association sprang from the working class.—The Chair- man : It is really intended to crush trade unionists.</p>
        <p>TEE ACTION OF PARLIAMENTARY COMMITTEE OF TEI TRADES' COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A letter was received from the London Trades'Council requesting the Warringten Council to agree to the forma- tion of a conference for the purpose of condemning the action of the Parliamentary Committee of the Trades' Congress—Mr. Lang: I don't think it is necessary to call a conference to condemn their action. That could be done by resolut ion.—The secretary said that the War- rington Council was one of the 18 Councils who protested against the fstanding orders.—Mr.;Barnes thought that the standing orders had worked very efEciently,and they pourtrayed more correctly, the fesiing of the trades' unionists of the ceonts , without being transferred with adventurers who occasionally got into the ranks. He thought that the best course should be upon the table, and he moved that that course be taken.—Mr. Taylor seconded.—Mr. Lang said that the standing orders, as first set out,had a very different interpretation than they now had. In the original all representatives from Trades' Councils were prohibited. In the explanation of the Parliamentary Committee, the complexion was changed, but as to the wisdom, or un wisdom, that was another matter.—The motion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The SICRETART, in his annual report, said that he re- gretted that he was unable to congratulate the members of the Council upon its increased strength either numeri- cally or financially. There were no secessions ef societies, but there was a decrease in membership of branches connected with the Council, with the Conse- quent result of a diminution of contributions. In the last report, he had to refer to the industrial deprossion which had afflicted the country for a long time, but during the past six months, the state of trade had steadily improved and still continued, and he trusted that the ensuing year viould prove to be better than the one just concluded. It might not generally be known that the meetings of the Council were open to all the members who paid their contributions through the re- spective societies and oy permiasion of the chairman were allowed to speak but not to vote. With regard to the labour representation on public bodies, the secretary expressed gratification that Mr. Malkin had been re- turned unopposed for Bewsey ward and Mr. Lang for St. John's ward after a contested election, to the Board of Guardians. Disputes had not been common in the trades affiliated with the Council, only one trade being in dispute with their employers, viz : the wire drawers employed at Messrs. Greening. The firm wished to make a very large reduction of wages, and consequently a strike took place, the Council upholding the course of action taken by the men. As a result of the negotia- tions the steam loom weavers returned to work on the old price list and were instrumental in raising the prices at other shops in the town. After referring to various bills and other matters dealing with labour, Mr. BULLOCK said that it had been necessary during the past year for the Council to change their place of meeting from the Cocoa rooms in Horeemarket-street, and meetings would in future be held in the Wagon and Horses Inn,Butter- market-street. Negotiations had been opened with the, Town Clerk, to have the meeting in a room in the Town Hall, but no room was available, as they were all occu- pied by the officials of the Corporation. A circular dur- ing the past yearthad been issued by the Manchester and Salford Trades Council, stating that in November, Mr. Postlethwaite. C.C., would bring the fair contracts clause before the Lancashire County Council and urged the Trades Council in Lancashire to approach their county councillors pith a view to their adoption of that necessary provision. The Warrington Council had agreed to do se. A resolution bad been forwarded tss Mr. Sate Woods, ex-M.P. for Ince, congratulating him upon gaining the verdict in the trial of Woods v. Keen for slander. Finally, the Council urged those societies not connected with the Association, to become affiliated and examine for themselves the necessity of being more united.—The report was adopted unarimously on the motion of Mr. Barnes, seconded by Mr. Ellison.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. WRIGHT. J.P., moved that Mr. Batteraby be the president of the Council for the coming year. Mr. Barnes seconded and it was agreed to.—Mr. A.Lang was elected 'vice-president on the motion of Mr. Oakdeia seconded by Mr. Edwardson.—Mr. Wright moved and Mr. Sambrook roconded the re-election of Mr. Bullock as secretary which was carried unanimously.—Mr.Oakden wa3 re-elected treasurer for the ensuing twelve months, en the motion of Mr. Lang,seconded by Mr. Elliso2. The following gentlemen were elected the Executive Com- mittee of the Council :—Messrs. Malkin, Morris, Barnes, Ellison, Sambrook, Gregson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-11-09_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>PENICETH.</head>
        <p>PENICETH.</p>
        <p>SIICCESS4o PENIETH BIIZEDEIRS.—At the second annual exhibition, of the Widnes, Runcorn and district gird Breeders' Association, held at Widnes on Saturday, Penketh breeders had no cause to complain of their suc- cess. Messrs. J. Hampson and E. Goodwin secured no fewer than nine prizes and four specials, A. Davies two firsts and one second, and A. J. Hobson six prizes, these being for canaries and British birds. In the classes for pigeons open to all England, the entries were not so numerous as was expected,but what exhibits there were were of exceptional merit. Mr. B. Cross, of Penketh, easily took first prize with his pigeon in class 7. In poins of merit this bird was by far the best in the show, and Mr. Cross is to be congratulated on his great success. There were no specials in these classes or this bird would, in the opinion of the judge, have taken them. Mr. F. C. Dixon took two firsts and a second and J. E. Edgerley one third and one second in the pigeon classes.</p>
        <p>THE WORKINGMEN'S GOSIOIL TEMPERANCS 111ISSION HALL. An excellent service of song, entitled "More than Conqueror," was well rendered by the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church choir under the able leadership of Mr. Walter Brough, there being an or- chestral band and a chorus ot about 30 artistes. The principals were Mrs. A. B. Bailey, Miss Hannah Brough, Mr. Parkinson, Mr. N. Cook, with Mr. T. Brou .h as accompanist. Toese all rendered their respective parts well. Miss B. Dutton, of Moore, who was announced to preside and give the con- nective readings,was unfortunately unable to attend owing to illness. The readings were, however given by Mrs. Storey in an excellent manner. A vote of thanks was moved by Mr. William Nightin- gale and seconded by Mr. Fleury Heald to all the artistes and the reader.—On Sunday night, a gospel temperance meeting was held at the end of the Farmer's Stores. Mr. R. Storey led the meeting. Mr. William Nightingale charmed his hearers by his solos, " The Cup and the Streamlet,' and " Oo child,. and beg a bit of bread." Afterwards at the Hall, Mr. J. Drury pres.ded. Mr. Nightingale sang" All will be well," and gave another bright, stirring address: Mr. Adam Iforrocks also addressed the meeting, and Mr. Henry Heald, sang, "A thing that is done and Mr. Whiteman, also spoke. Some pledgas wara' signed. The usual Band of flope meeting wag held on Monday and well attende I. L 4.114 PS. —J. S. GARLAND, Tl'ollllloll ler , Warrington* STOVES.—Iron Merchant, GARLAND, Bridge-street. Warrington. SEWING MACHINES, Rani £2 181. 6d. Treadle £44' With handsom.., walnut cover —3 and W. Durrol. 16 and 18, Sankev-et., Warrington —Advt.</p>
